            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the family of lines \\(x+y=\\text{constant}\\)).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  A covering with no sunny line  \n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_n^{(0)}:=\\{\\,x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); i.e. every \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_n^{(0)}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(a\\le n-1\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which belongs to (1)) contains \\((a,b)\\).  \nIf \\(a=n\\) then \\(b\\le 1\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a+b=n+1\\) and lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered. ∎  \n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal F_n^{(0)}\\) have slope \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore they are non‑sunny.  \nSince \\(|\\mathcal F_n^{(0)}|=(n-1)+1=n\\), the value \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Turning a vertical line into a sunny line  \n\nFix a vertical line \\(x=t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\).  \nThe points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lying on this line are  \n\n\\[\n(t,1),\\;(t,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(t,\\,n+1-t\\bigr). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nChoose an integer \\(r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le n+1-t\\) and consider the line  \n\n\\[\ns_{t,r}:\\; y = \\frac{r-1}{t-1}\\,(x-1)+1 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(s_{t,r}\\) passes through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((t,r)\\); its slope is \\(\\dfrac{r-1}{t-1}\\).  \nBecause \\(t\\ge2\\) and \\(1\\le r\\le n+1-t\\), we have \\(0<\\dfrac{r-1}{t-1}<\\infty\\) and \\(\\dfrac{r-1}{t-1}\\neq -1\\); hence \\(s_{t,r}\\) is sunny.\n\n**Claim.** Replacing the vertical line \\(x=t\\) by the sunny line \\(s_{t,r}\\) leaves the covering property intact.\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nFor any \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le r\\) the point \\((t,b)\\) belongs to the line \\(s_{t,r}\\) because the points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((t,r)\\) determine the whole line and the segment between them contains all intermediate integer points of the form \\((t,b)\\) with \\(b\\le r\\).  \nFor any \\(b\\) with \\(r<b\\le n+1-t\\) we have \\(t+b\\le n+1\\); consequently \\((t,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\), which is still present in the family.  \nAll other points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are unaffected by the replacement. ∎  \n\nThus a single vertical line can be swapped for a sunny line without losing any required point.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Obtaining any number \\(k\\) of sunny lines  \n\nStarting from the base family \\(\\mathcal F_n^{(0)}\\) we perform the replacement described in Section 3 on **exactly \\(k\\)** distinct vertical lines (any choice of admissible \\(r\\) for each replacement).  \nAfter the \\(k\\) replacements we obtain the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_n^{(k)}=\n\\underbrace{\\{s_{t_1,r_1},\\dots ,s_{t_k,r_k}\\}}_{\\text{k sunny lines}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{x=i\\mid i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\setminus\\{t_1,\\dots ,t_k\\}\\}}_{\\text{n-1-k vertical lines}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\{x+y=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\nk+(n-1-k)+1=n .\n\\]\n\nBy the claim of Section 3 each replacement preserves the covering of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); therefore \\(\\mathcal F_n^{(k)}\\) still covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nBy construction exactly \\(k\\) of its lines are sunny.  \n\nSince the choice of the \\(k\\) vertical lines and the corresponding parameters \\(r_i\\) is completely free (subject only to the obvious bounds), this construction works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Upper bound on \\(k\\)\n\nA family contains only \\(n\\) lines, so it cannot contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines. Hence \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Final conclusion  \n\nFor each integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nBoth existence (Sections 2–4) and the trivial upper bound (Section 5) have been proved rigorously, completing the solution. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
