fine the line\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{t}:\\; y=\\frac{\\,t-1\\,}{\\,n-t\\,}(x-1)+1 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   The line \\(\\ell_{t}\\) passes through the two points \\((1,1)\\) and \\(\\bigl(n+1-t,\\,t\\bigr)\\); its slope is\n   \\[\n   m_t=\\frac{t-1}{\\,n-t\\,}.\n   \\]\n   Because \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) we have \\(t-1>0\\) and \\(n-t>0\\), so \\(m_t\\neq0,\\infty\\).  \n   Moreover \\(m_t\\neq-1\\) because \\(t-1\\neq -(n-t)\\) (the left‑hand side is positive while the right‑hand side is negative).  \n   Hence \\(\\ell_{t}\\) is **sunny**.\n\n   **Lemma 2 (Covering after a single replacement).**  \n   Replace the horizontal line \\(y=t\\) in \\(\\mathcal F_n^{(0)}\\) by the sunny line \\(\\ell_{t}\\).  \n   The resulting family still covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   - The point \\(\\bigl(n+1-t,\\,t\\bigr)\\) belongs to \\(\\ell_{t}\\) by construction.  \n   - The point \\((1,1)\\) also belongs to \\(\\ell_{t}\\); it remains covered because the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) is still present.  \n   - Let \\((x,t)\\) be any other point of row \\(t\\) (so \\(1\\le x\\le n-t\\)).  \n     Since the maximal abscissa in this row is \\(n+1-t\\), we have \\(x\\le n-t\\).  \n     Consequently \\(x\\le n-2\\) (because \\(t\\ge2\\)), and the vertical line \\(x\\) belongs to the original family (it is one of the lines \\(x=i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\)).  \n     The point \\((x,t)\\) lies on that vertical line because its first coordinate equals \\(x\\).  \n     Thus \\((x,t)\\) remains covered after the removal of \\(y=t\\). ∎\n\n   The key observation is that every point of row \\(t\\) other than the right‑most one can be “handed over’’ to the vertical line whose index equals its \\(x\\)‑coordinate; such a vertical line is still present because we never delete a vertical line with index \\(\\le n-t\\).\n\n4. **Obtaining any number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.**  \n   Starting from the base configuration \\(\\mathcal F_n^{(0)}\\), apply the replacement of L