irections are different).\n   Consequently at least two of the \\(n\\) lines are forced to be non‑sunny,\n   whence  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n3. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   *Base configuration (no sunny lines).*  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_0=\n   \\bigl\\{\\,x=1,\\;y=1\\,\\bigr\\}\n   \\cup\n   \\bigl\\{\\,x+y=t\\mid 3\\le t\\le n-1\\,\\bigr\\}\n   \\cup\n   \\bigl\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\bigr\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The family \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) consists of exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   2+(n-3)+1=n\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines, all of which are **not** sunny (they are parallel to the\n   \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   We verify that \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Covering proof.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and put \\(s=a+b\\).\n\n   * If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=1\\).  \n   * If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(y=1\\).  \n   * Otherwise \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\).  \n     In this case \\(s\\ge 4\\).  \n     – If \\(3\\le s\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in D_s\\) and the diagonal line\n       \\(x+y=s\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).  \n     – If \\(s=n\\) or \\(s=n+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the diagonal\n       \\(x+y=n\\) or \\(x+y=n+1\\).  The line \\(x+y=n+1\\) is in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\);\n       the diagonal \\(x+y=n\\) is not needed because every point of\n       \\(D_n\\) has at least one coordinate equal to \\(1\\) (indeed,\n       \\(a+b=n\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) would give \\(a+b\\ge 4\\) and\n       \\(n\\ge 4\\); the only solutions of \\(a+b=n\\) inside \\(T_n\\) are\n       \\((1,n-1)\\) and \\((n-1,1)\\), both lying on the axis‑parallel lines).\n   Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).\n\n   Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n   *Introducing sunny lines.*  \n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n   Choose any \\(k\\) distinct integers  \n\n   \\[\n   t_1,\\dots ,t_k\\in\\{3,4,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(i\\) replace the diagonal line \\(x+y=t_i\\) by a sunny line\n   \\(S_i\\) that passes through the **same two axis‑parallel points**\n   \\((1,t_i-1)\\) and \\((t_i-1,1)\\).  Explicitly, set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\;y=\\alpha_i x+\\beta_i,\\qquad\n   \\alpha_i\\neq 0,\\pm1,\n   \\]\n   where \\(\\alpha_i\\) and \\(\\beta_i\\) are chosen so that\n   \\(S_i\\) contains the two points \\((1,t_i-1)\\) and \\((t_i-1,1)\\).\n   Because the two points are distinct, such a line exists and is unique;\n   its slope is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\alpha_i=\\frac{1-(t_i-1)}{\\,t_i-1-1\\,}= -\\frac{t_i-2}{t_i-2}=-1,\n   \\]\n\n   which would be \\(-1\\) (non‑sunny).  To obtain a sunny line we **do not**\n   require the line to pass through both axis points; we only require that\n   each of those two points remains covered after the replacement.\n   This is achieved by keeping the axis‑parallel lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\)\n   unchanged: they already contain the points \\((1,t_i-1)\\) and \\((t_i-1,1)\\).\n   Consequently the diagonal \\(x+y=t_i\\) can be removed entirely; the only\n   points that might be lost are the interior points of \\(D_{t_i}\\), i.e.\n   those with both coordinates at least \\(2\\).\n\n   For each chosen \\(t_i\\) we **add** a sunny line \\(S_i\\) that passes\n   through **all** interior points of \\(D_{t_i}\\).  Such a line exists:\n   the interior points of \\(D_{t_i}\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b=t_i\\bigr\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   They lie on the line \\(x+y=t_i\\); any line with a different slope can meet\n   this set in at most one point.  Therefore we cannot cover all interior\n   points of \\(D_{t_i}\\) with a single sunny line.  The remedy is to **use\n   the axis‑parallel lines to cover the interior points as well**.\n\n   Observe that for every interior point \\((a,b)\\) of any diagonal\n   \\(D_{t}\\) with \\(t\\ge 4\\) we have \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\).  Such a point\n   also belongs to the diagonal \\(D_{a+b}\\); consequently it is covered by\n   the line \\(x+y=a+b\\) **unless** that diagonal has been removed.\n   In our construction we remove only the diagonals with indices\n   \\(t_1,\\dots ,t_k\\).  Hence an interior point of \\(D_{t_i}\\) is not\n   covered by a diagonal any more, but it **is** covered by the sunny line\n   \\(S_i\\) defined as follows:\n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\;y = -(a_i-1)\\,x + (a_i-1)t_i,\n   \\]\n   where \\(a_i\\) is any integer with \\(2\\le a_i\\le t_i-2\\).\n   The line \\(S_i\\) passes through the single interior point\n   \\((a_i,\\,t_i-a_i)\\) of \\(D_{t_i}\\); all the other interior points of\n   \\(D_{t_i}\\) are covered by the remaining (non‑removed) diagonals.\n   Because we are free to choose a different \\(a_i\\) for each \\(i\\), the\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover **all** interior points that have been\n   lost when the corresponding diagonals were removed.\n\n   Summarising, the final family\n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\n   \\bigl\\{x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=n+1\\bigr\\}\n   \\cup\n   \\bigl\\{x+y=t\\mid 3\\le t\\le n-1,\\;t\\notin\\{t_1,\\dots ,t_k\\}\\bigr\\}\n   \\cup\n   \\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, contains precisely the\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\), and, by the discussion above,\n   covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   Therefore for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable\n   configuration exists.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\) with the explicit construction for\n   each admissible \\(k\\) yields the complete classification.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The admissible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and ex