\nDefine  \n\n\\[\ns_{c,m}:\\; y-b=m(x-a).\n\\]\n\nThen \\(s_{c,m}\\) is sunny (its slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\)), it passes through \\((a,b)\\), and it is distinct from every line of \\(\\mathcal D_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe slope of \\(s_{c,m}\\) is \\(m\\); the three forbidden slopes correspond precisely to being parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\). By the choice of \\(m\\) none of these occurs, so \\(s_{c,m}\\) is sunny. It contains \\((a,b)\\) by construction. Since every line of \\(\\mathcal D_n\\) has slope \\(-1\\), \\(s_{c,m}\\) cannot coincide with any \\(\\ell_{c'}\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. Start from the diagonal family \\(\\mathcal D_n\\) (Lemma 1).  \n2. Choose a subset \\(C\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) with \\(|C|=k\\).  \n3. For each \\(c\\in C\\) pick a point \\((a_c,b_c)\\in T_n\\cap\\ell_c\\) (such a point exists because \\(\\ell_c\\) meets \\(T_n\\)).  \n4. Choose a rational slope \\(m_c\\) satisfying the conditions of Lemma 2, and make the choices so that the slopes \\(\\{m_c\\mid c\\in C\\}\\) are pairwise distinct (possible because the set of admissible slopes is infinite).  \n5. Replace each \\(\\ell_c\\) with the sunny line \\(s_{c,m_c}\\) defined in Lemma 2.\n\nDenote the resulting family of lines by  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n,k}= \\bigl(\\mathcal D_n\\setminus\\{\\ell_c\\mid c\\in C\\}\\bigr)\\;\\cup\\;\\{\\,s_{c,m_c}\\mid c\\in C\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Properties of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).*\n\n* **Cardinality and distinctness.**  \n  The set contains exactly \\(n\\) lines: the \\(n-k\\) untouched diagonal lines are mutually distinct, and the \\(k\\) sunny lines are distinct from each other (different slopes) and from all diagonal lines (different slopes).\n\n* **Number of sunny lines.**  \n  By construction precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(s_{c,m_c}\\) are sunny.\n\n* **Covering condition.**  \n  Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and set \\(c=a+b\\).  \n  – If \\(c\\notin C\\) then \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_c\\subset\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).  \n  – If \\(c\\in C\\) then we chose \\((a_c,b_c)=(a,b)\\) when constructing \\(s_{c,m_c}\\); hence \\((a,b)\\in s_{c,m_c}\\subset\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).  \n\n  Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).\n\nTherefore \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) satisfies both required conditions, and exactly \\(k\\) of its lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), and  \n* contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) not exceeding \\(n\\) are admissible. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
