.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) denote by  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\;x+y=c\n\\]\n\nthe diagonal line of slope \\(-1\\).  The set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_n=\\{\\ell_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, and  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c\\cap T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=c\\},\n\\qquad |\\ell_c\\cap T_n|=c-1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Intersection bound)\n\nIf a line \\(L\\) is not parallel to the diagonals (i.e. its slope \\(\\neq-1\\)), then for each integer \\(c\\) the intersection \\(L\\cap\\ell_c\\) consists of at most one point.  \n\n*Proof.*  Two non‑parallel lines intersect in at most one point. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Maximum number of lattice points on a line)\n\n*Diagonal line.*  By (2) a diagonal \\(\\ell_c\\) contains exactly \\(c-1\\le n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Sunny line.*  Let \\(L\\) be a line whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  By Lemma 1 it meets each diagonal \\(\\ell_c\\) in at most one point, hence it contains at most one point of \\(T_n\\) from each diagonal.  Since there are \\(n\\) diagonals,  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_n|\\le n. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Construction for any \\(k\\))\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  There exists a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny, and  \n* every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. **Choice of sunny lines.**  \n   Choose \\(k\\) distinct real numbers \\(\\alpha_1,\\dots ,\\alpha_k\\) with \\(\\alpha_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \n   For each \\(i\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[