gonal lines.**  \n   Include exactly those \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines in the family:\n\n   \\[\n   D_j=\\ell_{k+1+j}\\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(D_j\\) has slope \\(-1\\) (hence is not sunny) and contains all points of its diagonal, thereby covering the remaining points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n5. **Counting lines.**  \n   The total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal L|=k\\;(\\text{sunny})+(n-k)\\;(\\text{diagonal})=n,\n   \\]\n\n   and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny by construction.\n\nThus a covering family exists for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (No larger \\(k\\))\n\nThe problem requires exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  Since a line is either sunny or not sunny, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines.  Hence \\(k\\le n\\).  The case \\(k<0\\) is impossible because \\(k\\) is a non‑negative integer. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Result\n\nCombining Lemma 3 (existence for all \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)) with Lemma 4 (impossibility of any other \\(k\\)) we obtain:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The admissible values of }k\\text{ are precisely }0,1,2,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
