ed.**  \n   Choose the first \\(k\\) diagonals \\(\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3},\\dots ,\\ell_{k+1}\\).  \n\n2. **Choice of a single lattice point on each omitted diagonal.**  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) set  \n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,\\;k+2-i).\n   \\]  \n   Since \\(i\\ge1\\) and \\(k+2-i\\ge1\\) and \\(i+(k+2-i)=k+2\\le n+1\\), each \\(P_i\\) belongs to \\(T_n\\) and lies on the diagonal \\(\\ell_{i+1}\\). The points \\(P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\) are distinct and lie on distinct diagonals.\n\n3. **Construction of sunny lines through the chosen points.**  \n   For a fixed \\(i\\) consider all slopes that would make the line through \\(P_i\\) pass through another point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   For any distinct \\((a,b),(a',b')\\in T_n\\) the slope of the line joining them is \\(\\frac{b-b'}{a-a'}\\); there are only finitely many such slopes.  \n   Choose a real number \\(\\alpha_i\\) satisfying  \n\n   \\[\n   \\alpha_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\cup\\Bigl\\{\\frac{b-b'}{a-a'}\\;\\Big|\\;(a,b),(a',b')\\in T_n,\\;(a,b)\\neq (a',b')\\Bigr\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   This is possible because the excluded set is finite.  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y-(k+2-i)=\\alpha_i\\bigl(x-i\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   By construction \\(S_i\\) passes through \\(P_i\\) and through no other point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   The slopes \\(\\alpha_i\\) are distinct, so the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are distinct and, by the choice of slopes, each is sunny.\n\n4. **Adding the remaining diagonals.**  \n   Include the diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   D_j=\\ell_{k+1+j}\\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,n-k)\n   \\]\n\n   in the family. Each \\(D_j\\) has slope \\(-1\\) (hence is not sunny) and contains all points of its diagonal.\n\n5. **Verification of coverage.**  \n\n   *Points on the first \\(k\\) diagonals.*  \n   For \\(c\\le k+1\\) the only point of \\(\\ell_c\\) that belongs to the family is the chosen point \\(P_{c-1}\\), which lies on the sunny line \\(S_{c-1}\\). Thus every point of these diagonals is covered.\n\n   *Points on the remaining d