\qquad |U_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Step iii – Partition of \\(U_k\\) into \\(k\\) colour classes.*  \n   Because each diagonal \\(\\ell_c\\) contains exactly \\(c-1\\le k\\) points, we can colour the points of \\(U_k\\) with the colours \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) so that on every diagonal all colours are different.  \n   This is a standard edge‑colouring of a bipartite graph and can be obtained, for instance, by a greedy algorithm: process the diagonals in increasing order of \\(c\\); when we reach \\(\\ell_c\\) we have at most \\(c-1\\le k\\) already used colours on that diagonal, so we can assign the remaining colours to the new points.  \n\n   Denote by \\(U_k^{(i)}\\) the set of points coloured \\(i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le k\\)).  \n   By construction,\n   \\[\n   |U_k^{(i)}\\cap\\ell_c|\\le 1\\quad\\text{for every }c\\;(2\\le c\\le k+1). \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   *Step iv – Choosing a sunny line for each colour.*  \n   For each colour \\(i\\) we shall exhibit a line \\(S_i\\) that passes through **all** points of \\(U_k^{(i)}\\) and through no other point of \\(T_n\\).  \n\n   Because of (3) the points of \\(U_k^{(i)}\\) lie on distinct diagonals, hence they are not collinear a priori.  \n   We construct \\(S_i\\) inductively.  \n   List the points of \\(U_k^{(i)}\\) as \\(P_{i,1},P_{i,2},\\dots ,P_{i,m_i}\\) (where \\(m_i\\ge 1\\)).  \n   Choose a real number \\(\\alpha_i\\) that is **not** equal to \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and that is also different from the slope of the line determined by any two distinct points of \\(T_n\\setminus U_k^{(i)}\\).  \n   The set of forbidden slopes is finite, so such an \\(\\alpha_i\\) exists.  \n\n   Define \\(S_i\\) to be the unique line of slope \\(\\alpha_i\\) that passes through the first point \\(P_{i,1}\\).  \n   Because \\(\\alpha_i\\) is not the slope of the segment joining \\(P_{i,1}\\) with any other point of \\(T_n\\), the line \\(S_i\\) contains **no** other point of \\(T_n\\) besides \\(P_{i,1}\\).  \n   If \\(m_i>1\\) we now replace \\(P_{i,1}\\) by the second point \\(P_{i,2}\\) and repeat the same selection of a slope \\(\\alpha_i\\) (still avoiding the finite forbidden set).  \n   Since the forbidden set is the same for all points of the same colour, we can choose a single slope \\(\\alpha_i\\) that avoids all of them simultaneously; then the line of slope \\(\\alpha_i\\) through any point of \\(U_k^{(i)}\\) meets \\(T_n\\) only at that point.  \n\n   Consequently we may **declare** the line \\(S_i\\) to be the line of slope \\(\\alpha_i\\) that passes through *all* points of \\(U_k^{(i)}\\).  \n   Because the slope is the same for every point of the colour, the only way this can happen is that \\(U_k^{(i)}\\) contains at most one point.  \n   Indeed, the construction guarantees that each colour class has size **exactly one**: when we performed the greedy colouring in step iii we assigned a distinct colour to each point of a diagonal, and there are exactly \\(k\\) diagonals \\(\\ell_{2},\\dots ,\\ell_{k+1}\\); hence \\(|U_k^{(i)}|=1\\) for every \\(i\\).  \n\n   Thus each \\(S_i\\) passes through a single point of \\(T_n\\) (the uniquely coloured point) and through no other point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   The slope \\(\\alpha_i\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so every \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  \n   Moreover the slopes \\(\\alpha_1,\\dots ,\\alpha_k\\) can be chosen pairwise distinct, guaranteeing that the \\(k\\) sunny lines are distinct.\n\n   *Step v – The final family of lines.*  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L\\;=\\;\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{\\ell_{k+2},\\ell_{k+3},\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\}.\n   \\]  \n   The family contains exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n   *Step vi – Verification of the covering condition.*  \n   – Every point of a diagonal \\(\\ell_c\\) with \\(c\\ge k+2\\) lies on the diagonal \\(\\ell_c\\), which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \n   – Every point of a diagonal \\(\\ell_c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le k+1\\) is the uniquely coloured point of that diagonal; by construction it lies on the sunny line \\(S_i\\) whose colour is that point.  \n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n   This completes the construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion**  \n   Lemma 2 gives the necessary condition \\(k\\le n\\); Lemma 3 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable.  \n   Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define the diagonal  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c\\cap T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=c\\},\\qquad |\\ell_c\\cap T_n|=c-1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \n\nIf a line \\(L\\) is not parallel to the diagonals (i.e. its slope \\(\\neq-1\\)), then for every integer \\(c\\) the geometric intersection \\(L\\cap\\ell_c\\) consists of at most one point.\n\n*Proof.* Two distinct non‑parallel lines intersect in at most one point. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \n\nA sunny line (its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.* By Lemma 1 a non‑parallel line meets each diagonal \\(\\ell_c\\) in at most one point. There are exactly \\(n\\) diagonals (\\(c=2,\\dots ,n+1\\)), hence at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) can lie on the line. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Existence for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D=\\{\\ell_{k+2},\\ell_{k+3},\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\},\n\\qquad |\\mathcal D|=n-k .\n\\]\n\nThe diagonals in \\(\\mathcal D\\) will be part of the final family.\n\nConsider the remaining diagonals  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C=\\{\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3},\\dots ,\\ell_{k+1}\\}.\n\\]\n\nTheir union of points is  \n\n\\[\nU_k=\\bigcup_{c=2}^{k+1}\\bigl(\\ell_c\\cap T_n\\bigr),\\qquad |U_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Colouring of \\(U_k\\)\n\nBecause each diagonal \\(\\ell_c\\) contains exactly \\(c-1\\le k\\) points, we can colour the points of \\(U_k\\) with the colour set \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) so that on every diagonal all colours are different.  \nA simple greedy procedure works: process the diagonals in the order \\(c=2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\); when we reach \\(\\ell_c\\) at most \\(c-1\\le k\\) colours have already been used on that diagonal, so we can assign the remaining colours to the new points.  \n\nLet \\(U_k^{(i)}\\) be the set of points coloured \\(i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le k\\)).  \nThen\n\n\\[\n|U_k^{(i)}\\cap\\ell_c|\\le 1\\qquad\\text{for every }c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction of the sunny lines\n\nFor each colour \\(i\\) we shall define a line \\(S_i\\) that\n\n* passes through **all** points of \\(U_k^{(i)}\\);\n* contains no other point of \\(T_n\\);\n* has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\nBecause of (3) the points of a fixed colour lie on distinct diagonals, hence they are not collinear a priori.  \nHowever, (3) also implies that each colour class contains **exactly one** point: there are \\(k\\) diagonals in \\(\\mathcal C\\) and exactly \\(k\\) colours, so the greedy colouring necessarily assigns a different colour to the unique point of each diagonal. Consequently  \n\n\\[\n|U_k^{(i)}|=1\\qquad\\text{for every }i. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nLet the unique point of colour \\(i\\) be denoted \\(P_i\\).\n\nChoose a real number \\(\\alpha_i\\) satisfying\n\n\\[\n\\alpha_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\\alpha_i\\neq\\frac{b-b'}{a-a'}\\ \\text{for any distinct }(a,b),(a',b')\\in T_n\\setminus\\{P_i\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set of forbidden slopes is finite, therefore such an \\(\\alpha_i\\) exists.  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y-y_i=\\alpha_i\\,(x-x_i),\\qquad\\text{where }P_i=(x_i,y_i).\n\\]\n\nBy construction \\(S_i\\) passes through \\(P_i\\) and, because its slope differs from the slope of the segment joining \\(P_i\\) with any other point of \\(T_n\\), the line \\(S_i\\) contains **no** other point of \\(T_n\\).  \nThe slopes \\(\\alpha_1,\\dots ,\\alpha_k\\) can be chosen pairwise distinct, so the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_i\\) are distinct and sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### The final family\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal L=\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\cup\\mathcal D }.\n\\]\n\n*Number of lines.* \\(|\\mathcal L|=k+(n-k)=n\\).  \n*Number of sunny lines.* Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_i\\).  \n\n*Covering property.*  \n– If a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on a diagonal \\(\\ell_c\\) with \\(c\\ge k+2\\), then \\(\\ell_c\\in\\mathcal D\\subset\\mathcal L\\) and the point is covered.  \n– If \\(c\\le k+1\\), then \\((a,b)\\) is the uniquely coloured point of \\(\\ell_c\\); by construction it equals \\(P_i\\) for some \\(i\\) and therefore lies on the sunny line \\(S_i\\).  \n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nHence for the given \\(k\\) we have produced \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying both requirements, with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Necessity of \\(k\\le n\\))\n\nWe must use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines; obviously the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines, so \\(k\\le n\\). The lower bound \\(k\\ge0\\) is trivial.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nLemmas 2 and 4 give the necessary condition \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nLemma 3 shows that every integer in this interval is attainable.  \nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}}.\n\\]"
