   Thus we have exactly \\(k\\) distinct points, each lying on a different diagonal.\n\n   *Step\u202fiii \u2013 A sunny line through a prescribed lattice point that avoids all other points of \\(T_n\\).*  \n\n   **Lemma\u202f3.1.** *Given a point \\(P\\in T_n\\) there exists a sunny line that passes through \\(P\\) and through no other point of \\(T_n\\).*  \n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Let \\(P=(x_0,y_0)\\).  \n   For any other point \\(Q=(x,y)\\in T_n\\setminus\\{P\\}\\) the slope of the line \\(PQ\\) is \\(\\displaystyle m_{PQ}=\\frac{y-y_0}{\\,x-x_0\\,}\\).  \n   The set  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S=\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\cup\\{m_{PQ}\\mid Q\\in T_n\\setminus\\{P\\}\\}\n   \\]\n   is finite (there are only \\(|T_n|-1\\) points \\(Q\\)).  \n   Choose a real number \\(\\alpha\\) not belonging to \\(\\mathcal S\\); such an \\(\\alpha\\) exists because \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\mathcal S\\) is infinite.  \n   The line of slope \\(\\alpha\\) through \\(P\\) is then not parallel to the axes nor to \\(\\ell_c\\) (so it is sunny) and, by construction, its slope differs from that of any segment joining \\(P\\) to another point of \\(T_n\\); consequently it contains no other point of \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n   Apply Lemma\u202f3.1 to each of the points \\(P_c\\) (\\(c=2,\\dots ,k+1\\)).  \n   Choose the slopes \\(\\alpha_c\\) pairwise distinct; this is possible because we have infinitely many admissible slopes.  \n   Denote by \\(S_c\\) the line of slope \\(\\alpha_c\\) passing through \\(P_c\\).  \n   Each \\(S_c\\) is sunny, contains exactly the point \\(P_c\\) of \\(T_n\\), and the lines \\(S_2,\\dots ,S_{k+1}\\) are distinct.\n\n   *Step\u202fiv \u2013 The final family of lines.*  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L\\;=\\;\\{S_2,S_3,\\dots ,S_{k+1}\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{\\ell_{k+2},\\ell_{k+3},\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\}.\n   \\]  \n   The family contains \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which (the \\(S_c\\)) are sunny.\n\n   *Step\u202fv \u2013 Verification of the covering condition.*  \