            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define the diagonal  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c\\cap T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=c\\},\\qquad |\\ell_c\\cap T_n|=c-1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \n\nIf a line \\(L\\) is not parallel to the diagonals (i.e. its slope \\(\\neq-1\\)), then for every integer \\(c\\) the geometric intersection \\(L\\cap\\ell_c\\) consists of at most one point.\n\n*Proof.* Two distinct non‑parallel lines intersect in at most one point. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \n\nA sunny line (its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.* By Lemma 1 a non‑parallel line meets each diagonal \\(\\ell_c\\) in at most one point, and there are exactly \\(n\\) diagonals (\\(c=2,\\dots ,n+1\\)). Hence at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) can lie on the line. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3.1 (Existence of a sunny line through a prescribed lattice point)\n\nLet \\(P\\in T_n\\). There exists a sunny line that passes through \\(P\\) and through no other point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each \\(Q\\in T_n\\setminus\\{P\\}\\) let \\(m_{PQ}=\\dfrac{y_Q-y_P}{x_Q-x_P}\\) be the slope of the line \\(PQ\\) (if \\(x_Q=x_P\\) we regard the slope as \\(\\infty\\)).  \nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S=\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\cup\\{m_{PQ}\\mid Q\\in T_n\\setminus\\{P\\}\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal S\\) is finite because \\(T_n\\) is finite. Choose a real number \\(\\alpha\\notin\\mathcal S\\); such an \\(\\alpha\\) exists because \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\mathcal S\\) is infinite.  \nThe line of slope \\(\\alpha\\) through \\(P\\) is not parallel to the axes nor to any diagonal, so it is sunny. Moreover its slope differs from every \\(m_{PQ}\\); consequently the line contains no point \\(Q\\neq P\\) of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Construction for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\). There exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that together cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. **Diagonals kept.**  \n   Keep the diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal D=\\{\\ell_{k+2},\\ell_{k+3},\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   There are \\(n-k\\) of them, and each of them is a line of the required family.\n\n2. **Selection of points for sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(c\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) choose the lattice point  \n\n   \\[\n   P_c=(1,c-1)\\in\\ell_c\\cap T_n .\n   \\]\n\n   The points \\(P_2,\\dots ,P_{k+1}\\) are distinct because they lie on different diagonals.\n\n3. **Sunny lines through the selected points.**  \n   Apply Lemma 3.1 to each \\(P_c\\).  \n   For each \\(c\\) obtain a sunny line \\(S_c\\) that passes through \\(P_c\\) and through no other point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   The slopes of the lines \\(S_c\\) can be chosen pairwise distinct (there are infinitely many admissible slopes), so the lines \\(S_2,\\dots ,S_{k+1}\\) are distinct.\n\n4. **The family.**  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{S_2,S_3,\\dots ,S_{k+1}\\}\\cup\\mathcal D .\n   \\]\n\n   Then \\(|\\mathcal L|=k+(n-k)=n\\); exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_c\\) are sunny.\n\n5. **Covering property.**  \n\n   * If a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on a diagonal \\(\\ell_c\\) with \\(c\\ge k+2\\), then \\(\\ell_c\\in\\mathcal D\\subset\\mathcal L\\) and the point is covered.  \n\n   * If \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\), the only point of \\(\\ell_c\\) that belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\) is the chosen point \\(P_c\\); by construction \\(P_c\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_c\\).  \n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThus for the given \\(k\\) we have produced the required family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Necessity)\n\nBecause we must use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines; therefore \\(k\\le n\\). The trivial bound \\(k\\ge0\\) also holds.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Conclusion\n\nLemma 2 together with Lemma 4 gives the necessary condition \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nLemma 3 shows that every integer in this interval is attainable.  \nConsequently the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
