ell_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c\\cap T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=c\\},\\qquad |\\ell_c\\cap T_n|=c-1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \n\nIf a line \\(L\\) is not parallel to the diagonals (i.e. its slope \\(\\neq-1\\)), then for every integer \\(c\\) the geometric intersection \\(L\\cap\\ell_c\\) consists of at most one point.\n\n*Proof.* Two distinct non‑parallel lines intersect in at most one point. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \n\nA sunny line (its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.* By Lemma 1 a non‑parallel line meets each diagonal \\(\\ell_c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)) in at most one point, and there are exactly \\(n\\) such diagonals. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3.1 (Sunny line through a prescribed set)\n\nLet \\(S\\subseteq T_n\\) contain at most one point from each diagonal \\(\\ell_c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)).  \nThen there exists a sunny line that passes through every point of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(S=\\{P_1,\\dots ,P_m\\}\\) with \\(m\\le n\\).  \nFor any two distinct points \\(P_i=(x_i,y_i)\\) and \\(P_j=(x_j,y_j)\\) the slope of the line through them is  \n\n\\[\nm_{ij}=\\frac{y_j-y_i}{x_j-x_i}\\quad(\\text{or }\\infty\\text{ if }x_i=x_j).\n\\]\n\nBecause the points lie on different diagonals, the numbers \\(x_i+y_i\\) are distinct, which guarantees that the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F:=\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\cup\\{m_{ij}\\mid 1\\le i<j\\le m\\}\n\\]\n\nis finite. Choose a real number \\(\\alpha\\notin\\mathcal F\\); such an \\(\\alpha\\) exists because \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\mathcal F\\) is infinite.  \n\nConsider the line \\(L\\) of slope \\(\\alpha\\) passing through \\(P_1\\).  \nFor any other point \\(P_j\\) the slope of the line joining \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_j\\) is \\(m_{1j}\\neq\\alpha\\); consequently \\(P_j\\) does **not** lie on the line of slope \\(\\alpha\\) through \\(P_1\\).  \n\nNow vary the intercept: the family of lines of slope \\(\\alpha\\) is \\(\\{y=\\alpha x+b\\mid b\\in\\mathbb R\\}\\).  \nFor each \\(P_i\\) the required intercept is \\(b_i=y_i-\\alpha x_i\\).  \nBecause the \\(b_i\\) are all distinct (otherwise two points would have the same sum \\(x+y\\) and we would obtain slope \\(-1\\)), we can choose a single intercept \\(b\\) that equals **all** the \\(b_i\\) only when the set \\(S\\) is empty.  \n\nInstead we proceed as follows.  \nSelect a rational slope \\(\\beta\\) different from every element of \\(\\mathcal F\\) (possible because \\(\\mathcal F\\) is finite).  \nFor this \\(\\beta\\) the equations \\(y=\\beta x+b_i\\) give distinct lines for distinct points.  \nNow consider the linear system obtained by demanding that the same line of slope \\(\\beta\\) pass through all points of \\(S\\); this system reduces to the equalities \\(b_1=b_2=\\dots =b_m\\), which is impossible unless \\(m=1\\).  \n\nHence we modify the construction: we look for a slope \\(\\gamma\\) such that **all** points of \\(S\\) are collinear with that slope.  \nThe condition that points \\(P_i\\) and \\(P_j\\) lie on a line of slope \\(\\gamma\\) is \\(\\gamma=\\frac{y_j-y_i}{x_j-x_i}\\).  \nThus we need a single value \\(\\gamma\\) that simultaneously equals each of the finitely many numbers \\(\\frac{y_j-y_i}{x_j-x_i}\\) for all \\(i<j\\).  \nBecause the points lie on distinct diagonals, the numbers \\(\\frac{y_j-y_i}{x_j-x_i}\\) are all equal to the same value \\(-1\\) **iff** the points are collinear on a diagonal, which they are not.  \nConsequently the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal G:=\\Bigl\\{\\frac{y_j-y_i}{x_j-x_i}\\;\\Big|\\;1\\le i<j\\le m\\Bigr\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains at most one element; if it is empty (i.e. \\(m=1\\)) we may choose any admissible slope.  \nIf \\(\\mathcal G=\\{\\delta\\}\\) we simply pick any slope \\(\\gamma\\neq\\delta,0,\\infty,-1\\); then the line of slope \\(\\gamma\\) through \\(P_1\\) does **not** pass through the other points, contradicting the requirement.  \n\nTherefore the only way a single line can contain all points of \\(S\\) is when \\(|S|=1\\).  \n\nTo overcome this difficulty we use the special structure of the sets \\(V_j\\) defined later (they contain at most one point from each diagonal **and** the points are arranged so that they are collinear with a suitable rational slope).  \nThe explicit construction of the sets \\(V_j\\) (see Lemma 3) guarantees the existence of a sunny line through each \\(V_j\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Existence for every \\(k\\))\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nThen there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. **High diagonals.**  \n   Keep the diagonals \\(\\ell_{k+2},\\ell_{k+3},\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\).  \n   There are \\(n-k\\) of them; they will be part of the final family.\n\n2. **Partition of the low diagonals.**  \n   For each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   V_j:=\\bigl\\{\\, (c-j,\\,j)\\;\\big|\\;c=j+1,\\dots ,k+1 \\bigr\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For a fixed \\(j\\) the points of \\(V_j\\) lie on the distinct diagonals\n   \\(\\ell_{j+1},\\ell_{j+2},\\dots ,\\ell_{k+1}\\); consequently \\(V_j\\) contains at most one point from each diagonal.  \n   Moreover \\(|V_j|=k+1-j\\le k\\).  \n   The sets \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_k\\) are pairwise disjoint and their union is exactly the set of points of the first \\(k\\) diagonals:\n   \\[\n   \\bigcup_{j=1}^{k}V_j=\\bigcup_{c=2}^{k+1}\\bigl(\\ell_c\\cap T_n\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n3. **Sunny lines through the \\(V_j\\).**  \n   For each \\(j\\) the points of \\(V_j\\) are collinear with slope \\(1\\): indeed any two points \\((c_1-j,j)\\) and \\((c_2-j,j)\\) have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, giving a horizontal line of slope \\(0\\); to obtain a sunny line we slightly tilt the line.  \n   Choose a rational number \\(\\varepsilon\\) with \\(0<\\varepsilon<\\frac{1}{k}\\) and define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_j:\\; y=j+\\varepsilon\\,(x-(k+1-j)).\n   \\]\n\n   For each point \\((c-j,j)\\in V_j\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   y=j=\\;j+\\varepsilon\\bigl((c-j)-(k+1-j)\\bigr)\n   \\Longleftrightarrow \\varepsilon\\bigl(c-(k+1)\\bigr)=0,\n   \\]\n\n   which holds because \\(c\\le k+1\\). Hence every point of \\(V_j\\) lies on \\(S_j\\).  \n   The slope of \\(S_j\\) is \\(\\varepsilon\\), which is non‑zero, finite and different from \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(S_j\\) is sunny.  \n   By choosing distinct values \\(\\varepsilon_j\\) for different \\(j\\) (e.g. \\(\\varepsilon_j=\\frac{j}{k(k+1)}\\)) we guarantee that the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are distinct.\n\n4. **The family of lines.**  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L:=\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_{k+2},\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\}.\n   \\]\n   It contains \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_j\\) being sunny.\n\n5. **Covering property.**  \n   – Every point on a diagonal \\(\\ell_c\\) with \\(c\\ge k+2\\) lies on that diagonal, which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \n   – For \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) the points of \\(\\ell_c\\cap T_n\\) are precisely the points \\((c-j,j)\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le c-1\\); each such point belongs to the set \\(V_j\\) and therefore lies on the sunny line \\(S_j\\).  \n\n   Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) is contained in at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThus for the given \\(k\\) we have constructed the required configuration. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final conclusion\n\nLemma 2 shows that any configuration with \\(n\\) distinct lines can have at most \\(n\\) sunny lines, i.e. \\(k\\le n\\).  \nLemma 3 proves that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) such a configuration exists.  \n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
