ints of \\(V_j\\) are collinear with slope \\(1\\): indeed any two points \\((c_1-j,j)\\) and \\((c_2-j,j)\\) have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, giving a horizontal line of slope \\(0\\); to obtain a sunny line we slightly tilt the line.  \n   Choose a rational number \\(\\varepsilon\\) with \\(0<\\varepsilon<\\frac{1}{k}\\) and define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_j:\\; y=j+\\varepsilon\\,(x-(k+1-j)).\n   \\]\n\n   For each point \\((c-j,j)\\in V_j\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   y=j=\\;j+\\varepsilon\\bigl((c-j)-(k+1-j)\\bigr)\n   \\Longleftrightarrow \\varepsilon\\bigl(c-(k+1)\\bigr)=0,\n   \\]\n\n   which holds because \\(c\\le k+1\\). Hence every point of \\(V_j\\) lies on \\(S_j\\).  \n   The slope of \\(S_j\\) is \\(\\varepsilon\\), which is non\u2011zero, finite and different from \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(S_j\\) is sunny.  \n   By choosing distinct values \\(\\varepsilon_j\\) for different \\(j\\) (e.g. \\(\\varepsilon_j=\\frac{j}{k(k+1)}\\)) we guarantee that the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are distinct.\n\n4. **The family of lines.**  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L:=\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_{k+2},\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\}.\n   \\]\n   It contains \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_j\\) being sunny.\n\n5. **Covering property.**  \n   \u2013 Every point on a diagonal \\(\\ell_c\\) with \\(c\\ge k+2\\) lies on that diagonal, which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \n   \u2013 For \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) the points of \\(\\ell_c\\cap T_n\\) are precisely the points \\((c-j,j)\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le c-1\\); each such point belongs to the set \\(V_j\\) and therefore lies on the sunny line \\(S_j\\).  \n\n   Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) is contained in at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThus for the given \\(k\\) we have constructed the required configuration. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final conclusion\n\nLemma\u202f2 shows that any configuration with \\(n\\)