lta,0,\\infty,-1\\); then the line of slope \\(\\gamma\\) through \\(P_1\\) does **not** pass through the other points, contradicting the requirement.  \n\nTherefore the only way a single line can contain all points of \\(S\\) is when \\(|S|=1\\).  \n\nTo overcome this difficulty we use the special structure of the sets \\(V_j\\) defined later (they contain at most one point from each diagonal **and** the points are arranged so that they are collinear with a suitable rational slope).  \nThe explicit construction of the sets \\(V_j\\) (see Lemma 3) guarantees the existence of a sunny line through each \\(V_j\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Existence for every \\(k\\))\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nThen there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. **High diagonals.**  \n   Keep the diagonals \\(\\ell_{k+2},\\ell_{k+3},\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\).  \n   There are \\(n-k\\) of them; they will be part of the final family.\n\n2. **Partition of the low diagonals.**  \n   For each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   V_j:=\\bigl\\{\\, (c-j,\\,j)\\;\\big|\\;c=j+1,\\dots ,k+1 \\bigr\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For a fixed \\(j\\) the points of \\(V_j\\) lie on the distinct diagonals\n   \\(\\ell_{j+1},\\ell_{j+2},\\dots ,\\ell_{k+1}\\); consequently \\(V_j\\) contains at most one point from each diagonal.  \n   Moreover \\(|V_j|=k+1-j\\le k\\).  \n   The sets \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_k\\) are pairwise disjoint and their union is exactly the set of points of the first \\(k\\) diagonals:\n   \\[\n   \\bigcup_{j=1}^{k}V_j=\\bigcup_{c=2}^{k+1}\\bigl(\\ell_c\\cap T_n\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n3. **Sunny lines through the \\(V_j\\).**  \n   For each \\(j\\) the points of \\(V_j\\) are collinear with slope \\(1\\): indeed any two points \\((c_1-j,j)\\) and \\((c_2-j,j)\\) have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, giving a