            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nd_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\nd_c\\cap T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=c\\},\\qquad |d_c\\cap T_n|=c-1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \n\nIf a line \\(L\\) is not parallel to the diagonals (i.e. its slope is not \\(-1\\)), then for every \\(c\\) the geometric intersection \\(L\\cap d_c\\) consists of at most one point.\n\n*Proof.* Two distinct non‑parallel lines intersect in at most one point. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \n\nA sunny line (its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) contains at most one point of \\(T_n\\) from each diagonal \\(d_c\\); consequently it contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.* By Lemma 1 a line that is not parallel to the diagonals meets each diagonal \\(d_c\\) in at most one point.  \nIf the intersection point lies in \\(T_n\\) then it belongs to that diagonal; otherwise it does not contribute to the count.  \nSince there are exactly \\(n\\) diagonals \\(d_c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\), the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can lie on the line is at most \\(n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Existence for every \\(k\\))\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nThen there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. **High diagonals.**  \n   Keep the diagonals  \n   \\[\n   d_{k+2},d_{k+3},\\dots ,d_{n+1}.\n   \\]\n   There are \\(n-k\\) of them; they will be part of the final family.\n\n2. **Lines of slope \\(1\\).**  \n   For each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n   \\[\n   s_j:\\; y = x + (j-1). \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   The slope of each \\(s_j\\) is \\(1\\); therefore every \\(s_j\\) is sunny (it is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).\n\n3. **Every low‑sum point lies on a unique \\(s_j\\).**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a+b\\le k+1\\).  \n   Set \\(j:=b-a+1\\).  \n   Because \\(a,b\\ge1\\) we have \\(1\\le j\\le k\\).  \n   Then \\(b = a + (j-1)\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies equation (2) of \\(s_j\\); hence \\((a,b)\\in s_j\\).\n\n   Conversely, if \\((a,b)\\) lies on some \\(s_j\\) then \\(b-a=j-1\\), whence \\(a+b = 2a + j -1\\).  \n   Since \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(j\\le k\\), we obtain \\(a+b\\le 2\\cdot\\frac{k-j+2}{2}+j-1 = k+1\\); thus \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and its sum does not exceed \\(k+1\\).\n\n   Therefore the set of points of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) is exactly the disjoint union  \n   \\[\n   \\bigcup_{j=1}^{k}\\bigl(s_j\\cap T_n\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n4. **The final family of lines.**  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L:=\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\}\\cup\\{d_{k+2},d_{k+3},\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\}.\n   \\]\n   The family contains \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines.  \n   The \\(k\\) lines \\(s_j\\) have slope \\(1\\) and are sunny; the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are the diagonals \\(d_c\\) (slope \\(-1\\)) and are not sunny.\n\n5. **Covering property.**  \n   – If \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\ge k+2\\) then it lies on the diagonal \\(d_{a+b}\\), which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \n   – If \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) then, as shown in step 3, it lies on the unique line \\(s_j\\) with \\(j=b-a+1\\).  \n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThus for the given \\(k\\) we have constructed the required configuration. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Conclusion\n\nLemma 2 shows that any configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain at most \\(n\\) sunny lines, i.e. \\(k\\le n\\).  \nLemma 3 proves that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) such a configuration exists.  \n\nConsequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
