            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nd_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\nd_c\\cap T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=c\\},\\qquad |d_c\\cap T_n|=c-1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \n\nIf a line \\(L\\) is not parallel to the diagonals (i.e. its slope is not \\(-1\\)), then for every \\(c\\) the geometric intersection \\(L\\cap d_c\\) consists of at most one point.\n\n*Proof.* Two distinct non‑parallel lines intersect in at most one point. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \n\nA sunny line (its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) contains at most one point of \\(T_n\\) from each diagonal \\(d_c\\); consequently it contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.* By Lemma 1 a line that is not parallel to the diagonals meets each diagonal \\(d_c\\) in at most one point.  \nIf such an intersection point belongs to \\(T_n\\) then it is counted; otherwise it does not affect the count.  \nSince there are exactly \\(n\\) diagonals \\(d_c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\), the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can lie on the line is at most \\(n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Construction for every \\(k\\))\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nThen there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*\n\n1. **High diagonals.**  \n   Keep the diagonals  \n   \\[\n   d_{k+2},d_{k+3},\\dots ,d_{n+1}.\n   \\]\n   There are \\(n-k\\) of them; they will be part of the final family.\n\n2. **Lines of slope \\(1\\).**  \n   For each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n   \\[\n   s_j:\\; y = x + (j-1). \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   The slope of each \\(s_j\\) is \\(1\\); therefore every \\(s_j\\) is sunny (it is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).\n\n3. **Every low‑sum point lies on a suitable \\(s_j\\).**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfy \\(a+b\\le k+1\\).  \n   Set \\(j:=b-a+1\\). Because \\(a,b\\ge1\\) we have\n   \\[\n   1\\le j = b-a+1 \\le (k+1)-2+1 = k .\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) and the equality \\(b = a + (j-1)\\) shows that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies equation (2) of \\(s_j\\); thus \\((a,b)\\in s_j\\).\n\n   Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) whose coordinates sum to at most \\(k+1\\) belongs to one of the lines \\(s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\).\n\n4. **The final family of lines.**  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L:=\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\}\\cup\\{d_{k+2},d_{k+3},\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\}.\n   \\]\n   The family contains \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines.  \n   Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(s_j\\) have slope \\(1\\) and are sunny; the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are the diagonals \\(d_c\\) (slope \\(-1\\)) and are not sunny.\n\n5. **Verification of the covering condition.**  \n\n   *If \\(a+b\\ge k+2\\):* then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(d_{a+b}\\), which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\) by construction.\n\n   *If \\(a+b\\le k+1\\):* by step 3 the point lies on the unique line \\(s_j\\) with \\(j=b-a+1\\).\n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThus for the given \\(k\\) we have produced \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, covering all required lattice points. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Conclusion\n\nLemma 2 shows that any admissible configuration can contain at most \\(n\\) sunny lines, i.e. \\(k\\le n\\).  \nLemma 3 demonstrates that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) such a configuration exists.  \n\nTherefore the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
