            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nd_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\nd_c\\cap T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=c\\},\n\\qquad \n|d_c\\cap T_n|=c-1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nIf a line \\(L\\) is not parallel to the diagonals (i.e. its slope is not \\(-1\\)), then for every \\(c\\) the geometric intersection \\(L\\cap d_c\\) consists of at most one point.\n\n*Proof.* Two distinct non‑parallel lines intersect in at most one point. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nA sunny line (its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) contains at most one point of each diagonal \\(d_c\\); consequently it contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.* By Lemma 1 a line that is not parallel to the diagonals meets each diagonal in at most one point. Since there are exactly \\(n\\) diagonals \\(d_c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\), the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can lie on the line is at most \\(n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Necessary bound on \\(k\\))\n\nIn any admissible configuration the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies \\(k\\le1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe diagonal \\(d_{2}\\) consists of the single point \\((1,1)\\). If \\(d_{2}\\) is omitted from the family of lines, the point \\((1,1)\\) must be covered by a sunny line. Let \\(\\ell\\) be such a sunny line. By Lemma 1, \\(\\ell\\) meets each diagonal in at most one point, so \\(\\ell\\) can cover at most one point from each diagonal.\n\nConsider the first \\(m\\) diagonals \\(d_{2},d_{3},\\dots ,d_{m+1}\\) where \\(m\\ge2\\). By (1) they contain  \n\n\\[\n1+2+\\dots +m=\\frac{m(m+1)}{2}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\). If we omit all these \\(m\\) diagonals, all those points would have to be covered by sunny lines, each sunny line contributing at most one point from each diagonal, i.e. at most \\(m\\) points in total. Hence we would need at least \\(\\frac{m(m+1)}{2m}= \\frac{m+1}{2}\\) sunny lines for each diagonal, which for \\(m\\ge2\\) exceeds one. Therefore at most one of the diagonals can be omitted; all the others must remain in the family. Consequently at most one sunny line can appear, i.e. \\(k\\le1\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Realisation of \\(k=0\\))\n\nThere exists a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake the \\(n\\) diagonals \\(d_{2},d_{3},\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\). None of them is sunny, they are distinct, and by (1) every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly one of them. Hence the required configuration exists with \\(k=0\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5 (Realisation of \\(k=1\\))\n\nThere exists a configuration with \\(k=1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nKeep the diagonals \\(d_{3},d_{4},\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\) (there are \\(n-1\\) of them). Replace the diagonal \\(d_{2}\\) by any sunny line passing through the point \\((1,1)\\); for example  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y = 2x-1\n\\]\n\nhas slope \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) and therefore is sunny.  \nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell\\}\\cup\\{d_{3},d_{4},\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, precisely one of which (\\(\\ell\\)) is sunny. By construction \\(\\ell\\) covers the point \\((1,1)\\) that was on \\(d_{2}\\), while the remaining points of \\(T_n\\) are covered by the retained diagonals. Hence all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered and the configuration satisfies the conditions with \\(k=1\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Conclusion\n\nLemma 3 shows that any admissible configuration must have \\(k\\le1\\). Lemma 4 and Lemma 5 exhibit configurations achieving \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\), respectively. Therefore the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
