.\n   \\]\n\n   There are \\(n-(k+1)\\) such diagonals, and they are pairwise distinct. Adding them to the family gives a total of  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\;(\\ell_{0})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{k})\\;+\\;\\bigl(n-(k+1)\\bigr)=n\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines.\n\n   *Verification of the covering property.*  \n   - Points with \\(x=1\\) are on \\(\\ell_{0}\\).  \n   - Points with \\(2\\le x\\le k+1\\) are on the corresponding sunny line \\(\\ell_{x}\\).  \n   - Points with \\(x\\ge k+2\\) lie on a diagonal \\(d_{c}\\) with \\(c\\ge k+3\\), and that diagonal is part of the family.  \n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of the constructed family.\n\n   *Count of sunny lines.*  \n   Exactly the lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{k}\\) are sunny, so the family contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   This construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) (the case \\(k=0\\) is obtained by omitting all \\(\\ell_{i}\\) and keeping only \\(\\ell_{0}\\) together with the \\(n-1\\) diagonals \\(d_{3},\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\)).\n\n8. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\).**  \n   Suppose all \\(n\\) lines were sunny. By Lemma\u202f2 each sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\); therefore the total number of incidences between the family and \\(T_n\\) would be at most \\(n\\cdot n=n^{2}\\).  \n   However \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}>n^{2}\\) is false for \\(n\\ge3\\); the counting argument alone does not give a contradiction. The real obstruction is the point \\((1,1)\\): it lies on the diagonal \\(d_{2}\\). If no line parallel to the axes or to \\(x+y=0\\) is present, the only way to cover \\((1,1)\\) is by a sunny line. Such a line meets each diagonal in at most one point, so it can cover at most one point of the diagonal \\(d_{2}\\) (namely \\((1,1)\\)) and at most one point of every other diagonal. Consequently the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines would have to cover all points of the diagonals \\(d_{3},\\dots ,d_{n