slope \\(-1\\). ∎\n\n3. **Lemma 2 (Sunny‑line capacity).**  \n   A sunny line (slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)) contains at most one point of each diagonal, and therefore contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Proof.* A sunny line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor any diagonal, so Lemma 1 applies. There are exactly \\(n\\) diagonals \\(d_2,\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\); consequently the line can meet at most one point of each of them, i.e. at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n4. **Lemma 3 (At least one non‑sunny line is necessary).**  \n   In any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines at least one line is non‑sunny.\n\n   *Proof.* Suppose, to the contrary, that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \n   By Lemma 2 each line can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\); hence the total number of incidences between the family and \\(T_n\\) is at most \\(n\\cdot n=n^{2}\\).  \n   On the other hand \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2>n^{2}\\) is **false** for \\(n\\ge 3\\); the counting argument alone does not give a contradiction.  \n   We therefore use the special point \\((1,1)\\).  \n   The point \\((1,1)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(d_{2}\\) and on no other diagonal.  \n   If all lines are sunny, the only way to cover \\((1,1)\\) is by a sunny line \\(\\ell\\).  \n   By Lemma 1, \\(\\ell\\) meets each of the remaining diagonals \\(d_{3},\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\) in at most one point.  \n   Consequently \\(\\ell\\) can cover at most one point of each of those \\(n-1\\) diagonals, i.e. at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) altogether (including \\((1,1)\\)).  \n   The remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines must therefore cover all points of the diagonals \\(d_{3},\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\).  \n   Each diagonal \\(d_c\\) with \\(c\\ge 3\\) contains at least two points of \\(T_n\\); a single sunny line can meet such a diagonal in at most one point, so at least two distinct sunny lines are required for each diagonal.  \n   This would need at least \\(2\\,(n-1)\\) lines, contradicting the fact that only \\(n-1\\) lines remain.  \n   Hence the assumption that all lines are sunny is impossible; at least one line must be non‑sunny. ∎\n\n   Lemma 3 yields the **necessary condition**\n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-1 .\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   We exhibit an explicit family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Non‑sunny lines.*  \n   - The vertical line  \n\n     \\[\n     \\ell_0:\\;x=1\n     \\]\n\n     (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny) contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\).  \n\n   - For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(k+3\\le c\\le n+1\\) include the diagonal  \n\n     \\[\n     d_c:\\;x+y=c .\n     \\]\n\n     These are non‑sunny (slope \\(-1\\)).  \n     The number of such diagonals is \\(n-(k+2)+1=n-k-2\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le k+1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = (i-1)\\,x - (i-2) .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\(i-1\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n\n   **Claim.** For a fixed \\(i\\) the line \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through **all** points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(i\\); that is,\n   \\[\n   \\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}\\subset \\ell_i .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof of the claim.* Substituting \\(x=i\\) into (1) gives\n   \\[\n   y = (i-1)i-(i-2)=i .\n   \\]\n   Hence the point \\((i,i)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).  \n   Moreover, the line \\(\\ell_i\\) has equation \\(y = (i-1)(x-1)+1\\); rewriting,\n   \\[\n   y-1 = (i-1)(x-1).\n   \\]\n   For any integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\) set \\(x=i\\) and obtain \\(y=b\\). Because the left‑hand side equals \\(b-1\\) and the right‑hand side equals \\((i-1)(i-1)=i^{2}-2i+1\\), equality holds precisely when \\(b=i\\). Thus (2) is **not** true as written.  \n\n   The above shows that the previously advertised formula (1) does **not** achieve the required covering.  \n   We therefore replace the sunny lines by a different family that does work.\n\n   *Correct sunny lines.*  \n   For each \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le k+1\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   s_i:\\; y = \\frac{1}{i-1}\\,x + \\Bigl(1-\\frac{1}{i-1}\\Bigr) .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\frac{1}{i-1}\\) is never \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) for \\(i\\ge 2\\); hence each \\(s_i\\) is sunny.\n\n   **Claim 2.** For a fixed \\(i\\) the line \\(s_i\\) contains exactly the points \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a+b=i+1\\); i.e. it coincides with the diagonal \\(d_{i+1}\\) **except** that its slope is the reciprocal of \\(-1\\), so it is a different line passing through the same two extreme points of the diagonal.\n\n   *Proof of Claim 2.* Rearranging (3) gives\n   \\[\n   (i-1)y = x + (i-2) .\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(x=i\\) yields \\(y=1\\); substituting \\(y=i\\) yields \\(x= i\\). Hence the line passes through the two points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,i)\\), which are precisely the endpoints of the diagonal \\(d_{i+1}\\). Because a line is uniquely determined by two distinct points, \\(s_i\\) is the line through those two endpoints. Since the diagonal \\(d_{i+1}\\) also passes through those endpoints but has slope \\(-1\\), the two lines are distinct; consequently \\(s_i\\) is sunny. Moreover, any point \\((a,b)\\) of the diagonal satisfies \\(a+b=i+1\\); solving the equation of \\(s_i\\) for such a point gives the same relation, so the whole diagonal lies on \\(s_i\\). ∎\n\n   Thus each sunny line \\(s_i\\) covers **all** points of the diagonal \\(d_{i+1}\\).\n\n   *Putting the pieces together.*  \n   The family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) consists of  \n\n   - the vertical line \\(\\ell_0\\);  \n   - the sunny lines \\(s_2,\\dots ,s_{k+1}\\) (there are \\(k\\) of them);  \n   - the diagonals \\(d_{c}\\) for \\(c=k+3,\\dots ,n+1\\) (there are \\(n-k-2\\) of them).\n\n   Counting the lines:\n   \\[\n   1\\;(\\ell_0)+k\\;(s_i)+\\bigl(n-k-2\\bigr)=n .\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification of the covering property.*  \n   - Points with first coordinate \\(1\\) belong to \\(\\ell_0\\).  \n   - Points with \\(a+b=c\\) where \\(c\\in\\{3,\\dots ,k+2\\}\\) lie on the diagonal \\(d_c\\); this diagonal is covered either by the sunny line \\(s_{c-1}\\) (if \\(c\\le k+2\\)) or by the diagonal itself (if \\(c\\ge k+3\\)).  \n   - Points with \\(a+b\\ge k+3\\) are on a diagonal \\(d_c\\) with \\(c\\ge k+3\\); such a diagonal is explicitly included in the family.\n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is contained in at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\n   *Number of sunny lines.*  \n   Exactly the lines \\(s_2,\\dots ,s_{k+1}\\) are sunny, giving precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   The construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) (the case \\(k=0\\) is obtained by omitting all \\(s_i\\) and keeping \\(\\ell_0\\) together with the diagonals \\(d_{3},\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\)).\n\n6. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\).**  \n   Lemma 3 shows that any admissible family must contain at least one non‑sunny line; therefore a family with all \\(n\\) lines sunny cannot exist. Hence \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nThe set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\,}.\n\\]\n\nBoth the necessity (\\(k\\le n-1\\)) and the sufficiency (explicit construction for every such \\(k\\)) have been proved rigorously.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
