ppose, to the contrary, that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \n   By Lemma\u202f2 each of them contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\); therefore the total number of incidences between the family and \\(T_n\\) is at most \\(n\\cdot n=n^{2}\\).\n\n   The set \\(T_n\\) contains  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n   \\]\n\n   points.  For \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2>n^{2}\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad n(3-n)>0,\n   \\]\n\n   which is false; the counting argument alone does not give a contradiction.  \n   We therefore use the structure of the diagonals.\n\n   The diagonal \\(d_{2}\\) consists of the single point \\((1,1)\\).  \n   Any sunny line that passes through \\((1,1)\\) meets each of the remaining diagonals\n   \\(d_{3},d_{4},\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\) in at most one point (Lemma\u202f1).  \n   Hence such a line can cover at most one point of each of those \\(n-1\\) diagonals, i.e. at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) altogether (including \\((1,1)\\)).\n\n   The remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines must therefore cover **all** points of the diagonals\n   \\(d_{3},\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\).  \n   For any \\(c\\ge 3\\) the diagonal \\(d_c\\) contains at least two points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Because a sunny line can meet \\(d_c\\) in at most one point, at least two distinct lines are required to cover the points of each such diagonal.  \n   Consequently at least \\(2\\,(n-1)\\) lines are needed, contradicting the fact that only \\(n-1\\) lines remain.\n\n   Hence the assumption that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny is impossible; at least one line must be non\u2011sunny. \u220e\n\n   Lemma\u202f3 yields the **necessary condition** \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\n5. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   We exhibit an explicit family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n   - The vertical line  \n\n     \\[\n     \\ell_0:\\;