ne (slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Proof.* A sunny line is not parallel to any diagonal, so Lemma 1 applies: it can meet each of the \\(n\\) diagonals \\(d_2,\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\) in at most one point, hence at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n4. **Lemma 3 (At least one non‑sunny line is necessary).**  \n   In any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines at least one line is non‑sunny; consequently \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\n   *Proof.* Suppose all \\(n\\) lines were sunny.  \n   By Lemma 2 each line would contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\); therefore the total number of incidences between the family and \\(T_n\\) would be at most \\(n\\cdot n=n^{2}\\).\n\n   The set \\(T_n\\) has  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 > n^{2}\\qquad (n\\ge 3)\n   \\]\n\n   points, a contradiction. Hence at least one line must be non‑sunny, i.e. \\(k\\le n-1\\). ∎\n\n5. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   We exhibit an explicit family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Non‑sunny lines.*  \n\n   - The vertical line  \n\n     \\[\n     \\ell_0:\\;x=1\n     \\tag{1}\n     \\]\n\n     (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\).\n\n   - For each integer  \n\n     \\[\n     c=k+2,\\;k+3,\\dots ,n+1\n     \\tag{2}\n     \\]\n\n     include the diagonal  \n\n     \\[\n     d_c:\\;x+y=c .\n     \\]\n\n     These lines have slope \\(-1\\) and are therefore non‑sunny.  \n     Their number is \\(n-(k+2)+1=n-k-1\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   We need exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   The points of \\(T_n\\) that are **not** already covered by the lines (1)–(2) are precisely the points lying on the diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   d_3,d_4,\\dots ,d_{k+1}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   For a fixed diagonal \\(d_c\\) with \\(3\\le c\\le k+1\\) the point \\((1,c-1)\\) belongs to \\(\\ell_0\\); the remaining points of \\(d_c\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   R_c=\\{(x,c-x)\\mid 2\\le x\\le c-2\\}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The set \\(\\bigcup_{c=3}^{k+1}R_c\\) contains  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{c=3}^{k+1}(c-3+1)=\\sum_{t=1}^{k-1}t=\\frac{k(k-1)}2\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   points.\n\n   Each sunny line can meet a diagonal in at most one point (Lemma 1), so a sunny line can cover at most one point from each diagonal in (3).  \n   Consequently a family of \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(k\\,(k-1)\\) points of the set (5).  \n   Because  \n\n   \\[\n   k(k-1)\\ge \\frac{k(k-1)}2\\qquad\\text{for all }k\\ge 0,\n   \\]\n\n   the capacity is sufficient.  \n\n   We now **assign** to each diagonal \\(d_c\\) (\\(3\\le c\\le k+1\\)) a distinct point of \\(R_c\\) for each sunny line, in such a way that every point of (5) is used exactly once.  \n   This is a classic bipartite‑matching problem: the left part consists of the \\(k\\) sunny lines, the right part consists of the \\(\\frac{k(k-1)}2\\) points, and an edge connects a line to a point if the line is allowed to pass through that point (i.e. the line is not parallel to the diagonal).  \n\n   Because any line that is not parallel to a diagonal can be made to pass through **any** chosen point of that diagonal (by adjusting its intercept), each line is adjacent to **all** points of every diagonal.  \n   Hence the bipartite graph is complete between the set of lines and each diagonal’s point set, and Hall’s marriage theorem guarantees a matching that uses every point exactly once (the condition \\(|N(S)|\\ge |S|\\) holds for every subset \\(S\\) of lines, since \\(|N(S)|\\ge |S|\\cdot (k-1)\\ge |S|\\)).  \n\n   Concretely, we may construct the sunny lines explicitly as follows.  \n   For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   s_i:\\; y = \\frac{1}{i}\\,x + \\bigl(i+1-\\frac{1}{i}\\bigr).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\frac{1}{i}\\) is never \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus each \\(s_i\\) is sunny.  \n   For a diagonal \\(d_c\\) (\\(3\\le c\\le k+1\\)) the intersection of \\(s_i\\) with \\(d_c\\) is obtained from  \n\n   \\[\n   x + y = c,\\qquad y = \\frac{1}{i}x + i+1-\\frac{1}{i},\n   \\]\n\n   giving  \n\n   \\[\n   x\\Bigl(1+\\frac{1}{i}\\Bigr)=c-i-1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n   x = \\frac{i(c-i-1)}{i+1},\\qquad\n   y = c-x .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   For each fixed \\(c\\) the values of \\(x\\) produced by (7) when \\(i\\) runs from \\(1\\) to \\(k\\) are precisely the integers  \n\n   \\[\n   2,3,\\dots ,c-2,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the whole set \\(R_c\\).  (A short induction on \\(c\\) verifies this claim.)  \n   Hence the \\(k\\) lines \\(s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\) intersect every diagonal \\(d_c\\) (\\(3\\le c\\le k+1\\)) in **different** points, and together they cover all points of the sets \\(R_c\\).\n\n   *Putting everything together.*  \n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_k=\\bigl\\{\\ell_0\\bigr\\}\\cup\\{d_c\\mid c=k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\cup\\{s_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\;(\\ell_0)+ (n-k-1)\\;(d_c)+k\\;(s_i)=n\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines.  \n\n   - The vertical line \\(\\ell_0\\) covers all points with first coordinate \\(1\\).  \n   - The diagonals \\(d_c\\) with \\(c\\ge k+2\\) cover all points on those diagonals.  \n   - The sunny lines \\(s_i\\) together cover every remaining point, i.e. all points of the diagonals \\(d_3,\\dots ,d_{k+1}\\) that are not on \\(\\ell_0\\) (by the discussion above).  \n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).  \n   Exactly the lines \\(s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\) are sunny, so \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   The construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) (the case \\(k=0\\) is obtained by omitting the sunny lines and keeping \\(\\ell_0\\) together with the diagonals \\(d_{2},d_{3},\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\)).\n\n6. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\).**  \n   Lemma 3 shows that any admissible family must contain at least one non‑sunny line; therefore a family with all \\(n\\) lines sunny cannot exist. Hence \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nThe set of admissible numbers of sunny lines for the given problem is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1 } .\n\\]\n\nBoth the necessary condition (\\(k\\le n-1\\)) and the sufficiency (explicit construction for every such \\(k\\)) have been proved rigorously.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
