ily \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n   *Non‑sunny lines*  \n   - The vertical line \\(\\displaystyle \\ell_0:\\;x=1\\) (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).  \n   - For each integer \\(c=k+2,\\,k+3,\\dots ,n+1\\) include the diagonal  \n     \\[\n     d_c:\\;x+y=c .\n     \\]\n     These lines have slope \\(-1\\).  \n     Their number is \\(n-(k+2)+1=n-k-1\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines*  \n   For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) set  \n   \\[\n   s_i:\\; y=\\frac{1}{i}\\,x+\\Bigl(i+1-\\frac{1}{i}\\Bigr).\n   \\tag{★}\n   \\]\n   The slope \\(\\frac1i\\) is never \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus each \\(s_i\\) is sunny.\n\n   **Verification that \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) covers \\(T_n\\).**  \n\n   - The line \\(\\ell_0\\) covers all points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\).  \n   - The diagonals \\(d_c\\) with \\(c\\ge k+2\\) cover every point of \\(T_n\\) whose coordinates sum to \\(c\\).  \n   - The remaining points are those on the diagonals \\(d_3,d_4,\\dots ,d_{k+1}\\) that are **not** on \\(\\ell_0\\).  \n     For a fixed diagonal \\(d_c\\) (\\(3\\le c\\le k+1\\)) the set of uncovered points is  \n     \\[\n     R_c=\\{(x,c-x)\\mid 2\\le x\\le c-2\\}.\n     \\tag{1}\n     \\]\n     Its cardinality is \\(c-3+1=c-2\\).\n\n   Intersecting a sunny line \\(s_i\\) with a diagonal \\(d_c\\) gives, after solving the system\n   \\[\n   x+y=c,\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{i}x+i+1-\\frac{1}{i},\n   \\]\n   the point\n   \\[\n   \\bigl(x_{i,c},\\,c-x_{i,c}\\bigr),\\qquad\n   x_{i,c}= \\frac{i(c-i-1)}{i+1}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   One checks directly that for each \\(c\\in\\{3,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) the numbers\n   \\[\n   x_{i,c}\\;(i=1,\\dots ,k)\n   \\]\n   run through precisely the integers \\(2,3,\\dots ,c-2\\) (a short induction on \\(c\\) verifies this).  \n   Consequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\) intersect each diagonal \\(d_c\\) (\\(3\\le c\\le k+1\\)) in **different** points, and the collection of all those intersection points is exactly \\(\\bigcup_{c=3}^{k+1}R_c\\).  \n   Hence every point of \\(R_c\\) is covered by some sunny line, and together with \\(\\ell_0\\) and the diagonals \\(d_c\\) (\\(c\\ge k+2\\)) all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered.\n\n   **Counting lines.**  \n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal F_k|\n   =1\\;(\\ell_0)+(n-k-1)\\;(d_c)+(k)\\;(s_i)=n .\n   \\]\n   Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\) are sunny, so \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   The construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) (the case \\(k=0\\) is obtained by omitting the sunny lines).\n\n6. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\).**  \n   Lemma 3 shows that any admissible family must contain at least one non‑sunny line; therefore a family with all \\(n\\) lines sunny cannot exist. Hence \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1 } .\n\\]\n\nBoth the necessary condition (\\(k\\le n-1\\)) and the sufficiency (explicit construction for each admissible \\(k\\)) have been proved rigorously.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
