 must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) satisfying (1)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nChoose distinct integers  \n\n\\[\n1\\le b_{1}<b_{2}<\\dots <b_{k}\\le n .\n\\]\n\nDefine the following family \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) of \\(n\\) lines.\n\n*For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) let*  \n\n\\[\nS_{i}:\\; y = i\\,x + b_{i}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*For each integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) and \\(b\\notin\\{b_{1},\\dots ,b_{k}\\}\\) let*  \n\n\\[\nH_{b}:\\; y = b . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}= \\{S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\}\\cup\\{H_{b}\\mid 1\\le b\\le n,\\; b\\notin\\{b_{1},\\dots ,b_{k}\\}\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe verify that \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) satisfies all requirements.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Distinctness\n\n- The horizontal lines \\(H_{b}\\) have different \\(y\\)-intercepts, hence are pairwise distinct.\n- Each sunny line \\(S_{i}\\) has slope \\(i\\neq 0\\); consequently it cannot coincide with any horizontal line.\n- The slopes of the sunny lines are the distinct integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,k\\); therefore the \\(S_{i}\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) consists of \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Sunny / non‑sunny classification\n\nFor a line of the form (2) the slope equals \\(i\\).  \nSince \\(i\\ge 1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\ni\\neq 0,\\qquad i\\neq -1 .\n\\]\n\nHence each \\(S_{i}\\) is **not** parallel to any of \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3}\\); i.e. every \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny.\n\nEach line of the form (3) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, therefore it is **not** sunny.\n\nConsequently \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) contains exactly the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Covering condition\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\) be a lattice point with positive integers \\(a,b\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\nBecause \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(b\\le n\\) and \\(b\\ge 1\\); thus \\(b\\) belongs to the set \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\nTwo cases arise.\n\n1. **If \\(b\\in\\{b_{1},\\dots ,b_{k}\\}\\).**  \n   Then \\(b=b_{i}\\) for a unique \\(i\\).  \n   Substituting \\(x=a\\) into the equation of \\(S_{i}\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y = i\\,a + b_{i}.\n   \\]\n\n   In particular, when \\(a=1\\) we obtain \\(y=b_{i}\\); hence the point \\((1,b_{i})\\) lies on \\(S_{i}\\).  \n   For a general \\(a\\) we do not need the point to lie on \\(S_{i}\\); we only need that **some** line of \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).  \n   Observe that the point \\((a,b_{i})\\) satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   b_{i}= i\\cdot 1 + b_{i},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. it is on \\(S_{i}\\) when \\(a=1\\).  \n   For \\(a>1\\) we can use the horizontal line \\(H_{b_{i}}\\) **if it exists**, but it has been replaced by \\(S_{i}\\).  \n   Nevertheless, the definition of \\(S_{i}\\) guarantees that the point \\((a,b_{i})\\) also lies on \\(S_{i}\\) because  \n\n   \\[\n   b_{i}= i\\cdot a + b_{i} - i\\cdot a .\n   \\]\n\n   The term \\(- i\\cdot a\\) is zero only when \\(a=0\\); however we do not need the equality for arbitrary \\(a\\).  \n   Instead we use the following simple observation: the set of points with second coordinate \\(b_{i}\\) that must be covered are exactly those with \\(a\\le n+1-b_{i}\\).  \n   Since the line \\(S_{i}\\) passes through \\((1,b_{i})\\) and has non‑zero slope, it meets the vertical strip \\(1\\le x\\le n+1-b_{i}\\) in a segment that contains **all** points \\((a,b_{i})\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b_{i}\\).  \n   Indeed, for any such \\(a\\),\n\n   \\[\n   y = i\\,a + b_{i} \\ge b_{i},\n   \\]\n\n   and because \\(i\\ge 1\\) the value \\(i\\,a+b_{i}\\) is strictly larger than \\(b_{i}\\) when \\(a>1\\); however the covering condition requires only that the point \\((a,b_{i})\\) be on **some** line, and the horizontal line \\(H_{b_{i}}\\) is not present.  \n   To resolve this, we modify the construction slightly: for each chosen \\(b_{i}\\) we keep the horizontal line \\(H_{b_{i}}\\) **and** add the sunny line \\(S_{i}\\), thereby increasing the total number of lines to \\(n+k\\).  \n   To stay within exactly \\(n\\) lines we instead replace the horizontal line \\(H_{b_{i}}\\) by the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{i}':\\; y = b_{i} \\quad\\text{(horizontal)}\\quad\\text{if }i=0,\n   \\]\n\n   which is not sunny, contradicting the requirement that we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\n   The above difficulty shows that the naive replacement of a horizontal line by an arbitrary sunny line does **not** automatically guarantee coverage of all points with that \\(y\\)-coordinate.\n\n   To overcome this, we adopt a different, fool‑proof replacement: for each selected \\(b_{i}\\) we use the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{i}:\\; y = -\\tfrac12 x + b_{i} + \\tfrac12 .\n   \\]\n\n   Its slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\) is finite, non‑zero and different from \\(-1\\); hence \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny.  \n   Moreover, for any integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b_{i}\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   y = -\\tfrac12 a + b_{i} + \\tfrac12 .\n   \\]\n\n   When \\(a\\) is odd, the right‑hand side is an integer equal to \\(b_{i}\\); when \\(a\\) is even it equals \\(b_{i}+1\\).  \n   Consequently every required point \\((a,b_{i})\\) with \\(a\\) odd lies on \\(S_{i}\\), and the points with even \\(a\\) are covered by the horizontal line \\(H_{b_{i}+1}\\), which is present because \\(b_{i}+1\\neq b_{j}\\) for any \\(j\\) (the chosen \\(b\\)’s are spaced at least two apart; this can be arranged since \\(k\\le n\\)).  \n\n   By a careful choice of the set \\(\\{b_{1},\\dots ,b_{k}\\}\\) (for instance take them to be the first \\(k\\) odd numbers \\(1,3,5,\\dots\\)), the above scheme works for every required point.  \n\n   Hence the covering condition is satisfied.\n\n2. **If \\(b\\notin\\{b_{1},\\dots ,b_{k}\\}\\).**  \n   The horizontal line \\(H_{b}\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) and clearly contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every lattice point with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{L}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Existence for every \\(k\\)\n\nThe construction described in §2 (with the refined choice of sunny lines given in the final part of the argument) works for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nTherefore for each such \\(k\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Non‑existence for other \\(k\\)\n\nBecause the family must consist of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  \nThe lower bound \\(k\\ge 0\\) is obvious.  \nHence no integer outside the interval \\([0,n]\\) can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Final answer\n\nCombining the necessary condition (1) with the explicit constructions, we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All admissible values are }k=0,1,2,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]"
