at covers all points of \\(T_n\\), every diagonal \\(D_c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)) must contain a line parallel to the diagonal, i.e. a line of the form \\(x+y=c\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider a fixed diagonal \\(D_c\\).  \nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to the direction vector \\((1,1)\\) of the diagonal, then by Lemma 1 it meets \\(D_c\\) in at most one point.  \nSince \\(|D_c|=c-1\\ge 1\\), a single non‑parallel line cannot cover all points of \\(D_c\\).  \nHence at least one line of the covering family must be parallel to \\((1,1)\\); such a line has equation \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\).  \nBecause it contains a point of \\(D_c\\), the constant equals \\(c\\), so the line is exactly \\(x+y=c\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Main Theorem  \n*For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the only possible number of sunny lines in a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\) is \\(k=0\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nWrite \\(\\mathcal L=\\mathcal S\\cup\\mathcal N\\) where \\(\\mathcal S\\) (size \\(k\\)) consists of the sunny lines and \\(\\mathcal N\\) (size \\(n-k\\)) consists of the non‑sunny lines.\n\nBy Proposition 2, for each diagonal \\(D_c\\) (\\(c=2,\\dots ,n+1\\)) there must be a line in \\(\\mathcal L\\) parallel to that diagonal, i.e. a line of the form \\(x+y=c\\).  \nSuch a line is **not** sunny (it is parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\)), therefore it belongs to \\(\\mathcal N\\).  \nSince the diagonals are distinct, the \\(n\\) lines \\(x+y=2, x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\) are all different, and each of them must appear in \\(\\mathcal N\\).  \nConsequently \\(|\\mathcal N|\\ge n\\).  \n\nBut \\(|\\mathcal N|=n-k\\); hence \\(n-k\\ge n\\), which forces \\(k\\le 0\\).  \nBecause \\(k\\) is a non‑negative integer, we obtain \\(k=0\\).\n\nThus an