            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nD_c=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=c\\},\\;2\\le c\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe sets \\(D_c\\) are disjoint and \\(|D_c|=c-1\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \n*A sunny line (slope \\(m\\neq0,\\;m\\neq\\infty,\\;m\\neq-1\\)) meets each diagonal \\(D_c\\) in at most one point.*\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the line as \\(y=mx+b\\). Suppose two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in D_c\\) lie on it.  \nBecause they belong to the same diagonal, \\(a_1+b_1=c\\) and \\(a_2+b_2=c\\), hence \\(b_2-b_1=-(a_2-a_1)\\).  \nSubstituting into the line equation gives  \n\n\\[\nb_1=ma_1+b,\\qquad b_2=ma_2+b .\n\\]\n\nSubtracting yields \\(b_2-b_1=m(a_2-a_1)\\).  \nCombining with \\(b_2-b_1=-(a_2-a_1)\\) gives \\((m+1)(a_2-a_1)=0\\).  \nSince the points are distinct, \\(a_2\\neq a_1\\); therefore \\(m=-1\\), contradicting the definition of a sunny line. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \n*If a diagonal \\(D_c\\) is covered without using a line parallel to \\(x+y=c\\), then at least \\(|D_c|=c-1\\) distinct lines are required.*\n\n*Proof.*  \nAny line not parallel to the direction \\((1,1)\\) intersects the diagonal \\(D_c\\) in at most one point (the same argument as in Lemma 1, without the restriction on the slope). Hence each such line can contribute at most one point of \\(D_c\\). To cover all \\(|D_c|\\) points we need at least \\(|D_c|\\) different lines. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Main Argument\n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nLet \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) and let \\(n-k\\) be the number of non‑sunny lines.\n\n1. **Capacity of a single line.**  \n   By Lemma 1 a sunny line meets each of the \\(n\\) diagonals in at most one point, so it can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   A non‑sunny line that is horizontal or vertical also meets each diagonal in at most one point, thus its capacity is at most \\(n\\).  \n   A diagonal non‑sunny line \\(x+y=c\\) covers exactly the points of \\(D_c\\), i.e. at most \\(n\\) points because \\(c\\le n+1\\).  \n   Hence **every line contributes at most \\(n\\) new points**.\n\n2. **Total number of points that can be covered by \\(n\\) lines.**  \n   The maximal possible number of points covered by \\(n\\) lines is therefore \\(n\\cdot n=n^{2}\\).\n\n3. **Comparison with the required number of points.**  \n   From (1) we need to cover \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points. For \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n   \\[\n   n^{2}<\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\quad\\text{is false};\\qquad\n   n^{2}\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Equality holds only when each line attains its maximal capacity \\(n\\).\n\n4. **When does a line attain capacity \\(n\\)?**  \n   A line attains capacity \\(n\\) precisely when it meets **every** diagonal.  \n   * A horizontal line \\(y=b\\) misses the diagonal \\(D_{b+1}\\) (because that diagonal consists of points with second coordinate \\(b\\) and first coordinate \\(1\\), which does not satisfy \\(y=b\\)).  \n   * A vertical line \\(x=a\\) misses the diagonal \\(D_{a+1}\\).  \n   * A sunny line, while it can intersect each diagonal, does so at a *different* point on each diagonal; this is allowed, so a sunny line can also meet all \\(n\\) diagonals and thus reach capacity \\(n\\).  \n   * A diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) meets exactly one diagonal (namely \\(D_c\\)) and therefore its capacity is \\(|D_c|=c-1\\le n-1\\) unless \\(c=n+1\\), in which case \\(|D_{n+1}|=n\\). Thus only the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) can reach capacity \\(n\\); all other diagonal lines fall short.\n\n   Consequently, to achieve the total of \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points with only \\(n\\) lines, **every diagonal \\(D_c\\) must be covered by a line that is parallel to it**; otherwise Lemma 2 forces us to use at least \\(|D_c|\\) distinct non‑parallel lines, exceeding the budget of \\(n\\) lines.\n\n5. **Forced set of lines.**  \n   For each \\(c\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) we must have a line parallel to \\(x+y=c\\). Since the lines must be distinct, the only possible choice is the collection  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n   \\]\n\n   All these lines are parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore none of them is sunny, i.e. \\(k=0\\).\n\n6. **Verification of existence.**  \n   The family \\(\\{\\ell_c\\mid c=2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.  \n   For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\) in the above range, so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_c\\). Hence the covering condition is satisfied.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nThe covering requirement forces every line to be parallel to the diagonal direction, and the only way to obtain exactly \\(n\\) distinct such lines is to take the \\(n\\) lines \\(x+y=2, x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\). All of them are non‑sunny, so the number of sunny lines must be  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
