ines each meet every diagonal, contributing exactly one point on each diagonal.  \nIf any such line were present, the total number of incidences with the diagonals would be at most  \n\n\\[\na\\cdot n + b\\cdot n = n^{2},\n\\]\n\nbut we must realise the exact distribution of points on each diagonal: diagonal \\(D_c\\) contains \\(c-1\\) points, and the only way to obtain the required numbers \\(c-1\\) for **all** \\(c\\) with only \\(n\\) lines is to have, for each \\(c\\), a line that is parallel to that diagonal, because a line that is not parallel contributes at most one point to \\(D_c\\).  \nIf a diagonal \\(D_c\\) were covered only by non‑parallel lines, we would need at least \\(c-1\\) distinct such lines, and summing over all \\(c\\) would exceed the available \\(n\\) lines. Hence for every \\(c\\) we must have a line parallel to \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nSince the lines must be distinct, the only possible collection is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore none of them is sunny. Hence the number of sunny lines in any admissible configuration is\n\n\\[\nk=0 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence\n\nThe family \\(\\{\\ell_c\\mid c=2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\) in \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_c\\).  \nThus the required covering exists with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion** – For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the only possible number of sunny lines in a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]"
