\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr).\n   \\]\n   For any \\(s\\ge1\\) this bound is strictly smaller than the required \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points, because \\(n-\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor-1\\ge1\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n   Consequently a covering with \\(n\\) lines cannot contain a sunny line; i.e. \\(s=0\\).\n\n4. **Show that with \\(s=0\\) the only possible choice is the family of diagonal lines**  \n   If a diagonal \\(D_c\\) were covered only by horizontal/vertical lines, each such line could contribute at most one point to \\(D_c\\); therefore we would need at least \\(|D_c|=c-1\\) distinct horizontal/vertical lines for that diagonal. Summing over all \\(c\\) would require more than \\(n\\) lines, contradicting the hypothesis. Hence for every \\(c\\) we must have the line \\(x+y=c\\) in the family.  \n   The \\(n\\) lines \\(\\ell_c:\\;x+y=c\\;(c=2,\\dots ,n+1)\\) are distinct, none of them is sunny, and they cover every point of \\(T_n\\).  \n\nThus the only admissible value of \\(k\\) (the number of sunny lines) is \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set \\(T_n\\) and its diagonal decomposition  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) set  \n\n\\[\nD_c=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=c\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe sets \\(D_c\\) are pairwise disjoint, and a simple count gives  \n\n\\[\n|D_c|=c-1\\qquad(2\\le c\\le n+1).\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}|D_c|\n      =\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)\n      =\\frac{n(n+1)}2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Maximal number of points of \\(T_n\\) that a line can contain  \n\nWe distinguish three families of lines.\n\n#### 2.1  Diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\)\n\nSuch a line meets exactly the diagonal \\(D_c\\); consequently it contains precisely \\(|D_c|=c-1\\le n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n#### 2.2  Horizontal and vertical lines  \n\nConsider a horizontal line \\(y=b\\) with integer \\(b\\ge1\\).  \nA point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(T_n\\) iff \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), i.e.  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le n+1-b .\n\\]\n\nThus the line \\(y=b\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\}= \\max\\{0,\\,n+1-b\\}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\). In particular the number is **at most** \\(n\\), and the value \\(n\\) is attained only for \\(b=1\\).  \nThe same analysis holds for a vertical line \\(x=a\\).\n\nHence for any horizontal or vertical line we have the universal bound  \n\n\\[\n\\text{(horizontal/vertical)}\\le n. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.3  Sunny lines  \n\nA sunny line is one that is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nWrite its equation as  \n\n\\[\ny=mx+b,\\qquad m\\notin\\{0,-1\\},\n\\]\n\nand express the slope in lowest terms \\(m=p/q\\) with \\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1\\).\n\nFor a diagonal \\(D_c\\) the intersection point (if it is a lattice point) must satisfy  \n\n\\[\na+b=c,\\qquad b=ma+b_0,\n\\]\n\nwhich yields  \n\n\\[\na=\\frac{c-b_0}{1+m}\n   =\\frac{q(c-b_0)}{q+p}.\n\\]\n\nThus a lattice point of \\(D_c\\) lies on the line **iff**  \n\n\\[\nq+p\\mid q(c-b_0).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\gcd(q,q+p)=\\gcd(q,p)=1\\), this divisibility is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\nq+p\\mid (c-b_0). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(c\\) runs through the integers \\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\), the numbers \\(c-b_0\\) form a set of \\(n\\) consecutive integers.  \nAmong any \\(n\\) consecutive integers at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{q+p}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n\\]\n\nare divisible by the fixed integer \\(q+p\\ge2\\). Consequently a sunny line can meet at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\). (The bound is attained for slopes \\(\\pm1\\) when \\(n\\) is even, but those slopes are excluded; nevertheless (4) is a valid universal upper bound.)\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Incidence counting  \n\nAssume we have a collection \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nLet  \n\n* \\(a\\) = number of diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\) in \\(\\mathcal L\\);\n* \\(h\\) = number of horizontal lines;\n* \\(v\\) = number of vertical lines;\n* \\(s\\) = number of sunny lines.\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\na+h+v+s=n. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nUsing the maximal capacities derived above, the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by \\(\\mathcal L\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\na\\cdot n \\;+\\; (h+v)\\cdot n \\;+\\; s\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr)\n= n\\bigl(n-s\\bigr)+s\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(n\\ge3\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nn-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-1\\ge1,\n\\]\n\nhence for any \\(s\\ge1\\)\n\n\\[\nn\\bigl(n-s\\bigr)+s\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr)\n= n^{2}-s\\Bigl(n-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-1\\Bigr)\n< n^{2}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) the required number of points is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 = n^{2}-\\frac{n(n-1)}2.\n\\]\n\nFor every \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\nn^{2}-\\frac{n(n-1)}2 > n^{2}-\\bigl(n-\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor-1\\bigr),\n\\]\n\nso the bound (7) is **strictly smaller** than the needed \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) whenever \\(s\\ge1\\).  \nTherefore a covering with exactly \\(n\\) lines cannot contain any sunny line:\n\n\\[\ns=0. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Necessity of the diagonal family  \n\nWith \\(s=0\\) equation (5) reduces to \\(a+h+v=n\\).  \nSuppose that for some diagonal \\(D_c\\) we do **not** have the line \\(x+y=c\\) in the family.  \nThen the points of \\(D_c\\) must be covered by horizontal and/or vertical lines.  \nEach horizontal (or vertical) line can contribute **at most one** point to a given diagonal, because on a fixed diagonal the equation \\(a+b=c\\) determines a unique \\(a\\) (resp. \\(b\\)).  \nConsequently we would need at least \\(|D_c|=c-1\\) distinct horizontal/vertical lines to cover \\(D_c\\).\n\nSumming this requirement over all diagonals gives a lower bound on the total number of horizontal/vertical lines:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>n,\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts the fact that we have only \\(n\\) lines in total.  \nHence for **every** \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) the line \\(x+y=c\\) must belong to the family.  \n\nBecause the lines must be distinct, the only possible configuration is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_c : x+y=c\\mid c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore none of them is sunny, in agreement with (8).  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\) in the displayed range, so it lies on the corresponding \\(\\ell_c\\).  \nThus the configuration exists and uses exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the only non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]"

