al and vertical lines must satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   h+v\\ge|D_c|=c-1.\n   \\]\n\n   Since the largest possible value of \\(c-1\\) is \\(n\\) (attained for \\(c=n+1\\)), we obtain \\(h+v\\ge n\\). Together with \\(a+h+v=n\\) this forces \\(a=0\\) and \\(h+v=n\\). But then the diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) (which contains \\(n\\) points) would have to be covered by the \\(n\\) horizontal/vertical lines, each contributing at most one point, leaving no room for the other diagonals – a contradiction. Hence **every** diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) must belong to the family, i.e. \\(a=n\\).\n\n   Because the lines are required to be distinct, the only possible configuration is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_c:\\;x+y=c\\mid c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   None of these lines is sunny, in agreement with (8), and they clearly cover every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion**  \n\n   For any integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the existence condition can be satisfied only with the \\(n\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\). Consequently the number of sunny lines in any admissible configuration is forced to be  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k=0}.\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set \\(T_n\\) and its diagonal decomposition  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) set  \n\n\\[\nD_c=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=c\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe sets \\(D_c\\) are pairwise disjoint, and a direct count gives  \n\n\\[\n|D_c|=c-1\\qquad(2\\le c\\le n+1).\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}|D_c|\n      =\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)\n      =\\frac{n(n+1)}2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Maximal number of points of \\(T_n\\) that a line can contain  \n\nWe distinguish three families of lines.\n\n#### 2.1  Diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\)\n\nSuch a line meets exactly the diagonal \\(D_c\\); therefore it contains precisely \\(|D_c|=c-1\\le n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n#### 2.2  Horizontal and vertical lines  \n\nA horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (\\(b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\)) contains the points \\((a,b)\\) with  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le n+1-b .\n\\]\n\nThus it contains \\(\\max\\{0,n+1-b\\}\\le n\\) points of \\(T_n\\); the value \\(n\\) occurs only for \\(b=1\\).  \nThe same bound holds for a vertical line \\(x=a\\).\n\n#### 2.3  Sunny lines  \n\nA sunny line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nWrite its equation in the form  \n\n\\[\ny=mx+b_0,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(m\\neq0,-1\\). Two cases have to be considered.\n\n*Case 1 – integer slope.*  \nLet \\(m\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\\). For a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) on the line we have  \n\n\\[\nb=ma+b_0,\\qquad a+b\\le n+1.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\na+ma\\le n+1-b_0\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;a(1+m)\\le n+1-b_0.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(|1+m|\\ge2\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\na\\le\\frac{n+1}{|1+m|}\\le\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\) can lie on such a line.\n\n*Case 2 – non‑integer rational slope.*  \nWrite \\(m=p/q\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and \\(q\\ge2\\).  \nThe condition that both coordinates be integers forces  \n\n\\[\npa\\equiv c\\pmod q\n\\]\n\nfor a fixed residue \\(c\\) (determined by \\(b_0\\)). Consequently the admissible abscissae form an arithmetic progression with step \\(q\\). Within the interval \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) there are at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{q}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) such values, and because \\(q\\ge2\\),\n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{q}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nHence a sunny line with non‑integer slope also contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nCombining the two cases we obtain the universal bound  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{any sunny line contains at most } \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\text{ points of }T_n.}\\tag{L}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Incidence counting  \n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nLet  \n\n* \\(a\\) = number of diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\) in \\(\\mathcal L\\);\n* \\(h\\) = number of horizontal lines;\n* \\(v\\) = number of vertical lines;\n* \\(s\\) = number of sunny lines.\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\na+h+v+s=n. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nUsing the maximal capacities derived in Section 2, the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\na\\cdot n\\;+\\;(h+v)\\cdot n\\;+\\;s\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n= n\\bigl(n-s\\bigr)+s\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) (see (1)), we must have  \n\n\\[\nn\\bigl(n-s\\bigr)+s\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge3\\) we have \\(n-\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\ge1\\); therefore the left‑hand side of (7) is a strictly decreasing function of \\(s\\). Substituting \\(s=1\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nn(n-1)+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n<\\frac{n(n+1)}2,\n\\]\n\nand the inequality fails for every larger \\(s\\). Consequently (7) can hold only when  \n\n\\[\ns=0. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus **no sunny line can appear** in a covering consisting of exactly \\(n\\) lines.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Necessity of all diagonal lines  \n\nWith \\(s=0\\) equation (5) reduces to \\(a+h+v=n\\).  \nSuppose that for some \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) the diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) is missing, i.e. \\(a<n\\).  \nAll points of the diagonal \\(D_c\\) must then be covered by horizontal and/or vertical lines.  \n\nOn a fixed diagonal each horizontal (resp. vertical) line can contribute **at most one** point, because the relation \\(a+b=c\\) determines a unique abscissa (resp. ordinate). Hence we need  \n\n\\[\nh+v\\ge|D_c|=c-1. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSince the largest possible value of \\(c-1\\) is \\(n\\) (attained for \\(c=n+1\\)), (9) forces \\(h+v\\ge n\\). Together with \\(a+h+v=n\\) this yields \\(a=0\\) and \\(h+v=n\\). But then the diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\), which contains \\(n\\) points, would have to be covered by the \\(n\\) horizontal/vertical lines, each contributing at most one point; consequently no line would be left to cover any point of the other diagonals, contradicting the requirement that **all** points of \\(T_n\\) be covered.  \n\nTherefore the assumption that a diagonal is missing is impossible; we must have  \n\n\\[\na=n.\n\\]\n\nBecause the lines are required to be distinct, the only possible configuration is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_c:\\;x+y=c\\mid c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_c\\) is parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\) and hence is **not** sunny, in agreement with (8). Moreover every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\) in the displayed range, so it lies on the corresponding \\(\\ell_c\\). Thus the configuration indeed covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the covering condition can be satisfied only by the \\(n\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\;(c=2,\\dots ,n+1)\\). Consequently the number of sunny lines in any admissible family is forced to be  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]"
