our families of lines.\n\n   *If the diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) is present* (i.e. counted in \\(a\\)), it already supplies all \\(|D_c|\\) points, and the other families may contribute **zero** additional points on that diagonal.\n\n   *If the diagonal line is absent*, the only possible contributors are horizontal, vertical and sunny lines, each of which can give **at most one** point on that diagonal. Hence, when the diagonal is missing we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   h+v+s\\;\\ge\\;|D_c|=c-1. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Deriving a contradiction when any diagonal is missing.**  \n\n   Suppose that for some \\(c_0\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) the diagonal line \\(x+y=c_0\\) is not among the \\(a\\) diagonal lines; i.e. \\(a<n\\).  \n   Apply (3) with \\(c=c_0\\):\n\n   \\[\n   h+v+s\\ge c_0-1\\ge 1. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Because the right‑hand side grows with \\(c\\), the *largest* requirement comes from the diagonal with the most points, namely \\(c=n+1\\) where \\(|D_{n+1}|=n\\). Thus (3) for \\(c=n+1\\) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   h+v+s\\ge n. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (5) with (2) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   a = n-(h+v+s)\\le n-n =0,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. **no diagonal line is present at all**. But then the diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) (which contains \\(n\\) points) would have to be covered solely by the \\(h+v+s=n\\) non‑diagonal lines, each contributing at most one point on that diagonal. Consequently every point of \\(D_{n+1}\\) would be used up, leaving **no line left** to supply any point of the other diagonals \\(D_c\\) (\\(c\\le n\\)), contradicting the requirement that *all* points of \\(T_n\\) be covered.\n\n   Hence our assumption that a diagonal is missing is impossible; we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   a=n. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Consequences for the number of sunny lines.**  \n\n   From (6) and (2) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   h=v=s=0.\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore **every admissible covering consists exactly of the \\(n\\) diagonal lines**  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{\\,x+y=c\\mid c=2,3,\