n none).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Counting incidences over all diagonals  \n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nDenote by  \n\n* \\(a\\) – the number of diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\) that belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\);\n* \\(h\\) – the number of horizontal lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\);\n* \\(v\\) – the number of vertical lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\);\n* \\(s\\) – the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nClearly  \n\n\\[\na+h+v+s=n. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFix a diagonal \\(D_c\\).  \n\n*If the line \\(x+y=c\\) is present* (i.e. counted in \\(a\\)), then all \\(|D_c|=c-1\\) points of that diagonal are already covered, and the other families of lines may contribute **zero** additional points on that diagonal.\n\n*If the line \\(x+y=c\\) is absent*, the only possible contributors are horizontal, vertical and sunny lines, each of which can supply **at most one** point on \\(D_c\\) (by the observation of §2). Consequently, when the diagonal is missing we must have  \n\n\\[\nh+v+s\\;\\ge\\;|D_c|=c-1. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4.  No diagonal can be missing  \n\nAssume, for contradiction, that some diagonal is missing; i.e. \\(a<n\\).  \nLet \\(c_{\\max}=n+1\\); the corresponding diagonal \\(D_{c_{\\max}}\\) contains \\(|D_{c_{\\max}}|=n\\) points, the largest possible size among all diagonals.  \nApplying (3) with \\(c=c_{\\max}\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nh+v+s\\ge n. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nTogether with (2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na=n-(h+v+s)\\le n-n=0,\n\\]\n\nso \\(a=0\\); that is, **no diagonal line is present at all**.  \nBut then the diagonal \\(D_{c_{\\max}}\\) (which has \\(n\\) points) must be covered solely by the \\(h+v+s=n\\) non‑diagonal lines, each contributing at most one point on that diagonal. Hence every point of \\(D_{c_{\\max}}\\) is used up, leaving **no line left** to supply any point of the remaining diagonals \\(D_c\\) with \\(c\\le n\\). This contradicts the requirement that *all* points of \\(T_n\\) be covered.\n\nTh