t one point on that diagonal. Hence every point of \\(D_{c_{\\max}}\\) is used up, leaving **no line left** to supply any point of the remaining diagonals \\(D_c\\) with \\(c\\le n\\). This contradicts the requirement that *all* points of \\(T_n\\) be covered.\n\nTherefore the assumption is false; every diagonal must be present:\n\n\\[\na=n. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Determination of \\(k\\)\n\nFrom (5) and (2) we deduce  \n\n\\[\nh=v=s=0.\n\\]\n\nConsequently the only possible family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{\\,x+y=c\\mid c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains **no sunny line**. Hence the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines is forced to be\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\nThe displayed family indeed covers every point of \\(T_n\\) (each \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\) in the given range), so the value \\(k=0\\) is attainable and is the only admissible one.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
