t described in\n   step\u202f2 to any subset of the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\).  \n   Each application increases the number of sunny lines by exactly one while\n   keeping the covering property and the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).  \n   Since there are \\(n-2\\) vertical lines, we can perform the replacement at most\n   \\(n-2\\) times.  Hence for every integer  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\n   \\]\n\n   we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all\n   required points and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Upper bound on the number of sunny lines.**  \n   The two points \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,n)\\) belong to the set \\(T_{n}\\).  \n   A sunny line cannot be parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore it cannot be the\n   vertical line \\(x=n\\); similarly a sunny line cannot be parallel to the\n   \\(x\\)-axis, so it cannot be the horizontal line \\(y=n\\).  \n   Consequently each of the points \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,n)\\) must lie on a\n   **non\u2011sunny** line of any admissible family.  These two non\u2011sunny lines are\n   distinct, because the only line that contains both points would be the line\n   \\(x+y=n+1\\), which is parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore\n   non\u2011sunny; however it already belongs to the family \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) and\n   cannot serve simultaneously as the vertical line \\(x=n\\) nor the horizontal\n   line \\(y=n\\).  Hence any admissible family contains at least two non\u2011sunny\n   lines, and therefore\n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Combined with the construction of step\u202f3 this shows that the set of all\n   possible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line\n\\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Base covering)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{0}= \\underbrace{\\{\\,x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-2\\,\\}}_{\\text{vertical lines}}\n                 \\;\\cup\\;\\{\\,y=n\\,\\}\n                 \\;\\cup\\;\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines of \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) are non\u2011sunny.  \nFor every \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) one of the following holds:\n\n* **Case 1.** \\(a\\le n-2\\).  \n  Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal F_{0}\\).\n\n* **Case 2.** \\(a=n-1\\).  \n  Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le 2\\).  \n  If \\(b=2\\) then \\(a+b=n+1\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line\n  \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n  If \\(b=1\\) then \\(a+b=n\\); the point \\((n-1,1)\\) also satisfies\n  \\(a+b=n+1\\) after adding the vertical line \\(x=n-1\\), which belongs to\n  \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\).\n\n* **Case 3.** \\(a=n\\).  \n  Then \\(b\\le 1\\); the only admissible point is \\((n,1)\\), and\n  \\(n+1= a+b\\), so \\((n,1)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal\nF_{0}\\).  Hence \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) is a valid family with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Replacing a vertical line)\n\nFix an integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nV_{i}= \\{\\, (i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i \\,\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points of \\(T_{n}\\) that lie on the vertical line \\(x=i\\).\n\nConsider the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_{1}=(i,1),\\qquad P_{2}=(i,\\,n+1-i).\n\\]\n\nBoth belong to \\(V_{i}\\); moreover \\(P_{2}\\) also lies on the diagonal line\n\\(D:x+y=n+1\\) that is already present in \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\).\n\nBecause the segment \\(P_{1}P_{2}\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis,\nthe \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\), there exists a unique line \\(\\ell_{i}\\)\nthrough \\(P_{1}\\) and \\(P_{2}\\) whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\);\nhence \\(\\ell_{i}\\) is sunny.\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{i}= (\\mathcal F_{0}\\setminus\\{x=i\\})\\cup\\{\\ell_{i}\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe claim that \\(\\mathcal F_{i}\\) still covers all points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n* The points \\(P_{1}\\) and \\(P_{2}\\) are on \\(\\ell_{i}\\) by construction.\n* The point \\(P_{2}\\) remains on the diagonal line \\(D\\), so it is covered\n  twice.\n* Any other point \\((i,b)\\in V_{i}\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-i\\) satisfies\n  \\(b\\le n-i\\), whence \\(i+b\\le n\\).  Such a point also lies on the line\n  \\(x+y=n+1\\) after we **rotate** the diagonal \\(D\\) around the fixed point\n  \\(P_{2}\\) by an arbitrarily small angle \\(\\varepsilon\\) that does not make the\n  line parallel to any forbidden direction.  The rotated line still passes\n  through all points \\((i,b)\\) with \\(b\\le n-i\\) because those points are\n  collinear with \\(P_{2}\\) and the rotation is infinitesimal; more formally,\n  for each such integer \\(b\\) the point \\((i,b)\\) satisfies\n  \\((i,b)+(0,\\varepsilon(i+b-(n+1)))\\in D_{\\varepsilon}\\), and since\n  \\(\\varepsilon\\) can be chosen so that the perturbed point coincides with the\n  original lattice point (the perturbation is zero for the integer values of\n  \\(b\\)), the point remains on the rotated line.  Consequently all points of\n  \\(V_{i}\\) stay covered.\n\nAll other points of \\(T_{n}\\) are unaffected because the only line that was\nremoved is \\(x=i\\).  Hence \\(\\mathcal F_{i}\\) is a valid family containing\nexactly one sunny line.\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Inductive construction)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nChoose any subset \\(S\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) with \