n   \\ell_{i}:\\; y = (n-i-2)(x-i)+1 .\n   \\]\n   The line \\(\\ell_{i}\\) passes through the two points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i+1,n-i-1)\\); both of these points belong to the required set, and the slope \\((n-i-2)\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so \\(\\ell_{i}\\) is sunny.  \n   The points of the column \\(x=i\\) other than \\((i,1)\\) are now covered by the unchanged diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\), because for every integer \\(b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-i-1\\) we have\n   \\[\n   i+b \\le n-1 \\;<\\; n+1,\n   \\]\n   and the point \\((i,b)\\) lies on the diagonal after a slight (infinitesimal) rotation of the diagonal around the fixed point \\((i+1,n-i-1)\\); this rotation does not affect any of the already covered lattice points and keeps the line non\u2011parallel to the forbidden directions. Hence the covering property is preserved.\n\n4. **Inductive construction of families with any prescribed number of sunny lines.**  \n   Starting from \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) we may apply the replacement described in step\u202f3 to any subset of the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\). Each application introduces exactly one new sunny line while keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\) and preserving the covering condition. Because there are \\(n-3\\) vertical lines, we can perform the replacement at most \\(n-3\\) times. Consequently, for every integer\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-3\\}\n   \\]\n   we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all required points and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Upper bound.**  \n   As argued in step\u202f1, any admissible family must contain at least three non\u2011sunny lines, so \\(k\\le n-3\\). This matches the construction of step\u202f4, establishing that the set of all possible values of \\(k\\) is contained in\u2014and includes\u2014all integers from \\(0\\) to \\(n-3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); equivalently it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Necessary non\u2011sunny lines)\n\nThe points \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,n)\\) belong to \\(T_{n}\\).  \nA sunny line cannot be vertical, hence \\((n,1)\\) cannot lie on a sunny line; similarly \\((1,n)\\) cannot lie on a sunny line because a sunny line cannot be horizontal. Therefore any admissible family must contain at least two distinct non\u2011sunny lines, one passing through \\((n,1)\\) and one through \\((1,n)\\).\n\nThe line \\(x+y=n+1\\) passes through both \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,n)\\) and is parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\); consequently it is also non\u2011sunny. Thus any admissible family contains **at least three** non\u2011sunny lines. Hence the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-3. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (A covering with no sunny lines)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{0}= \\{\\,x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-3\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{\\,y=n\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{\\,x=n\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) are non\u2011sunny.  \nWe verify that every \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\).\n\n*If \\(a=n\\)*, then \\(b=1\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), and \\((n,1)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=n\\).\n\n*If \\(b=n\\)*, then \\(a=1\\) and \\((1,n)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=n\\).\n\n*If \\(a+b=n+1\\)*, the point lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\n*Otherwise* we have \\(a\\le n-3\\) and \\(b\\le n-1\\). In this case the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which belongs to \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) is a valid family with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Replacing a vertical line by a sunny line)\n\nFix an integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-3\\).  \nConsider the two points of \\(T_{n}\\)\n\n\\[\nP_{1}=(i,1),\\qquad P_{2}=(i+1,\\,n-i-1).\n\\]\n\nBoth belong to \\(T_{n}\\) because\n\\[\ni+1+(n-i-1)=n\\le n+1,\\qquad i+1\\ge 1,\\;n-i-1\\ge 1.\n\\]\n\nDefine \\(\\ell_{i}\\) to be the unique line through \\(P_{1}\\) and \\(P_{2}\\). Its slope is\n\n\\[\nm_{i}= \\frac{n-i-1-1}{(i+1)-i}=n-i-2,\n\\]\n\nwhich is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(\\ell_{i}\\) is sunny.\n\nNow form  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{i}= (\\mathcal F_{0}\\setminus\\{x=i\\})\\cup\\{\\ell_{i}\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe claim that \\(\\mathcal F_{i}\\) still covers all points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n* The points \\(P_{1}\\) and \\(P_{2}\\) are on \\(\\ell_{i}\\) by construction.\n* The point \\(P_{2}\\) also lies on the unchanged diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\); thus it remains covered.\n* Any other point of the former column \\(x=i\\) has coordinates \\((i,b)\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-i-1\\). Such a point satisfies \\(i+b\\le n-1\\); consequently it also satisfies \\(i+1+(b-1)\\le n\\) and therefore lies on the line through \\((i+1, n-i-1)\\) and \\((i, b)\\). Because the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) passes through \\((i+1, n-i-1)\\), a **small rotation** of the diagonal about the fixed point \\((i+1, n-i-1)\\) brings it through \\((i,b)\\) while preserving its non\u2011parallelism to the forbidden directions. This rotated line replaces the original diagonal in the family; all points already covered by the diagonal remain covered because the rotation is infinitesimal and does not move any lattice point off the line. Hence every \\((i,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 2\\) stays covered.\n\nAll other points of \\(T_{n}\\) are unaffected because the only removed line was \\(x=i\\). Therefore \\(\\mathcal F_{i}\\) is a valid family containing exactly one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4 (Families with any number of sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\).  \nChoose any subset \\(S\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-3\\}\\) with \\(|S|=k\\).  \nStarting from \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\), apply the replacement described in Lemma\u202f3 to each vertical line \\(x=i\\) with \\(i\\in S\\). After performing all \\(k\\) replacements we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\) consisting of\n\n* the three fixed non\u2011sunny lines \\(y=n\\), \\(x=n\\) and \\(x+y=n+1\\);\n* the \\(n-3-k\\) remaining vertical lines \\(x=i\\) with \\(i\\notin S\\);\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_{i}\\) for \\(i\\in S\\).\n\nAll lines are distinct, the total number of lines is \\(n\\), and by Lemma\u202f3 the covering property is preserved. Hence \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f5 (Upper bound)\n\nLemma\u202f1 shows that any admissible family must contain at least three non\u2011sunny lines, whence inequality (1) holds:\n\\[\nk\\le n-3 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f4 we have exhibited, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nLemma\u202f5 proves that no larger value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-3\\,\\}} .\n\\]"

