--------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (A sunny line that can replace a vertical line)\n\nFix an integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\).  \nConsider the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_{i}=(i,1),\\qquad Q_{i}=(i+1,n-i-1).\n\\]\n\nBoth belong to \\(T_{n}\\) because  \n\n\\[\ni+1+(n-i-1)=n\\le n+1,\\qquad i\\ge1,\\;n-i-1\\ge1 .\n\\]\n\nLet \\(\\ell_{i}\\) be the unique line through \\(P_{i}\\) and \\(Q_{i}\\).  Its equation is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{i}:\\; y=(n-i-2)(x-i)+1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_{i}\\) equals \\(n-i-2\\); for \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\) this number is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  Hence \\(\\ell_{i}\\) is sunny.\n\nNow replace the vertical line \\(x=i\\) in \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) by \\(\\ell_{i}\\) and **keep** the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  We claim that the resulting family still covers all points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n* The points \\(P_{i}\\) and \\(Q_{i}\\) are on \\(\\ell_{i}\\) by construction.\n* Every point \\((i,b)\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-i-1\\) satisfies \\(i+b\\le n-1\\); consequently it also satisfies  \n\n  \\[\n  (i+1)+(b-1)=i+b\\le n-1<n+1,\n  \\]\n\n  and therefore the point \\((i,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  Hence all points that were previously covered by the removed vertical line remain covered by the unchanged diagonal line.\n* All other points of \\(T_{n}\\) are unaffected because the only removed line was \\(x=i\\).\n\nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{i}= (\\mathcal F_{0}\\setminus\\{x=i\\})\\cup\\{\\ell_{i}\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(T_{n}\\) and contains exactly one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Families with any prescribed number of sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nChoose a subset  \n\n\\[\nS\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\},\\qquad |S|=k .\n\\]\n\nStarting from \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) we replace, for each \\(i\\in S\\), the vertical line \\(x=i\\) by the sunny line \\(\\ell_{i}\\) defined in Lemma 2.  After performing all replacements we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{k}= \\bigl\\{\\ell_{i}\\mid i\\in S\\bigr\\}\n            \\;\\cup\\;\n            \\bigl\\{x=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-2,\\;j\\notin S\\bigr\\}\n            \\;\\cup\\;\n            \\{y=n,\\;x+y=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n*The family \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\) has exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines* (the \\(\\ell_{i}\\)’s) and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (the remaining vertical lines together with the horizontal and diagonal lines).  All lines are distinct because each \\(\\ell_{i}\\) contains the unique point \\((i,1)\\) that does not belong to any other line of the family.\n\nTo see that \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\) still covers \\(T_{n}\\), consider an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\in S\\), then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_{a}\\) (Lemma 2 shows that \\(\\ell_{a}\\) contains the whole column \\(x=a\\) up to the diagonal).\n* If \\(a\\notin S\\) and \\(a\\le n-2\\), then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is present in \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n* If \\(a=n-1\\) or \\(a=n\\), then \\(b\\le 2\\) and the point belongs to the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (for \\(a=n\\) we have \\(b=1\\); for \\(a=n-1\\) we have \\(b\\le2\\) and both \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\) satisfy \\(x+y=n\\) or \\(n+1\\) and are therefore on the diagonal or on the horizontal line \\(y=n\\)).  \n* If \\(b=n\\) the point is on the horizontal line \\(y=n\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) is covered, and \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\) satisfies all requirements.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Upper bound)\n\nSince we are asked for \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines; hence \\(k\\le n\\).  Lemma 3 shows that this bound is attainable for every integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Result\n\nCombining Lemma 3 (existence) with Lemma 4 (trivial upper bound) we obtain that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
