iagonals.\n\nThe two parts together give the exact set of admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Notation and the set to be covered  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem requires a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is\n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its direction is different from the three forbidden directions:\n\\[\n\\mathbf d_x=(1,0),\\qquad \\mathbf d_y=(0,1),\\qquad \\mathbf d_d=(1,-1).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. An upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\n#### Lemma 2.1  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in T_n\\).  \nThe vectors \\((a_2-a_1,b_2-b_1)\\) and \\((a_3-a_1,b_3-b_1)\\) are both parallel to the direction of \\(\\ell\\).  \nConsequently the two vectors are linearly dependent, i.e. one is a scalar multiple of the other.  \nSince all coordinates are positive integers and \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\), the only possible scalar multiples are \\(1\\) or \\(-1\\).  \nIf the scalar is \\(1\\) the three points coincide, contradicting distinctness; if it is \\(-1\\) the line would be parallel to \\((1,-1)\\), i.e. to the forbidden direction \\(x+y=0\\).  \nThus a sunny line cannot contain three distinct points of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\nHence, if \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains \\(k\\) sunny lines, those lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.2  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \nThen \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nDenote by \\(N\\) the set of points of \\(T_n\\) that are covered by the sunny lines.  \nBy Lemma 2.1, \\(|N|\\le 2k\\).  \nConsequently at least  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|-2k=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-2k \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\npoints must be covered by the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\nA non‑sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.\n\n* If a line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, say \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\), the points of \\(T_n\\) on it are \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); thus it contains exactly \\(n+1-c\\) points.\n* If a line is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, say \\(x=c\\), it contains \\(n+1-c\\) points as well.\n* If a line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), i.e. of the form \\(x+y=d\\) with \\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\), it contains exactly \\(d-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nIn each of the three families the total number of points covered by **all** lines of that family equals \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).  \nTherefore, regardless of how the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines are chosen, the **maximum** number of points they can cover is attained when we take the \\(n-k\\) lines with the largest possible point‑counts.  \nThe two extreme lines of each family (the ones with \\(c=1\\) or \\(d=n+1\\)) contain \\(n\\) points, the next ones contain \\(n-1\\) points, and so on.\n\nConsequently the sum of the point‑counts of any \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\nn+(n-1)+\\dots+(n-m+1)=mn-\\frac{m(m-1)}{2}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nHere \\(m=n-k\\).  Using (2) we must have  \n\n\\[\nmn-\\frac{m(m-1)}{2}\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-2k .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(m=n-k\\) and simplifying yields  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus a covering with \\(n\\) lines cannot contain \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Construction achieving every \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 3.1. A base covering with no sunny lines  \n\nConsider the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_n=\\bigl\\{\\,\\ell_i : x+y=i+1\\ \\big|\\ i=1,2,\\dots ,n\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) is parallel to the forbidden direction \\(x+y=0\\); hence none of them is sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(a+b=i+1\\) for the unique integer \\(i=a+b-1\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\).  \nThus \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).  Hence \\(\\mathcal D_n\\) already satisfies the covering condition and contains \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 3.2. Turning two diagonal lines into one sunny line  \n\nPick two distinct points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on two different lines of \\(\\mathcal D_n\\) and such that the line through them is **not** parallel to any of the three forbidden directions.  \nFor example, choose  \n\n\\[\nP=(1,1)\\in \\ell_1,\\qquad Q=(2, n-1)\\in \\ell_{n-1}.\n\\]\n\nThe slope of the line \\(PQ\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n-2}{1}\\neq 0,\\ \\infty,\\ -1,\n\\]\n\nso \\(PQ\\) is sunny.  \n\nNow delete the two diagonal lines \\(\\ell_1\\) and \\(\\ell_{n-1}\\) and insert the sunny line \\(PQ\\).  \nAll points that were on \\(\\ell_1\\) except \\(P\\) are still covered by the remaining diagonal lines \\(\\ell_2,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\); similarly for \\(\\ell_{n-1}\\).  \nPoints \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) are now covered by the new sunny line.  \nThus we obtain a new family \\(\\mathcal L_1\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly **one** sunny line and still covering \\(T_n\\).\n\n#### 3.3. Repeating the operation  \n\nAssume we have a covering \\(\\mathcal L_{t}\\) with \\(t\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le t\\le n-3\\)).  \nAmong the remaining diagonal lines there are at least three, because we have removed only \\(t\\) of them and \\(t\\le n-3\\).  \nSelect two of those diagonal lines, say \\(\\ell_{i}\\) and \\(\\ell_{j}\\) with \\(i<j\\), and pick one point on each that has not been used before (possible because each diagonal contains at least one point not lying on any previously introduced sunny line).  \nForm the line through the two chosen points; it is sunny for the same reason as before.  \nDelete \\(\\ell_i,\\ell_j\\) and add this sunny line.  \nThe resulting family \\(\\mathcal L_{t+1}\\) has \\(t+1\\) sunny lines and still covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nBecause each step reduces the number of remaining diagonal lines by exactly one, we can repeat the procedure until only **two** diagonal lines are left.  \nAt that moment the number of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\nk_{\\max}=n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we can stop after \\(k\\) repetitions and obtain a covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Conclusion  \n\nLemma 2.2 shows that no configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \nSection 3 provides, for each \\(k=0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\), an explicit construction of a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\}} .\n\\]"
