w exhibit, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 3.1. A base covering with no sunny lines  \n\nConsider the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_n=\\bigl\\{\\,\\ell_i : x+y=i+1\\ \\big|\\ i=1,2,\\dots ,n\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) is parallel to the forbidden direction \\(x+y=0\\); hence none of them is sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(a+b=i+1\\) for the unique integer \\(i=a+b-1\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\).  \nThus \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).  Hence \\(\\mathcal D_n\\) already satisfies the covering condition and contains \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 3.2. Turning two diagonal lines into one sunny line  \n\nPick two distinct points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on two different lines of \\(\\mathcal D_n\\) and such that the line through them is **not** parallel to any of the three forbidden directions.  \nFor example, choose  \n\n\\[\nP=(1,1)\\in \\ell_1,\\qquad Q=(2, n-1)\\in \\ell_{n-1}.\n\\]\n\nThe slope of the line \\(PQ\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n-2}{1}\\neq 0,\\ \\infty,\\ -1,\n\\]\n\nso \\(PQ\\) is sunny.  \n\nNow delete the two diagonal lines \\(\\ell_1\\) and \\(\\ell_{n-1}\\) and insert the sunny line \\(PQ\\).  \nAll points that were on \\(\\ell_1\\) except \\(P\\) are still covered by the remaining diagonal lines \\(\\ell_2,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\); similarly for \\(\\ell_{n-1}\\).  \nPoints \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) are now covered by the new sunny line.  \nThus we obtain a new family \\(\\mathcal L_1\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly **one** sunny line and still covering \\(T_n\\).\n\n#### 3.3. Repeating the operation  \n\nAssume we have a covering \\(\\mathcal L_{t}\\) with \\(t\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le t\\le n-3\\)).  \nAmong the remaining diagonal lines there are at least three, because we have removed only \\(t\\) of them and \\(t\\le n-3\\).  \nSelect two of those di