 2.1  \nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) is **not** parallel to a diagonal \\(D_i\\), then \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(D_i\\) in at most one point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose \\(\\ell\\) intersected \\(D_i\\) in two distinct points \\(P,Q\\in T_n\\).  \nBoth \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) satisfy the equations of \\(\\ell\\) and of \\(D_i\\); hence the vector \\(\\overrightarrow{PQ}\\) is simultaneously parallel to the direction of \\(\\ell\\) and to the direction of \\(D_i\\) (which is \\((1,-1)\\)).  \nTherefore \\(\\ell\\) would be parallel to \\(D_i\\), contradicting the hypothesis. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.2  \nIn any covering \\(\\mathcal L\\) we must have \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the two longest diagonals \\(D_n\\) and \\(D_{n-1}\\).  \nBy (2) they contain \\(n\\) and \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\), respectively.\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that both \\(D_n\\) and \\(D_{n-1}\\) are absent from \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nThen every point of \\(D_n\\) must be covered by a line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) that is **not** parallel to \\(D_n\\); by Lemma 2.1 each such line can contain at most one point of \\(D_n\\).  \nHence at least \\(n\\) distinct lines would be needed to cover \\(D_n\\).  \nSimilarly, covering \\(D_{n-1}\\) would require at least \\(n-1\\) distinct lines.  \nTogether this would demand at least \\(n+(n-1)=2n-1\\) lines, far exceeding the allowed total of \\(n\\).  \n\nTherefore at least one of the two longest diagonals must belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nApply the same argument to the remaining diagonal among \\(\\{D_n,D_{n-1}\\}\\): after keeping one of them, the other still needs to be covered, and the only way to do so without using \\(n\\) separate lines is to keep it as well.  \n\nConsequently **both** \\(D_n\\) and \\(D_{n-1}\\) must be present in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nThese two lines are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and hence are **non‑sunny**.  \n\nSince \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk = n - (\\text{number of non‑sunny lines}) \\le n-2 .\n\\] ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Construction achieving every \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers all points of \\(T_n\\), and  \n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1. Base family (no sunny lines)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_n=\\{D_i : x+y=i+1\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=i+1\\) for the unique \\(i=a+b-1\\); hence it lies on \\(D_i\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal D_n\\) covers \\(T_n\\) and contains **no** sunny lines.  \nSet \\(\\mathcal L_0=\\mathcal D_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2. Turning two diagonals into one sunny line\n\nAssume we have already constructed \\(\\mathcal L_t\\) (\\(0\\le t\\le n-3\\)) with exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines and still covering \\(T_n\\).  \nAmong the diagonal lines present in \\(\\mathcal L_t\\) there are at least three, because at most \\(t\\) of the original \\(n\\) diagonals have been removed and \\(t\\le n-3\\).\n\nChoose two distinct remaining diagonals, say \\(D_{i}\\) and \\(D_{j}\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \nSelect a point \\(P\\) on \\(D_i\\) and a point \\(Q\\) on \\(D_j\\) such that the line \\(\\ell_{PQ}\\) is **not** parallel to any of the three forbidden directions.  \nThis is always possible: for instance take  \n\n\\[\nP=(1,i),\\qquad Q=(j,1).\n\\]\n\nThe direction vector of \\(\\ell_{PQ}\\) is \\((j-1,1-i)\\).  \nSince \\(j\\neq i\\) and both coordinates are non‑zero, the slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\), nor \\(-1\\); hence \\(\\ell_{PQ}\\) is sunny.\n\nNow form a new family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{t+1}= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_t\\setminus\\{D_i,D_j\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell_{PQ}\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Coverage:*  \nAll points of \\(D_i\\) other than \\(P\\) remain covered by the other diagonal lines (each diagonal appears at most once in \\(\\mathcal L_t\\)).  \nSimilarly, all points of \\(D_j\\) other than \\(Q\\) stay covered.  \nThe two points \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) are now covered by the newly added sunny line \\(\\ell_{PQ}\\).  \nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is still covered.\n\n*Distinctness:*  \n\\(\\ell_{PQ}\\) is not parallel to any diagonal, so it cannot coincide with any remaining line of \\(\\mathcal L_t\\); consequently the lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{t+1}\\) are still distinct.\n\nHence \\(\\mathcal L_{t+1}\\) contains exactly \\(t+1\\) sunny lines and satisfies the covering condition.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.3. Reaching any admissible \\(k\\)\n\nStarting from \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) and applying the replacement described above repeatedly, we obtain families \\(\\mathcal L_1,\\mathcal L_2,\\dots\\).  \nEach step increases the number of sunny lines by one while preserving the covering property.  \nWe may continue as long as at least three diagonal lines remain; this allows up to \\(n-2\\) replacements, after which exactly the two longest diagonals \\(D_n\\) and \\(D_{n-1}\\) are left untouched (they are required to stay non‑sunny by Lemma 2.2).  \n\nTherefore for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) the family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) obtained after \\(k\\) replacements is a valid configuration containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Conclusion  \n\nLemma 2.2 shows that no configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \nThe constructive procedure of Section 3 produces, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), an explicit configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
