 \n\n\\[\nP_i=(1,i)\\in D_i ,\\qquad\nR_i=(n-i,\\,i+1)\\in D_n .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_i\\) through \\(P_i\\) and \\(R_i\\) has direction vector  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf v_i=(n-i-1,\\;1).\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1}{\\,n-i-1\\,}\\), which is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\) because \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\).  \nHence \\(\\ell_i\\) is **sunny**.\n\nNow replace the diagonal \\(D_i\\) by the sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\).  \nThe resulting family still has \\(n\\) distinct lines because \\(\\ell_i\\) is not parallel to any diagonal and therefore cannot coincide with any remaining line.\n\n*Coverage after the replacement.*  \n\n* The point \\(P_i\\) (the only point of \\(D_i\\) that does **not** lie on the long diagonal \\(D_n\\)) is now on \\(\\ell_i\\).  \n* Every other point of \\(D_i\\) has coordinates \\((a,i+1-a)\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\). For such a point we have  \n  \\[\n  a+(i+1-a)=i+1\\le n-1,\n  \\]\n  so the point also belongs to the diagonal \\(D_{i+1}\\) when \\(i+1\\le n\\). In particular, for \\(a\\ge 2\\) the point lies on a diagonal of index at most \\(n-1\\), which is still present in the family (we have not removed any diagonal of index \\(\\ge i+1\\)).  \n  Consequently all these points remain covered.  \n* The line \\(D_n\\) is still present, so every point of \\(D_n\\) (including the auxiliary point \\(R_i\\)) is covered.\n\nThus the covering property is preserved while the number of sunny lines increases by one.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.3. Obtaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nChoose any subset \\(S\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) with \\(|S|=k\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\in S\\) perform the replacement described in §3.2, i.e. replace \\(D_i\\) by the sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\).  \nAll replacements involve distinct indices, so the resulting lines are all distinct.\n\nThe final family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of  \n\n* the two longest diagonals \\(D_n\\) and \\(D_{n-1}\\)