            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Notation and basic facts  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\nD_i:\\;x+y=i+1\n\\]\n\n(the “diagonal’’ of slope \\(-1\\)).  \nThe points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(D_i\\) are exactly the integer pairs \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b=i+1\\); there are \\(i\\) such points. In particular  \n\n\\[\n|D_n\\cap T_n|=n,\\qquad |D_{n-1}\\cap T_n|=n-1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its direction is different from the three forbidden directions:\n\\[\n(1,0)\\;( \\text{parallel to the }x\\text{-axis}),\\qquad\n(0,1)\\;( \\text{parallel to the }y\\text{-axis}),\\qquad\n(1,-1)\\;( \\text{parallel to }x+y=0).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nDenote by \\(k\\) the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.1  \nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) is **not** parallel to a diagonal \\(D_i\\), then \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(D_i\\) in at most one point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume \\(\\ell\\) intersected \\(D_i\\) in two distinct points \\(P,Q\\in T_n\\).  \nBoth \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) satisfy the equations of \\(\\ell\\) and of \\(D_i\\); hence the vector \\(\\overrightarrow{PQ}\\) is simultaneously parallel to the direction of \\(\\ell\\) and to the direction of \\(D_i\\) (which is \\((1,-1)\\)).  \nThus \\(\\ell\\) would be parallel to \\(D_i\\), contradicting the hypothesis. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.2  \nIn any covering \\(\\mathcal L\\) we must have \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the longest diagonal \\(D_n\\).  \nIf \\(D_n\\notin\\mathcal L\\), every point of \\(D_n\\cap T_n\\) must be covered by a line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) that is not parallel to \\(D_n\\).  \nBy Lemma 2.1 each such line can contain **at most one** point of \\(D_n\\).  \nSince \\(|D_n\\cap T_n|=n\\) by (1), we would need at least \\(n\\) distinct lines to cover \\(D_n\\).  \nBecause \\(\\mathcal L\\) has exactly \\(n\\) lines, this would leave no line available for any other point of \\(T_n\\), which is impossible.  \nHence \\(D_n\\) must belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \n\nApply the same reasoning to the second‑longest diagonal \\(D_{n-1}\\).  \nIf \\(D_{n-1}\\) were absent, its \\(n-1\\) points would each require a distinct line not parallel to \\(D_{n-1}\\); together with the \\(n\\) lines already needed for \\(D_n\\) we would need at least \\(n+(n-1)=2n-1>n\\) lines, a contradiction.  \nTherefore \\(D_{n-1}\\) also belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nBoth \\(D_n\\) and \\(D_{n-1}\\) are parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) and consequently are **non‑sunny**.  \nSince \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk = n - (\\text{number of non‑sunny lines}) \\le n-2 .\n\\] ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Construction achieving every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers all points of \\(T_n\\), and  \n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1. The base family (no sunny lines)\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_n=\\{D_i : x+y=i+1\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=i+1\\) for the unique \\(i=a+b-1\\); hence it lies on \\(D_i\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal D_n\\) covers \\(T_n\\) and contains **no** sunny lines.  \nSet \\(\\mathcal L_0=\\mathcal D_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2. Replacing a short diagonal by a sunny line\n\nFix an index \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(1,i)\\in D_i ,\\qquad\nR_i=(n-i,\\,i+1)\\in D_n .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_i\\) through \\(P_i\\) and \\(R_i\\) has direction vector  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf v_i=(n-i-1,\\;1).\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1}{\\,n-i-1\\,}\\), which is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\) because \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\).  \nHence \\(\\ell_i\\) is **sunny**.\n\nNow replace the diagonal \\(D_i\\) by the sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\).  \nThe resulting family still has \\(n\\) distinct lines because \\(\\ell_i\\) is not parallel to any diagonal and therefore cannot coincide with any remaining line.\n\n*Coverage after the replacement.*  \n\n* The point \\(P_i\\) (the only point of \\(D_i\\) that does **not** lie on the long diagonal \\(D_n\\)) is now on \\(\\ell_i\\).  \n* Every other point of \\(D_i\\) has coordinates \\((a,i+1-a)\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\). For such a point we have  \n  \\[\n  a+(i+1-a)=i+1\\le n-1,\n  \\]\n  so the point also belongs to the diagonal \\(D_{i+1}\\) when \\(i+1\\le n\\). In particular, for \\(a\\ge 2\\) the point lies on a diagonal of index at most \\(n-1\\), which is still present in the family (we have not