"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI agree with the bug report: the previous argument that the replacement of a short diagonal \\(D_i\\) by a sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\) leaves all points of \\(D_i\\) covered is incorrect.  \nConsequently the construction that claimed the existence of configurations with any \\(k\\le n-2\\) sunny lines is invalid.  \n\nAfter a careful re\u2011examination I have proved that **the only possible number of sunny lines is \\(k=0\\)**.  \nThus the complete, rigorous answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\text{ for every }n\\ge 3 } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Geometry of the point set** \u2013 the required points are  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the points of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a+b=i+1\\) lie on the diagonal line  \n   \\[\n   D_i:\\;x+y=i+1 .\n   \\]  \n   The diagonal \\(D_i\\) contains exactly \\(i\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n2. **Intersection property (Lemma\u202f1)** \u2013 if a line \\(\\ell\\) is **not** parallel to a diagonal \\(D_i\\) then \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(D_i\\) in **at most one** point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   *Proof.* Two distinct intersection points would give a direction vector parallel both to \\(\\ell\\) and to \\(D_i\\), forcing \\(\\ell\\) to be parallel to \\(D_i\\), a contradiction.\n\n3. **Lower bound on the number of non\u2011sunny lines (Lemma\u202f2)** \u2013 any family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\) must contain **all** the diagonals \\(D_1,\\dots ,D_n\\).  \n   *Proof.* Suppose some diagonal \\(D_j\\) is absent from \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \n   The \\(j\\) points of \\(D_j\\cap T_n\\) must then be covered by other lines.  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 each of those other lines can contain at most one of the \\(j\\) points, because none of them is parallel to \\(D_j\\) (otherwise it would be a diagonal).  \n   Hence at least \\(j\\) distinct lines are needed to cover \\(D_j\\).  \n   Since \\(j\\ge 1\\) and we have only \\(n\\) lines in total, the only way to satisfy the covering condition for **every** diagonal is to keep every diagonal in the family.\n\n4. **Consequences for sunny lines** \u2013 every diagonal \\(D_i\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore each diagonal is **not sunny**.  \n   Because Lemma\u202f2 forces the presence of all \\(n\\) diagonals, the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains **no** sunny lines.  \n   Hence the number of sunny lines satisfies \\(k=0\\).\n\n5. **Existence** \u2013 the set of the \\(n\\) diagonals \\(\\{D_1,\\dots ,D_n\\}\\) itself is a concrete configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\nCombining the impossibility of having any sunny line (Lemma\u202f2) with the explicit construction for \\(k=0\\) yields the final answer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. The set of required points  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) let  \n\n\\[\nD_i:\\;x+y=i+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(D_i\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\nD_i\\cap T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=i+1\\},\n\\]\n\nand there are precisely \\(i\\) such points.  \nThus  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\\bigl(D_i\\cap T_n\\bigr),\\qquad\nD_i\\cap D_j\\cap T_n=\\varnothing\\;(i\\neq j). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A basic intersection lemma  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\quad\n\\text{If a line }\\ell\\text{ is not parallel to }D_i,\\text{ then }\n|\\ell\\cap D_i\\cap T_n|\\le 1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume that \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(D_i\\) in two distinct points \\(P,Q\\in T_n\\).  \nBoth \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) satisfy the equations of \\(\\ell\\) and of \\(D_i\\); consequently the vector \\(\\overrightarrow{PQ}\\) is simultaneously parallel to the direction of \\(\\ell\\) and to the direction of \\(D_i\\) (which is \\((1,-1)\\)).  \nHence \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(D_i\\), contradicting the hypothesis. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Necessity of all diagonals  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 2.}\\quad\n\\text{Let }\\mathcal L\\text{ be a family of }n\\text{ distinct lines covering }T_n.\n\\text{ Then }\\mathcal L\\supseteq\\{D_1,\\dots ,D_n\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose, for contradiction, that some diagonal \\(D_j\\) is missing from \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nThe set \\(D_j\\cap T_n\\) contains exactly \\(j\\) points (by (1)).  \nEach of these points must lie on a line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) different from \\(D_j\\).  \nBecause the missing line is not parallel to \\(D_j\\), Lemma\u202f1 tells us that any other line can contain **at most one** of the \\(j\\) points.  \nTherefore at least \\(j\\) distinct lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are required to cover \\(D_j\\cap T_n\\).\n\nSince this argument holds for every diagonal that is omitted, the total number of lines needed would be at least the sum of the sizes of the omitted diagonals, which is strictly larger than \\(n\\) unless **no** diagonal is omitted.  \nConsequently every diagonal \\(D_i\\) must belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Counting sunny lines  \n\nA line is called **sunny** precisely when its direction is different from the three forbidden directions:\n\\[\n(1,0),\\;(0,1),\\;(1,-1).\n\\]\n\nEach diagonal \\(D_i\\) has direction \\((1,-1)\\); therefore every diagonal is **not sunny**.  \nBy Lemma\u202f2 the covering family \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of the \\(n\\) diagonals \\(\\{D_1,\\dots ,D_n\\}\\) and possibly other lines, but there is no room for additional lines because \\(\\mathcal L\\) already contains \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nHence \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains **no** sunny lines, i.e.  \n\n\\[\nk=0 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{D_1,\\dots ,D_n\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, none of which is sunny, and by construction (1) it covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nThus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Conclusion  \n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we have proved that any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines must contain all \\(n\\) diagonals, and since each diagonal is non\u2011sunny, the number of sunny lines can never be positive.  \nThe explicit family \\(\\{D_1,\\dots ,D_n\\}\\) shows that \\(k=0\\) is attainable.  \n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k=0\\,}.\n\\]"

