that passes through \\((a,b)\\); hence the point is covered.  \n   - All lines are distinct, and the number of sunny lines is exactly the prescribed \\(k\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion** – Lemma 2 gives the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\); Lemma 3 shows that this bound is also sufficient. Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. The required point set  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) let  \n\n\\[\nD_i:\\;x+y=i+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(D_i\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\nD_i\\cap T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=i+1\\},\n\\]\n\nand there are precisely \\(i\\) such points.  \nThus  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\\bigl(D_i\\cap T_n\\bigr),\\qquad\nD_i\\cap D_j\\cap T_n=\\varnothing\\;(i\\neq j). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Intersection Lemma  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\quad\n\\text{If a line }\\ell\\text{ is not parallel to }D_i,\\text{ then }\n|\\ell\\cap D_i\\cap T_n|\\le 1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume that \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(D_i\\) in two distinct points \\(P,Q\\in T_n\\).  \nBoth \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) satisfy the equations of \\(\\ell\\) and of \\(D_i\\); consequently the vector \\(\\overrightarrow{PQ}\\) is simultaneously parallel to the direction of \\(\\ell\\) and to the direction of \\(D_i\\) (which is \\((1,-1)\\)).  \nHence \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(D_i\\), contradicting the hypothesis. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. At least two non‑sunny lines are necessary  \n\nRecall that a line is **sunny** iff its direction is different from the three forbidden directions  \n\n\\[\n(1,0),\\;(0,1),\\;(1,-1),\n\\]\n\ni.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 2.}\\quad\n\\text{Let }\\mathcal L\\text{ be a family of }n\\text{ distinct lines covering }T_n.\n\\text{ Then at least two lines of }\\mathcal L\\text{ are non‑sunny.}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on the first row and the first column:\n\n\\[\nR=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-1\\},\\qquad\nC=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nBoth sets contain \\(n-1\\) points, and they intersect in the single point \\((1,1)\\).  \nHence \\(|R\\cup C|=2n-3\\).\n\nA sunny line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore it can contain **at most one** point of \\(R\\cup C\\).  \nIndeed, any two distinct points of \\(R\\cup C\\) have either the same \\(x\\)-coordinate (both in \\(C\\)), the same \\(y\\)-coordinate (both in \\(R\\)), or satisfy \\(x+y=2\\) (the pair \\((1,1)\\) and any other point of \\(R\\cup C\\)). In each case the line through them would be parallel to one of the forbidden directions, contradicting the definition of a sunny line.\n\nConsequently, if \\(\\mathcal L\\) contained \\(k\\) sunny lines, those lines could cover at most \\(k\\) points of \\(R\\cup C\\).  \nThe remaining \\(2n-3-k\\) points of \\(R\\cup C\\) must be covered by the non‑sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nSince each non‑sunny line can contain arbitrarily many points of \\(R\\cup C\\) (for example the vertical line \\(x=1\\) contains all of \\(R\\)), the only restriction is the total number of lines:\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L|=n\\ge k + (\\text{number of non‑sunny lines}).\n\\]\n\nIf there were at most one non‑sunny line, then we would have \\(n\\le k+1\\), i.e. \\(k\\ge n-1\\).  \nBut a sunny line can cover at most one point of \\(R\\cup C\\), so with \\(k\\ge n-1\\) we could cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(R\\cup C\\), far fewer than the required \\(2n-3\\) points (recall \\(n\\ge3\\)).  \nThus the assumption “at most one non‑sunny line’’ leads to a contradiction.  \n\nHence any covering family must contain **at least two** non‑sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLemma 2 shows that among the \\(n\\) lines at least two are forced to be non‑sunny.  \nTherefore the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 3.}\\quad\n\\text{For each integer }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n-2\\text{ there exists a family of }\nn\\text{ distinct lines covering }T_n\\text{ and containing exactly }k\\text{ sunny lines.}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe build the family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) as follows.\n\n1. **Two mandatory non‑sunny lines.**  \n   Take  \n   \\[\n   \\ell_{v}: x=1\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\ell_{h}: y=1 .\n   \\]  \n   Both are parallel to a forbidden direction (vertical and horizontal respectively), hence non‑sunny.  \n   They contain all points of \\(R\\cup C\\).\n\n2. **Lines covering the interior points.**  \n   For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n-2\\) define a line \\(\\ell_i\\) that passes through the point  \n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,2)\\in T_n .\n   \\]  \n   Choose the slopes of the \\(\\ell_i\\) as follows:\n   \\[\n   \\text{slope}(\\ell_i)=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   i & \\text{if }1\\le i\\le k,\\\\[2pt]\n   -i & \\text{if }k+1\\le i\\le n-2 .\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]  \n   Because the slopes are distinct, the lines \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct.  \n   Moreover, for \\(i\\le k\\) the slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); hence \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \n   For \\(i>k\\) the slope equals \\(-i\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\) when \\(i\\neq 1\\); however we may deliberately make these lines parallel to the forbidden direction \\(x+y=0\\) by taking slope \\(-1\\).  \n   To keep the construction simple we instead set, for \\(i>k\\), the line \\(\\ell_i\\) to be **parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)** and passing through \\(P_i\\); its equation is \\(x+y=i+2\\).  \n   Such a line is non‑sunny (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)) and distinct for different \\(i\\) because the constant term differs.\n\n3. **Verification of coverage.**  \n   - Every point of \\(R\\cup C\\) lies on \\(\\ell_v\\) or \\(\\ell_h\\).  \n   - Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\). Then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(a+b-1\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \n     Set \\(i=a+b-2\\); then \\(2\\le i\\le n-2\\) and the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the diagonal \\(D_{i+1}\\).  \n     By construction the line \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the point \\((i,2)\\) and, because it is not parallel to \\(D_{i+1}\\) (its direction is either a distinct slope or parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) which coincides with \\(D_{i+1}\\) only when \\(i+1\\) equals the constant of the line, i.e. the line is exactly \\(D_{i+1}\\)), it meets \\(D_{i+1}\\) in exactly one point of \\(T_n\\). That point is precisely \\((a,b)\\).  \n     Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\n   Therefore every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered by at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n4. **Counting sunny lines.**  \n   The family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of the two non‑sunny lines \\(\\ell_v,\\ell_h\\) and the \\(n-2\\) lines \\(\\ell_i\\) (\\(2\\le i\\le n-2\\)).  \n   By the choice of slopes, exactly the first \\(k\\) of the \\(\\ell_i\\) are sunny, the remaining \\(n-2-k\\) are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and thus non‑sunny.  \n   Consequently the total number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) is \\(k\\).\n\nThus for every admissible \\(k\\) we have produced a concrete configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all required points and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Final conclusion  \n\nLemma 2 gives the necessary condition