us the assumption “at most one non‑sunny line’’ leads to a contradiction.  \n\nHence any covering family must contain **at least two** non‑sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLemma 2 shows that among the \\(n\\) lines at least two are forced to be non‑sunny.  \nTherefore the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 3.}\\quad\n\\text{For each integer }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n-2\\text{ there exists a family of }\nn\\text{ distinct lines covering }T_n\\text{ and containing exactly }k\\text{ sunny lines.}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe build the family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) as follows.\n\n1. **Two mandatory non‑sunny lines.**  \n   Take  \n   \\[\n   \\ell_{v}: x=1\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\ell_{h}: y=1 .\n   \\]  \n   Both are parallel to a forbidden direction (vertical and horizontal respectively), hence non‑sunny.  \n   They contain all points of \\(R\\cup C\\).\n\n2. **Lines covering the interior points.**  \n   For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n-2\\) define a line \\(\\ell_i\\) that passes through the point  \n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,2)\\in T_n .\n   \\]  \n   Choose the slopes of the \\(\\ell_i\\) as follows:\n   \\[\n   \\text{slope}(\\ell_i)=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   i & \\text{if }1\\le i\\le k,\\\\[2pt]\n   -i & \\text{if }k+1\\le i\\le n-2 .\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]  \n   Because the slopes are distinct, the lines \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct.  \n   Moreover, for \\(i\\le k\\) the slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); hence \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \n   For \\(i>k\\) the slope equals \\(-i\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\) when \\(i\\neq 1\\); however we may deliberately make these lines parallel to the forbidden direction \\(x+y=0\\) by taking slope \\(-1\\).  \n   To 