            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis), from \\(\\infty\\) (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) and from \\(-1\\) (parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Covering by vertical lines)\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\ncover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b\\ge 1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le n\\); together with \\(a\\ge 1\\) this yields \\(1\\le a\\le n\\). Hence the point satisfies the equation \\(x=a\\) and lies on the line \\(V_a\\). ∎\n\nSince each \\(V_j\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, none of them is sunny. Consequently the value  \n\n\\[\nk=0\n\\]\n\nis realizable for every admissible \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (A family with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, \\(1\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\). Define\n\n* the **sunny** lines  \n\n  \\[\n  S_t:\\; y = x + t \\qquad (t=0,1,\\dots ,k-1),\n  \\]\n\n  whose slope is \\(1\\) (hence they are sunny);\n\n* the **vertical** lines  \n\n  \\[\n  V_j:\\; x=j \\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n  \\]\n\nThen the collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{S_0,S_1,\\dots ,S_{k-1}\\}\\cup\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-k}\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the columns whose abscissae are  \n\n\\[\na_i=n+1-k+i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed \\(i\\) the admissible ordinates satisfy  \n\n\\[\na_i\\le b\\le n+1-a_i = k-i+1,\n\\]\n\nhence the points of \\(T_n\\) lying in column \\(a_i\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\{(a_i,\\,a_i+t)\\mid t=0,1,\\dots ,k-i\\bigl\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le k-1\\) the line \\(S_t\\) contains the point \\((a_i, a_i+t)\\) whenever \\(t\\le k-i\\). Therefore the set \\(\\{S_t\\}_{t=0}^{k-1}\\) covers **all** points of the \\(k\\) columns \\(a_1,\\dots ,a_k\\).\n\nThe remaining columns are those with abscissae \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\). By Lemma 1 each vertical line \\(V_j\\) contains every point of column \\(j\\); consequently the vertical lines cover all points of these columns.\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).  \nThe lines are distinct because the vertical lines have infinite slope while the sunny lines have slope \\(1\\), and the sunny lines have different intercepts. Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_0,\\dots ,S_{k-1}\\) are sunny. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem (Complete description of admissible \\(k\\))\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe construction of Lemma 1 shows that \\(k=0\\) is attainable.  \nLemma 2 shows that for every \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) such a configuration exists.  \nSince we have precisely \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\); therefore no \\(k>n\\) is possible. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly all integers from \\(0\\) up to \\(n\\).**\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
