=\\{S'_0,\\dots ,S'_{k-1}\\}\\cup\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_c\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof of covering.*  \nThe vertical lines clearly cover all points whose abscissa \\(a\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le c\\).\n\nConsider a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a>c\\).  \nWrite \\(a=c+i\\) with \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\); thus \\(a=n+1-k+i\\).  \nFrom the defining inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a = n+1-(c+i)=k-i .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(b\\ge 1\\), (4) implies \\(1\\le b\\le k-i\\).  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nt:=i-b .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(1\\le b\\le k-i\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n0\\le t=i-b\\le i-1\\le k-1 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(t\\) is an integer between \\(0\\) and \\(k-1\\), so the line \\(S'_t\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).  \nMoreover\n\n\\[\ny = x-(c-t)\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; b = a-(c-t)\n      = (c+i)-(c-(i-b)) = b ,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S'_t\\).  \nThus every point with \\(a>c\\) is covered by a sunny line, and the claim follows. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem (Complete description of admissible \\(k\\))\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLemma 1 shows that \\(k=0\\) is attainable.  \nLemma 2 shows that for every \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) such a configuration exists.  \nSince we have precisely \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\); therefore no \\(k>n\\) is possible. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly all integers from \\(0\\) up to \\(n\\).**\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. I