ine \\(S_t\\) belongs to our family.  \n   Substituting \\(a_i=c+i\\) and \\(t=i-b\\) into (4) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y = x-(c-(i-b)) = x-(c-i+b) .\n   \\]\n\n   Evaluating at \\((x,y)=(a_i,b)=(c+i,b)\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   b = (c+i)-(c-i+b)=b ,\n   \\]\n\n   confirming that \\((a_i,b)\\) lies on \\(S_t\\).  \n   Consequently **every** point of each of the right\u2011hand columns \\(a_1,\\dots ,a_k\\) is covered by one of the sunny lines \\(S_0,\\dots ,S_{k-1}\\).\n\n   *Putting everything together.*  \n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\\{S_0,S_1,\\dots ,S_{k-1}\\}\\cup\\{\\,x=1,\\,x=2,\\dots ,x=c\\,\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\;+\\;c\\;=\\;k\\;+\\;(n-k)\\;=\\;n\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines.  \n   The first \\(k\\) lines are sunny, the remaining \\(c=n-k\\) vertical lines are not sunny.  \n   By the discussion above the vertical lines cover all points of the left\u2011hand columns \\(1,\\dots ,c\\), while the sunny lines cover all points of the right\u2011hand columns \\(a_1,\\dots ,a_k\\).  \n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   We have exhibited, for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), a configuration of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Because we have only \\(n\\) lines, no configuration can contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \n   Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}.\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis), from \\(\\infty\\) (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) and from \\(-1\\) (parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n---------------------------------------