line can cover at most one\nentire column.\n\nThus the total number of points that can be covered by the whole family of\n\\(n\\) lines is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\;+\\;(n-k)\\cdot \\max_{\\,\\text{column}}|C_i|\n= k+(n-k)\\cdot (k) .\n\\]\n\nBut the rightmost \\(k\\) columns contain \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points by (1), and\n\\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2 > k\\) for every integer \\(k\\ge 2\\).  Hence for \\(k\\ge 2\\) the\nrequired points cannot all be covered.\n\nIt remains to treat the case \\(k=1\\).  When \\(k=1\\) the rightmost column\n\\(C_1\\) consists of a single point, namely \\((n,1)\\).  That point could be\ncovered by the unique sunny line.  However the next column \\(C_2\\) contains\ntwo points \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\).  The sunny line can cover at most one of\nthem (Lemma\u202f1), so the other point must be covered by a non\u2011sunny line.\nA non\u2011sunny line that is not vertical cannot pass through both\n\\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\); a vertical line would be \\(x=n-1\\) and would also\ncontain the point \\((n-1,3)\\) which does **not** belong to \\(T_n\\) when\n\\(n\\ge 3\\).  Consequently the configuration would need an additional line,\ncontradicting the requirement of exactly \\(n\\) lines.\n\nTherefore no configuration with \\(k\\ge 1\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Existence for \\(k=0\\))\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\;x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) (because \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le n\\)),\nhence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_a\\).  All these lines are parallel to the\n\\(y\\)-axis, therefore none of them is sunny.  Thus the covering condition is\nsatisfied with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem (Complete description of admissible \\(k\\))\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the only non\u2011negative integer \