            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Size of \\(S_n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) the admissible values of \\(a\\) are\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-b\\); hence the number of points with second coordinate \\(b\\) is\n\\(n+1-b\\).  Summing over \\(b\\),\n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{b=1}^{n}(n+1-b)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Points that a sunny line can contain\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is not \\(0\\), not \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), and not\n\\(-1\\).  Let \\(\\ell\\) be such a line and let  \n\n\\[\n\\ell\\cap S_n=\\{(x_i,y_i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,m\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the slope of \\(\\ell\\) is different from \\(-1\\), the values\n\\(x_i+y_i\\) are all distinct; because the slope is different from \\(0\\), the\nvalues \\(y_i\\) are all distinct; and because the slope is different from\n\\(\\infty\\), the values \\(x_i\\) are all distinct.  Consequently the points of\n\\(\\ell\\cap S_n\\) lie in distinct rows, distinct columns and distinct\nanti‑diagonals of the triangular array \\(S_n\\).\n\nThe rows of \\(S_n\\) are indexed by \\(y=1,2,\\dots ,n\\); the columns by\n\\(x=1,2,\\dots ,n\\); the anti‑diagonals by the constant sum \\(x+y=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\).\nHence at most one point of \\(\\ell\\cap S_n\\) can belong to each of these three\nfamilies.  Since there are only \\(n\\) rows, we obtain  \n\n\\[\nm\\le n .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA sharper bound follows from the anti‑diagonal condition.  The smallest and\nlargest possible sums are \\(2\\) and \\(n+1\\); therefore the number of distinct\nsums that can appear among the points of \\(\\ell\\cap S_n\\) is at most\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus **any sunny line meets \\(S_n\\) in at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points.**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Points that a non‑sunny line can contain\n\nIf a line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, say \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), then\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\}\\subset S_n,\n\\]\nso the line contains exactly \\(n+1-b\\le n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \nThe same bound holds for a vertical line \\(x=a\\) and for a line of slope \\(-1\\)\n(i.e. \\(x+y=c\\)).  Hence a non‑sunny line can contain **at most \\(n\\) points** of\n\\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  No sunny line can appear in a covering by \\(n\\) lines\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and containing at least one\nsunny line.  Let \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line in \\(\\mathcal L\\) and let\n\\(\\mathcal L'=\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\); then \\(|\\mathcal L'|=n-1\\).\n\nUsing the bounds from Sections 2 and 3, the total number of points of \\(S_n\\)\nthat can be covered by \\(\\mathcal L\\) satisfies\n\n\\[\n|S_n|\n\\le (n-1)\\cdot n\n   +\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n= n^{2}-n+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBut by (1),\n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\nn^{2}-n+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n<\\frac{n^{2}+n}{2},\n\\]\n\nbecause the left‑hand side equals \\(\\frac{2n^{2}-2n+\\varepsilon}{2}\\) with\n\\(\\varepsilon\\le n+1\\), while \\(\\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}\\) is larger for \\(n\\ge3\\).\nThus (4) contradicts (1).  Hence **no sunny line can belong to a covering of\n\\(S_n\\) by exactly \\(n\\) lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Existence of a covering with \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, therefore it is not sunny.\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line\n\\(y=b\\).  Consequently the set \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) covers all points of\n\\(S_n\\) and contains **no sunny line**.  Hence a configuration with \\(k=0\\)\nexists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion\n\nA covering of the required set by exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines cannot contain a\nsunny line, and a covering with zero sunny lines does exist.  Therefore the\nonly possible value of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
