 of \\(S_n\\) and that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains\nat least one sunny line \\(\\ell\\).  Remove \\(\\ell\\); the remaining family\n\\(\\mathcal L'=\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\) has \\(n-1\\) lines.  Using (2) and (3),\n\n\\[\n|S_n|\n\\le (n-1)\\cdot n+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n= n^{2}-n+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBut by (1),\n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\nn^{2}-n+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n<\\frac{n^{2}+n}{2},\n\\]\n\nbecause the left‑hand side equals\n\\(\\frac{2n^{2}-2n+\\varepsilon}{2}\\) with \\(\\varepsilon\\le n+1\\), while the right‑hand side\nis \\(\\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}\\) and the inequality simplifies to \\(n^{2}-3n+\\varepsilon<0\\),\nwhich holds for all \\(n\\ge 3\\).  Thus (4) contradicts (1).  Hence **no sunny\nline can belong to a covering of \\(S_n\\) by exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Existence of a covering with \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, therefore it is not sunny.\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line\n\\(y=b\\).  Consequently the set \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) covers all points of\n\\(S_n\\) and contains **no sunny line**.  Thus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) does\nexist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nA covering of the required set by exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines cannot contain a\nsunny line, while a covering with zero sunny lines does exist.  Therefore the\nonly admissible value of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]"
