n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) the admissible values of \\(a\\) are\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-b\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{b=1}^{n}(n+1-b)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Points of \\(S_n\\) on a sunny line\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), from \\(\\infty\\) (vertical)\nand from \\(-1\\).  Let \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line and suppose  \n\n\\[\n\\ell\\cap S_n=\\{(x_i,y_i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,t\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the slope is not \\(0\\) the \\(y_i\\) are distinct; because the slope is\nnot \\(\\infty\\) the \\(x_i\\) are distinct; because the slope is not \\(-1\\) the\nsums \\(x_i+y_i\\) are distinct.  Hence the points can be written uniquely as  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i\\in I\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n* \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\),\n* \\(\\sigma:I\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is injective,\n* \\(i+\\sigma(i)\\le n+1\\) for every \\(i\\in I\\).\n\nThus we have a *partial permutation* of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) satisfying the\ninequality \\(i+\\sigma(i)\\le n+1\\).  The following lemma bounds its size.\n\n> **Lemma.**  \n> Let \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\(\\sigma:I\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) be injective\n> with \\(i+\\sigma(i)\\le n+1\\) for all \\(i\\in I\\). Then \\(|I|\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nPartition \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) into the \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) disjoint pairs\n\\(\\{i,n+1-i\\}\\) (and, when \\(n\\) is odd, a single middle element\n\\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\)).  For any \\(i\\in I\\) we have \\(\\sigma(i)\\le n+1-i\\); therefore\n\\(\\sigma(i)\\) belongs to the same pair as \\(i\\) or to a pair whose smaller\nelement is larger than \\(i\\).  Because \\(\\sigma\\) is injective, at most one\nelement of each pair can belong to \\(I\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n|I|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\nApplying the lemma to \\(\\