            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Size of \\(S_n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) the admissible values of \\(a\\) are\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-b\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{b=1}^{n}(n+1-b)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Points of \\(S_n\\) on a sunny line\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), from \\(\\infty\\) (vertical)\nand from \\(-1\\).  Let \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line and suppose  \n\n\\[\n\\ell\\cap S_n=\\{(x_i,y_i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,t\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the slope is not \\(0\\) the \\(y_i\\) are distinct; because the slope is\nnot \\(\\infty\\) the \\(x_i\\) are distinct; because the slope is not \\(-1\\) the\nsums \\(x_i+y_i\\) are distinct.  Hence the points can be written uniquely as  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i\\in I\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n* \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\),\n* \\(\\sigma:I\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is injective,\n* \\(i+\\sigma(i)\\le n+1\\) for every \\(i\\in I\\).\n\nThus we have a *partial permutation* of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) satisfying the\ninequality \\(i+\\sigma(i)\\le n+1\\).  The following lemma bounds its size.\n\n> **Lemma.**  \n> Let \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\(\\sigma:I\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) be injective\n> with \\(i+\\sigma(i)\\le n+1\\) for all \\(i\\in I\\). Then \\(|I|\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nPartition \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) into the \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) disjoint pairs\n\\(\\{i,n+1-i\\}\\) (and, when \\(n\\) is odd, a single middle element\n\\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\)).  For any \\(i\\in I\\) we have \\(\\sigma(i)\\le n+1-i\\); therefore\n\\(\\sigma(i)\\) belongs to the same pair as \\(i\\) or to a pair whose smaller\nelement is larger than \\(i\\).  Because \\(\\sigma\\) is injective, at most one\nelement of each pair can belong to \\(I\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n|I|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\nApplying the lemma to \\(\\ell\\cap S_n\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Points of \\(S_n\\) on a non‑sunny line\n\nIf a line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, say \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), then  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\}\\subset S_n,\n\\]\n\nso the line contains exactly \\(n+1-b\\le n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \nThe same bound holds for vertical lines \\(x=a\\) and for lines of slope \\(-1\\)\n(equations \\(x+y=c\\)).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\text{any non‑sunny line contains at most } n \\text{ points of } S_n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  No sunny line can appear when \\(n\\ge 4\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines\ncovers all points of \\(S_n\\) and that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least one sunny\nline \\(\\ell\\).  Removing \\(\\ell\\) leaves \\(n-1\\) lines; by (3) each of them\ncovers at most \\(n\\) points, while