            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Size of \\(S_n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) the admissible values of \\(a\\) are\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-b\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{b=1}^{n}(n+1-b)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Points of \\(S_n\\) on a sunny line\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), from \\(\\infty\\) (vertical)\nand from \\(-1\\).  Let \\(\\ell\\) be sunny and suppose  \n\n\\[\n\\ell\\cap S_n=\\{(x_i,y_i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,t\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the slope is not \\(0\\), the \\(y_i\\) are distinct; because it is not\nvertical, the \\(x_i\\) are distinct; because it is not \\(-1\\), the sums\n\\(x_i+y_i\\) are distinct.  Therefore we may relabel the points so that  \n\n\\[\n\\ell\\cap S_n=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i\\in I\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n* \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\),\n* \\(\\sigma:I\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is injective,\n* \\(i+\\sigma(i)\\le n+1\\) for every \\(i\\in I\\).\n\nThus the points form a *partial permutation* satisfying the inequality\n\\(i+\\sigma(i)\\le n+1\\).\n\n> **Lemma 2.**  \n> If \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\(\\sigma:I\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is injective\n> with \\(i+\\sigma(i)\\le n+1\\) for all \\(i\\in I\\), then \\(|I|\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nPair the numbers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\) as \\(\\{1,n\\},\\{2,n-1\\},\\dots\\).  (When \\(n\\) is\nodd the middle element \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) forms a singleton pair.)  \nIf \\(i\\in I\\) then \\(\\sigma(i)\\le n+1-i\\); consequently \\(\\sigma(i)\\) belongs to\nthe same pair as \\(i\\) or to a pair whose smaller element is larger than \\(i\\).\nBecause \\(\\sigma\\) is injective, at most one element of each pair can lie in\n\\(I\\).  Hence \\(|I|\\) does not exceed the number of pairs, i.e.  \n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\). ∎\n\nApplying Lemma 2 to \\(\\ell\\cap S_n\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Points of \\(S_n\\) on a non‑sunny line\n\nIf a line is horizontal, say \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), then the condition\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); thus the line contains exactly\n\\(n+1-b\\le n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  The same bound holds for vertical lines\n\\(x=a\\) and for lines of slope \\(-1\\) (equations \\(x+y=c\\)).  Therefore  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le n\\qquad\\text{for every non‑sunny line }\\ell .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Impossibility of using a sunny line\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of\n\\(S_n\\) and that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least one sunny line.\nWrite \\(\\mathcal L=\\mathcal S\\cup\\mathcal N\\) with \\(\\mathcal S\\) the sunny\nlines, \\(\\mathcal N\\) the non‑sunny lines, and let \\(s=|\\mathcal S|\\ge 1\\),\n\\(t=n-s\\).\n\nFrom (2) and (3),\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl|\\bigcup_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L}\\bigl(\\ell\\cap S_n\\bigr)\\Bigr|\n\\le s\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+t\\,n\n= s\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-s)n .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe right‑hand side of (4) is a decreasing affine function of \\(s\\) because\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le n-1\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\nHence the largest possible value under the condition \\(s\\ge 1\\) is attained at\n\\(s=1\\).  Define  \n\n\\[\nf(n):=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-1)n .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nWe compare \\(f(n)\\) with \\(|S_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).\n\n*If \\(n=2m\\) is even (\\(m\\ge 2\\)):*  \n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m\\), so  \n\n\\[\nf(2m)=m+(2m-1)2m=4m^{2}-m,\n\\qquad\n|S_{2m}|=2m^{2}+m .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(f(2m)-|S_{2m}|=2m^{2}-2m=2m(m-1)\\ge 4>0\\).\n\n*If \\(n=2m+1\\) is odd (\\(m\\ge 1\\)):*  \n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m+1\\), so  \n\n\\[\nf(2m+1)=(m+1)+(2m)(2m+1)=4m^{2}+3m+1,\n\\qquad\n|S_{2m+1}|=2m^{2}+3m+1 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(f(2m+1)-|S_{2m+1}|=2m^{2}\\ge 2>0\\).\n\nIn every case \\(f(n) > |S_n|\\).  Because (4) shows that any family containing a\nsunny line can cover at most \\(f(n)\\) points, such a family cannot cover all of\n\\(S_n\\).  Therefore **no covering of \\(S_n\\) by exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines can\ncontain a sunny line**.  Consequently any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk=0 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Construction with \\(k=0\\)\n\nThe \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n\n\\]\n\nare all non‑sunny, distinct, and for each \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(b\\in\\{1,\n\\dots ,n\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\).  Hence these \\(n\\) lines\ncover all points of \\(S_n\\) and contain exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) a family of