, (4) yields the\n   necessary inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   n^{2}-kn+k\\,m\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(m=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) and simplifying we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n-1,& n\\text{ even},\\\\[2pt]\n   n,   & n\\text{ odd}.\n   \\end{cases}}\\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus (6) is a *necessary* condition on \\(k\\).\n\n5. **Sufficiency \u2013 explicit constructions.**  \n\n   *Notation.*  Write \\(n=2p\\) (even) or \\(n=2p+1\\) (odd).  Set  \n\n   \\[\n   m=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   p,      & n=2p,\\\\\n   p+1,    & n=2p+1 .\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   *Partition of \\(S_n\\).*  \n   The points of \\(S_n\\) can be partitioned into \\(n\\) groups,\n   each group containing at most \\(m\\) points, in the following way.\n\n   - For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,p\\}\\) put  \n     \\[\n     G_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le n+1-i\\}\\cap S_n .\n     \\]\n     The set \\(G_i\\) consists of the points of the vertical line \\(x=i\\) that\n     belong to \\(S_n\\); its size is \\(n+1-i\\le n\\le m\\) when \\(i\\ge p\\) and\n     equals \\(m\\) when \\(i\\le p\\).\n\n   - When \\(n\\) is odd we need one extra group\n     \\[\n     G_{p+1}= \\{(p+1,p+1)\\},\n     \\]\n     a single point.\n\n   The total number of groups obtained is exactly \\(n\\); each group has size\n   \\(\\le m\\).\n\n   *Realising each group by a line.*  \n\n   - If a group consists of a single point, we may use any sunny line passing\n     through that point (for instance the line of slope \\(1\\) through it).\n\n   - If a group contains at least two points, we choose a sunny line that\n     passes through **all** points of the group.  This is possible because the\n     points of any group have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates\n     and distinct sums \\(x+y\\); consequently they lie on a line whose slope is\n     different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  An explicit formula is obtained by solving\n     for the line through the first two points of the group; the remaining points\n     satisfy the same linear equation by construction of the groups.\n\n   In this way we obtain exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, each covering one of the\n   groups, and the union of the groups is the whole set \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Counting sunny lines.*  \n   In the construction above the groups \\(G_i\\) with \\(|G_i|>1\\) are realised by\n   sunny lines, while a group consisting of a single point may be realised by a\n   non\u2011sunny line (e.g. a horizontal line) if we wish to reduce the number of\n   sunny lines.  By deciding for which of the \\(n\\) groups we use a horizontal\n   (or vertical, or slope \\(-1\\)) line we can obtain **any** integer \\(k\\) that\n   satisfies the bound (6).  Concretely:\n\n   - For even \\(n=2p\\) we keep exactly one group (say \\(G_{p}\\)) realised by a\n     horizontal line; the remaining \\(n-1\\) groups are realised by sunny lines.\n     Hence we obtain \\(k=n-1\\).  By merging several horizontal groups we can\n     obtain any smaller \\(k\\) down to \\(0\\).\n\n   - For odd \\(n=2p+1\\) we may keep all groups realised by sunny lines, giving\n     \\(k=n\\).  Merging any number of groups into horizontal lines reduces the\n     number of sunny lines by the same amount, so every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n     is attainable.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The inequality (6) is both necessary and sufficient.  Therefore the set of\n   admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly the one described in (6).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Size of \\(S_n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) the admissible values of \\(a\\) are\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-b\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{b=1}^{n}(n+1-b)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Points of \\(S_n\\) on a sunny line\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), from \\(\\infty\\) (vertical)\nand from \\(-1\\).  Suppose a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(S_n\\) in the points\n\\((x_i,y_i)\\;(i=1,\\dots ,t)\\).  Because the slope is not \\(0\\) the \\(y_i\\) are\npairwise distinct; because it is not vertical the \\(x_i\\) are distinct; because\nit is not \\(-1\\) the sums \\(x_i+y_i\\) are distinct.  Relabelling the points we\nmay write  \n\n\\[\n\\ell\\cap S_n=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i\\in I\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), \\(\\sigma:I\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is injective,\nand \\(i+\\sigma(i)\\le n+1\\) for every \\(i\\in I\\).\n\n**Lemma 2.1.**  \nIf \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\(\\sigma:I\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is injective with\n\\(i+\\sigma(i)\\le n+1\\) for all \\(i\\in I\\), then \\(|I|\\le m\\), where  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Pair the numbers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\) as \\(\\{1,n\\},\\{2,n-1\\},\\dots\\).\n(When \\(n\\) is odd the middle element \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) forms a singleton\npair.)  If \\(i\\in I\\) then \\(\\sigma(i)\\le n+1-i\\); consequently \\(\\sigma(i)\\) lies\nin the same pair as \\(i\\) or in a pair whose smaller element is larger than \\(i\\).\nBecause \\(\\sigma\\) is injective, at most one element of each pair can belong to\n\\(I\\).  Hence \\(|I|\\) does not exceed the number of pairs, i.e. \\(m\\). \u220e\n\nApplying Lemma\u202f2.1 to \\(\\ell\\cap S_n\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le m .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Points of \\(S_n\\) on a non\u2011sunny line\n\nIf a line is horizontal, say \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), then the condition\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); thus the line contains exactly\n\\(n+1-b\\le n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  The same bound holds for vertical lines\n\\(x=a\\) and for lines of slope \\(-1\\) (equations \\(x+y=c\\)).  Therefore  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le n\\qquad\\text{for every non\u2011sunny line }\\ell .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A counting inequality for a family with \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all\npoints of \\(S_n\\).  Suppose \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains \\(k\\) sunny lines\n(\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)).  Using (2) for each sunny line and (3) for each non\u2011sunny\nline we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl|\\bigcup_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L}(\\ell\\cap S_n)\\Bigr|\n\\le k\\,m+(n-k)n\n= n^{2}-kn+k\\,m .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSince the left\u2011hand side equals \\(|S_n|=n(n+1)/2\\), (4) yields the necessary\ncondition\n\n\\[\nn^{2}-kn+k\\,m\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(m=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) and simplify.  Write\n\\(n=2p\\) (even) or \\(n=2p+1\\) (odd).\n\n*Even case* \\(n=2p\\) (\\(p\\ge2\\)).  Then \\(m=p\\) and (5) becomes  \n\n\\[\n4p^{2}-2pk+kp\\ge p(2p+1)\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\nk\\le 2p-1=n-1 .\n\\tag{6e}\n\\]\n\n*Odd case* \\(n=2p+1\\) (\\(p\\ge1\\)).  Then \\(m=p+1\\) and (5) becomes  \n\n\\[\n(2p+1)^{2}-(2p+1)k+k(p+1)\\ge (2p+1)(p+1)\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\nk\\le 2p+1=n .\n\\tag{6o}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\le n-1\\text{ if }n\\text{ is even},\\qquad\n       k\\le n\\text{ if }n\\text{ is odd}. \\;}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Sufficiency \u2013 constructing families for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe now show that every integer \\(k\\) obeying (7) can indeed be realised.\n\n---\n\n##### 5.1  Partition of \\(S_n\\) into \\(n\\) blocks of size \\(\\le m\\)\n\nDefine for \\(i=1,\\dots ,p\\)\n\n\\[\nB_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le n+1-i\\}\\cap S_n .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(B_i\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=i\\); its cardinality is\n\\(n+1-i\\).  Because \\(i\\ge1\\),\n\n\\[\n|B_i|=\n\\begin{cases}\np      &\\text{if }i\\le p,\\\\\n\\le p  &\\text{if }i>p .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(|B_i|\\le m\\) for all \\(i\\).\n\nWhen \\(n\\) is odd we have one extra point \\((p+1,p+1)\\); put  \n\n\\[\nB_{p+1}=\\{(p+1,p+1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nNow we have exactly \\(n\\) blocks:\n\\[\n\\mathcal B=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{B_1,\\dots ,B_{2p}\\}, & n=2p,\\\\[2pt]\n\\{B_1,\\dots ,B_{2p+1}\\}, & n=2p+1 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nEach block contains at most \\(m\\) points and the blocks are pairwise disjoint;\ntheir union is \\(S_n\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 5.2  Realising each block by a line\n\n*If a block contains at least two points*, the points have distinct\n\\(x\\)-coordinates, distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates and distinct sums \\(x+y\\); therefore\nthey lie on a unique line whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  Hence\nthe block can be covered by a **sunny** line.\n\n*If a block consists of a single point* we may cover it either by a sunny line\nor by a non\u2011sunny line (horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\)).  Choosing a\nnon\u2011sunny line for a block reduces the total number of sunny lines by one.\n\nThus we obtain exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, each covering one block, and the\nunion of the lines is \\(S_n\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 5.3  Obtaining any admissible number \\(k\\) of sunny lines\n\nStart with the construction in which **every** block of size \\(\\ge2\\) is covered\nby a sunny line; this yields the maximal possible number of sunny lines,\nnamely the bound in (7) (i.e. \\(n-1\\) for even \\(n\\) and \\(n\\) for odd \\(n\\)).\nNow, for each block we wish to *de\u2011sunny*, replace the sunny line by a\nhorizontal line (or any non\u2011sunny line) that passes through the block\u2019s single\npoint (if the block has size\u202f1 we already have a non\u2011sunny line).  Each such\nreplacement reduces the count of sunny lines by exactly one and does not affect\nthe covering property because the block is still covered.\n\nConsequently, by performing the replacement on any desired number of blocks we\ncan achieve **any** integer \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and the maximal value given by\n(7).  In particular:\n\n* for even \\(n\\) we can realise every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\);\n* for odd \\(n\\) we can realise every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 5.4  Example (illustrating the construction)\n\nTake \\(n=5\\) (odd).  Here \\(m=\\lfloor(5+1)/2\\rfloor=3\\).\nThe blocks are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nB_1&=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4)\\},\\\\\nB_2&=\\{(2,1),(2,2),(2,3)\\},\\\\\nB_3&=\\{(3,1),(3,2)\\},\\\\\nB_4&=\\{(4,1)\\},\\qquad\nB_5=\\{(5,0)\\}\\ (\\text{empty, omitted}).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n\\(B_1,B_2,B_3\\) are covered by sunny lines (slopes \\(-\\tfrac13,-\\tfrac12,-1\\));\n\\(B_4\\) is covered by the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  Thus we have \\(k=3\\) sunny\nlines, which is allowed because the maximal value for \\(n=5\\) is \\(5\\).  By\nreplacing any of the sunny lines with a horizontal line we can obtain any\nsmaller \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Final answer\n\nCombining the necessary condition (7) with the explicit constructions of\nSection\u202f5, we conclude that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n0,1,\\dots ,n-1, & \\text{if }n\\text{ is even},\\\\[4pt]\n0,1,\\dots ,n,   & \\text{if }n\\text{ is odd}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nThus for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the problem is completely solved."

