(i)\\le n+1-i\\); consequently \\(\\sigma(i)\\) lies in the\nsame pair as \\(i\\) or in a pair whose smaller element exceeds \\(i\\).\nSince \\(\\sigma\\) is injective, at most one element of each pair can belong to\n\\(I\\).  Hence \\(|I|\\) does not exceed the number of pairs, i.e. \\(m\\). ∎\n\nApplying the lemma to \\(\\ell\\cap S_n\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le m .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Points of \\(S_n\\) on a non‑sunny line\n\nIf \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal, say \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), then\n\\(a\\) must satisfy \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); thus \\(|\\ell\\cap S_n|=n+1-b\\le n\\).\nThe same bound holds for vertical lines \\(x=a\\) and for lines of slope \\(-1\\)\n(equations \\(x+y=c\\)).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le n\\qquad(\\ell\\text{ non‑sunny}). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A necessary condition on the number of sunny lines\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all\npoints of \\(S_n\\), and suppose \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains \\(k\\) sunny lines\n(\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)).  Using (2) for each sunny line and (3) for each of the\nremaining \\(n-k\\) lines we obtain\n\n\\[\n|S_n|\n   =\\Bigl|\\bigcup_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L}(\\ell\\cap S_n)\\Bigr|\n   \\le k\\,m+(n-k)n .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) from (1) and \\(m=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\n*If \\(n=2p\\) (even), then \\(m=p\\) and (4) becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{2p(2p+1)}2\\le k p+(2p-k)2p\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\nk\\le 2p-1=n-1 .\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n=2p+1\\) (odd), then \\(m=p+1\\) and (4) becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(2p+1)(2p+2)}2\\le k(p+1)+(2p+1-k)(2p+1)\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\nk\\le 2p+1=n .\n\\]\n\nThus any feasible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn-1,& n\\text{ even},\\\\[2pt]\nn,   & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Sufficiency – constructing families for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) that satisfies (5).  We shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines\ncovering all points of \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n##### 5.1  Non‑sunny horizontal lines\n\nTake the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n-k .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nH_b:=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\}= \\ell_b\\cap S_n ,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\ell_b\\) denotes the line \\(y=b\\).  Because \\(\\ell_b\\) is horizontal,\nit is non‑sunny, and \\(|H_b|=n+1-b\\le n\\).  The lines in (6) are distinct and\ntogether cover all points of \\(S_n\\) whose second coordinate does not exceed\n\\(n-k\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 5.2  Remaining points\n\nAfter removing the points covered by (6) the set of uncovered points is  \n\n\\[\nU:=S_n\\setminus\\bigcup_{b=1}^{\\,n-k}H_b\n   =\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge n-k+1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA direct count gives  \n\n\\[\n|U|\n   =|S_n|-\\sum_{b=1}^{n-k}|H_b|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\sum_{b=1}^{n-k}(n+1-b)\n   =k .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus exactly \\(k\\) points remain uncovered.\n\nFor each \\((a,b)\\in U\\) we draw a sunny line that passes **only** through this\npoint.  For example, the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{(a,b)}:\\; y=\\frac12 x+\\Bigl(b-\\frac12 a\\Bigr)\n\\]\n\nhas slope \\(\\frac12\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence it is sunny.  Distinct points of\n\\(U\\) receive distinct lines, so we obtain precisely \\(|U|=k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n##### 5.3  Verification\n\nThe family consisting of the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines (6) together with the\n\\(k\\) sunny singleton lines \\(\\{\\ell_{(a,b)}\\mid (a,b)\\in U\\}\\) contains\nexactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers every point of \\(S_n\\), and has exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.  Because \\(k\\) was an arbitrary integer satisfying (5), the\nconstruction works for **all** admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Final answer\n\nThe necessary condition (5) and the explicit construction above show that the\ncondition is also sufficient.  Consequently, for each integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set\nof possible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n0,1,\\dots ,n-1, & \\text{if }n\\text{ is even},\\\\[4pt]\n0,1,\\dots ,n,   & \\text{if }n\\text{ is odd}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nThus the problem is completely solved."
