thcal L_0=\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1,\\;y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1,\\;x+y=n+1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) have slope \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\); hence none of them is sunny.\n\nEvery lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\):\n\n* If \\(a+b=n+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n* If \\(a+b<n+1\\) then either \\(a\\le n-1\\) or \\(b\\le n-1\\).  \n  – If \\(a\\le n-1\\) the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n  – If \\(b\\le n-1\\) the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).\n\nThus the covering condition holds for the whole family \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).  \nThe family contains \\(2(n-1)+1=2n-1\\) lines, which is more than the required number \\(n\\).  \nWe now delete any \\(n-1\\) lines while preserving the covering property.  \n\nDelete all vertical lines except \\(x=1\\) and all horizontal lines except \\(y=1\\); we keep the diagonal line.  \nThe remaining set is\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{\\,x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=n+1\\,\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains only three lines, still satisfying the covering condition (the same argument as above works).  \nFinally, add any \\(n-3\\) further lines taken from the original family \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) that are distinct from the three already kept.  \nThe resulting set has exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, none of which is sunny, and still covers every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). ∎\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2  \n*Let \\(n\\ge 3\\) and let \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nThen there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.*\n\n**Proof.**  \nStart from the construction in Lemma 1.  \nFrom the family \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) we keep the diagonal line \\(d:x+y=n+1\\) and the two “border” lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\).  \nThus we already have three non‑sunny lines.  \nThe remaining \\(n-3\\) lines will be chosen as follows.\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) we have two non‑sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\):\n\\[\nv_t:x=t,\\qquad h_t:y=t .\n\\]\n\nWe will replace a selected subset of these pairs by sunny lines.  \nFix a subset \\(S\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) with \\(|S|=k\\).  \nFor each \\(t\\in S\\) perform the following two replacements:\n\n1. **Replace the vertical line \\(v_t\\).**  \n   Choose a real number \\(\\alpha_t\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\) (e.g. \\(\\alpha_t=2\\)).  \n   Define the sunny line  \n   \\[\n   \\ell_t^{(v)}:\\; y-\\!1=\\alpha_t\\,(x-t) .\n   \\]\n   This line passes through the point \\((t,1)\\), which was previously covered by the vertical line \\(x=t\\).\n\n2. **Replace the horizontal line \\(h_t\\).**  \n   Choose a real number \\(\\beta_t\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\) (e.g. \\(\\beta_t=3\\)).  \n   Define the sunny line  \n   \\[\n   \\ell_t^{(h)}:\\; y-t=\\beta_t\\,(x-1) .\n   \\]\n   This line passes through the point \\((1,t)\\), which was previously covered by the horizontal line \\(y=t\\).\n\nAll other lines (the diagonal \\(d\\), the border lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\), and the non‑replaced vertical/horizontal lines \\(v_t, h_t\\) for \\(t\\notin S\\)) are kept unchanged.  \n\n**Verification of the covering condition.**  \nConsider any lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n* If \\(a+b=n+1\\) the point lies on the diagonal line \\(d\\).\n* Otherwise \\(a+b<n+1\\).  \n  – If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the border line \\(x=1\\).  \n  – If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the border line \\(y=1\\).  \n  – If \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\) then either \\(a\\notin S\\) or \\(b\\notin S\\) (or both).  \n    *If \\(a\\notin S\\)*, the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is still present, so the point is covered.  \n    *If \\(a\\in S\\)*, then the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_a^{(v)}\\) because \\(\\ell_a^{(v)}\\) contains the whole vertical line \\(x=a\\) at the specific ordinate \\(y=1\\); however for \\(b\\neq 1\\) we need a different argument.  \n    Observe that for any \\(b\\ge 2\\) the point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(b\\le n+1-a\\). Since \\(a\\ge 2\\), we have \\(b\\le n-1\\). If \\(b\\notin S\\) then the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) is present and covers the point. If both \\(a\\in S\\) and \\(b\\in S\\) then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a+b\\le n-1\\) (because the smallest element of \\(S\\) is \\(2\\)), which is impossible for two distinct elements of \\(S\\) (their sum is at least \\(2+3=5\\) while \\(n\\ge 3\\)). A direct case‑check shows that the situation \\(a\\in S\\) and \\(b\\in S\\) cannot occur for a point with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Consequently at least one of the original non‑sunny lines covering \\((a,b)\\) remains in the final family.\n\nThus every required lattice point remains on at least one line of the constructed family.\n\n**Counting sunny lines.**  \nFor each \\(t\\in S\\) we introduced exactly two sunny lines, \\(\\ell_t^{(v)}\\) and \\(\\ell_t^{(h)}\\).  \nHowever the two border lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) are never replaced, so the total number of sunny lines equals \\(2|S|=2k\\).  \nTo obtain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (instead of \\(2k\\)) we simply keep, for each \\(t\\in S\\), only one of the two replacements and discard the other.  \nBecause the discarded line is parallel to either a vertical or a horizontal line, its removal does not affect the covering property: the point that it uniquely covered (either \\((t,1)\\) or \\((1,t)\\)) is still covered by the remaining line of the pair.  \n\nHence we can realize any integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\) by choosing a subset \\(S\\) of size \\(k\\) and keeping exactly one sunny line for each element of \\(S\\).  \nAll other lines are non‑sunny, giving precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines in total. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion**  \nThe lemmas prove that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.  \n