o sunny line)\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) consider the following collection of lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\n\\Bigl\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1,\\;\n      y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1,\\;\n      x+y=n+1\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) have slope \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\); therefore none of them is sunny.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\) be a lattice point with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n* If \\(a+b=n+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n* If \\(a+b<n+1\\) then either \\(a\\le n-1\\) or \\(b\\le n-1\\).  \n  \u2013 If \\(a\\le n-1\\) the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n  \u2013 If \\(b\\le n-1\\) the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).\n\nHence every required point belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) contains \\(2(n-1)+1=2n-1\\) lines, more than the required number \\(n\\).  \nDelete any \\(n-1\\) lines while keeping the covering property.  \nFor instance keep the three lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\qquad y=1,\\qquad x+y=n+1,\n\\]\n\nwhich already cover all points as argued above, and then add any \\(n-3\\) further distinct lines from \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).  \nThe resulting set \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, none of which is sunny, and still covers every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\nThus \\(k=0\\) is always attainable. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Realising any \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\))\n\nFix \\(n\\ge 3\\) and an integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nStart from the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) constructed in Lemma\u202f1, which contains the three \u201cborder\u2019\u2019 lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\qquad y=1,\\qquad d:\\;x+y=n+1,\n\\]\n\nand the remaining \\(n-3\\) lines are a selection of vertical lines \\(x=t\\) (\\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\)) and horizontal lines \\(y=t\\) (\\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\)).  \nAmong the interior indices \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) choose an arbitrary subset  \n\n\\[\nS\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\},\\qquad |S|=k .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(t\\in S\\) we **replace** the vertical line \\(x=t\\) by a sunny line that still passes through the lattice point \\((t,1)\\) (which was previously covered by \\(x=t\\)).  \nChoose any slope \\(\\alpha_t\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\) (for instance \\(\\alpha_t=2\\)) and define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_t^{(v)}:\\; y-1=\\alpha_t\\,(x-t).\n\\]\n\nSimilarly replace the horizontal line \\(y=t\\) by a sunny line that passes through \\((1,t)\\).  \nPick any slope \\(\\beta_t\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\) (e.g. \\(\\beta_t=3\\)) and set  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_t^{(h)}:\\; y-t=\\beta_t\\,(x-1).\n\\]\n\nAll other lines (the diagonal \\(d\\), the two border lines, and the vertical/horizontal lines with index not in \\(S\\)) are left unchanged.  \nThe total number of lines remains \\(n\\) because each replacement removes one line and adds one line.\n\n**Covering property.**  \nLet \\((a,b)\\) be a lattice point with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n* If \\(a+b=n+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(d\\).\n* If \\(a+b<n+1\\) then at least one of \\(a\\) or \\(b\\) equals \\(1\\) or is not in \\(S\\):\n  * If \\(a=1\\) the point lies on the border line \\(x=1\\).\n  * If \\(b=1\\) the point lies on the border line \\(y=1\\).\n  * Suppose \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\).  \n    - If \\(a\\notin S\\) the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is still present, covering the point.  \n    - If \\(a\\in S\\) then the point \\((a,b)\\) is covered by the sunny line \\(\\ell_a^{(v)}\\) **only when \\(b=1\\)**; however \\(b\\ge2\\) in this subcase, so we must use the horizontal direction.  \n      Since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a\\ge2\\), we have \\(b\\le n-1\\).  \n      If \\(b\\notin S\\) the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) is present and covers the point.  \n      If both \\(a\\in S\\) and \\(b\\in S\\) then \\(a+b\\ge 2+2=4\\). Because the smallest element of \\(S\\) is \\(2\\), the sum of any two (not necessarily distinct) elements of \\(S\\) is at least \\(4\\).  \n      The inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is compatible with this, so we must check whether the point could be uncovered.  \n      In the situation \\(a\\in S,\\;b\\in S\\) the point \\((a,b)\\) is covered by the sunny line \\(\\ell_b^{(h)}\\), because \\(\\ell_b^{(h)}\\) passes through \\((1,b)\\) and has slope \\(\\beta_b\\neq0\\); its equation can be rewritten as  \n\n      \\[\n      y = \\beta_b x + (b-\\beta_b).\n      \\]\n\n      Substituting \\(x=a\\) gives \\(y = \\beta_b a + (b-\\beta_b)\\).  \n      Since \\(\\beta_b\\) was chosen arbitrarily (but fixed), we can guarantee that the line \\(\\ell_b^{(h)}\\) passes through \\((a,b)\\) **only when** \\(a=1\\). Hence this does not help.  \n\n      To resolve the remaining case we observe that the set \\(S\\) can be chosen so that no pair \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) has both coordinates in \\(S\\).  \n      Indeed, because \\(|S|=k\\le n-2\\), we can select \\(S\\) to be any \\(k\\) numbers among \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) that are **pairwise far enough**; for example take the first \\(k\\) numbers \\(2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\).  \n      Then the largest possible sum of two elements of \\(S\\) is \\((k+1)+(k)=2k+1\\).  \n      Since \\(k\\le n-2\\), we have \\(2k+1\\le 2(n-2)+1 = 2n-3\\).  \n      For any point with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we certainly have \\(a+b\\le n+1 < 2n-3\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\); consequently it is impossible that both \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) belong to \\(S\\).  \n\n      Hence at least one of \\(a,b\\) is not in \\(S\\), and the corresponding original vertical or horizontal line is still present, covering \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every required lattice point remains on at least one line after the replacements.\n\n**Number of sunny lines.**  \nFor each \\(t\\in S\\) we introduced exactly one sunny line (either \\(\\ell_t^{(v)}\\) or \\(\\ell_t^{(h)}\\); we may choose arbitrarily which one to keep).  \nConsequently the final configuration contains precisely \\(|S|=k\\) sunny lines, the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines being the unchanged non\u2011sunny ones.\n\nTherefore for every \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable configuration exists. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\))\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition contains at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \nThen at most one line is non\u2011sunny, i.e. has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).\n\nConsider the two lattice points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) (both satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) because \\(n\\ge3\\)).  \nA line that contains \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) must be vertical (\\(x=1\\)), which is non\u2011sunny.  \nHence the vertical line \\(x=1\\) must be present in the family.\n\nNow look at the point \\((2,1)\\).  \nIt is not on the line \\(x=1\\); any line passing through \\((2,1)\\) and also through \\((1,1)\\) would be horizontal (\\(y=1\\)), another non\u2011sunny direction.  \nSince we are allowed only one non\u2011sunny line, the point \\((2,1)\\) cannot be covered by the same line as \\((1,1)\\).  \nTherefore a second non\u2011sunny line is required (either the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) or the diagonal line \\(x+y=3\\)).  \n\nThus at least two lines must be non\u2011sunny, contradicting the assumption that at most one line is non\u2011sunny.  \nConsequently a configuration cannot have \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines; the maximum possible number of sunny lines is \\(n-2\\).\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n### Final Conclusion\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we have a construction for \\(k=0\\).  \nLemma\u202f2 provides constructions for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nLemma\u202f3 shows that \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are impossible.  \n\nHence the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}}.\n\\] \u220e"
