ty while increasing the number of sunny lines by exactly one.\n\n4. **Realising every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   Starting from the base family, choose any subset \\(S\\subseteq\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) with \\(|S|=k\\).  \n   Replace the vertical line \\(x=t\\) by a sunny line through \\((t,1)\\) for every \\(t\\in S\\).  \n   The resulting collection still consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines (the replacements), and continues to cover all required lattice points.\n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) (Lemma\u202f1).**  \n   Since at least two non\u2011sunny lines are forced, we cannot have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe lemmas together give the exact set of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f1 (At least two non\u2011sunny lines are necessary)\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition.\nThe points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) all satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) (because \\(n\\ge3\\)) and therefore must belong to \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n*If a single line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) contained both \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\), its equation would be \\(x=1\\); this line is vertical, hence non\u2011sunny.*\n\n*If a single line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) contained both \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\), its equation would be \\(y=1\\); this line is horizontal, hence non\u2011sunny.*\n\nConsequently the vertical line \\(x=1\\) and the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) must both belong to \\(\\mathcal F\\).  \nThus any admissible configuration contains **at least two** non\u2011sunny lines, and therefore **at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines** can appear.\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f2 (A concrete family of \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\n\\Bigl\\{\\,x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=n+1\\Bigr\\}\n\\cup\\{\\,x=t\\mid t=2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) have slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\