b=1\\) the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\in\\mathcal L_S\\).\n4. Otherwise \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\).  \n   - If \\(a\\notin S\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is still present in \\(\\mathcal L_S\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).  \n   - If \\(a\\in S\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) has been replaced, but the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) is still present (all horizontal lines \\(y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1\\) belong to \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) and were never removed). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(y=b\\).\n\nThus every required lattice point belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_S\\).\n\nSince \\(S\\) can be any subset of size \\(k\\), for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we obtain a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_S\\) that satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f4 (Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\))\n\nLemma\u202f1 showed that any admissible configuration must contain the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\).  \nTherefore the number of sunny lines can never exceed \\(n-2\\). In particular, configurations with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) do not exist.\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n### Final Conclusion\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we have a concrete family with zero sunny lines.  \nLemma\u202f3 shows how to obtain, for each \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\), a family with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines while preserving the covering property.  \nLemma\u202f4 proves that larger values of \\(k\\) are impossible.\n\nHence the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\] \u220e"

