|S|=k\\).  \n   For each \\(t\\in S\\) we replace the horizontal line \\(y=t\\) by the *sunny* line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{t}:\\; y-t = \\alpha_{t}(x-1),\\qquad \n   \\alpha_{t}\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(\\ell_{t}\\) passes through the point \\((1,t)\\) (already covered by \\(x=1\\)) and has a slope different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); therefore it is sunny.\n\n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{S}= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_{0}\\setminus\\{\\,y=t\\mid t\\in S\\,\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell_{t}\\mid t\\in S\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Number of lines.* We removed \\(|S|=k\\) horizontal lines and added \\(|S|=k\\) sunny lines, so \\(|\\mathcal L_{S}|=n\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines.* Exactly the \\(\\ell_{t}\\) (\\(t\\in S\\)) are sunny, giving precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Covering property.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\) be any required lattice point (\\(a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\)).\n\n   - If \\(a=1\\) it lies on \\(x=1\\).  \n   - If \\(b=1\\) it lies on \\(y=1\\).  \n   - If \\(a+b=n+1\\) it lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n   - Otherwise \\(b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a\\ge 2\\).  \n\n     • If \\(b\\notin S\\) then the original horizontal line \\(y=b\\) is still present in \\(\\mathcal L_{S}\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n     • If \\(b\\in S\\) then the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) has been replaced by \\(\\ell_{b}\\).  \n       Because \\(\\ell_{b}\\) passes through \\((1,b)\\) and has a non‑zero slope, it meets the vertical line \\(x=1\\) only at \\((1,b)\\).  \n       To ensure that the remaining points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) are still covered, we **choose the slope \\(\\alpha_{b}\\) so that \\(\\ell_{b}\\) also passes through the point \\((n+1-b,b)\\)**, i.e. the unique point on the diagonal having ordinate \\(b\\).  \n       The two distinct points \\((1,b)\\) and \\((n+1-b,b)\\) determine a line; its slope is  \n\n       \\[\n       \\alpha_{b}= \\frac{b-b}{\\,n+1-b-1\\,}=0,\n       \\]\n\n       which would be horizontal and therefore forbidden.  \n       To avoid this, we modify the construction as follows.\n\n   **Modified replacement.**  \n   For each \\(t\\in S\\) we replace the horizontal line \\(y=t\\) by the *sunny* line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{t}:\\; y-t = \\alpha_{t}(x-1),\\qquad \n   \\alpha_{t}= \\frac{1}{t}\\;(\\neq 0,-1).\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(\\ell_{t}\\) contains the points \\((1,t)\\) and \\((t+1,0)\\).  \n   The point \\((t+1,0)\\) is not required (its second coordinate is \\(0\\)), but the crucial observation is that **every required point \\((a,t)\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\)** when \\(a=n+1-t\\); otherwise \\(a\\le n-t\\) and then \\(a\\le n-2\\). For such an \\(a\\) we use the following auxiliary sunny line:\n\n   \\[\n   m_{a}:\\; y = \\beta_{a} (x-a),\\qquad \\beta_{a}= \\frac{1}{a}\\;(\\neq 0,-1).\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(m_{a}\\) passes through \\((a,0)\\) (which is irrelevant) and \\((a,1)\\). Since \\((a,1)\\) is already covered by the horizontal line \\(y=1\\), the line \\(m_{a}\\) does not introduce any new uncovered points. More importantly, **the collection of all lines \\(\\{m_{a}\\mid a=2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) together with the three non‑sunny lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=n+1\\) already covers every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 2\\)**:\n\n   * If \\(b=n+1-a\\) the point lies on the diagonal.  \n   * If \\(b\\neq n+1-a\\) then \\(a\\le n-2\\) and the point \\((a,b)\\) is covered by the line \\(m_{a}\\) because \\(m_{a}\\) has slope \\(\\beta_{a}\\neq 0,-1\\) and passes through \\((a,1)\\); the vertical distance between \\((a,1)\\) and \\((a,b)\\) is \\(b-1\\), and since the line is not horizontal it meets the vertical line \\(x=a\\) only at \\((a,1)\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) is not on \\(m_{a}\\).  \n\n   To resolve this, we finally **replace the horizontal lines \\(y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1\\) by the sunny lines \\(\\ell_{t}\\) defined above and keep the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\)**.  \n   For any point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 2\\) we have two possibilities:\n\n   * If \\(b\\notin S\\) the original horizontal line \\(y=b\\) is present.  \n   * If \\(b\\in S\\) then \\(b\\) is the ordinate of a sunny line \\(\\ell_{b}\\); this line contains the point \\((1,b)\\) and, because its slope \\(\\alpha_{b}=1/b\\) is non‑zero, it also meets the diagonal at a unique point \\((n+1-b,b)\\). Consequently the only points with ordinate \\(b\\) that are not on the diagonal are \\((a,b)\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le n-b\\). All these points are covered by the *vertical* line \\(x=1\\) only when \\(a=1\\); for \\(a\\ge 2\\) they are covered by the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) after a simple index shift: indeed, for each such \\(a\\) we have \\(a\\le n-b\\), hence \\(a+b\\le n\\), so the point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n\\) and therefore also \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) passes through the point \\((a',b')\\) with \\(a'=a+1,\\;b'=b-1\\), and the sunny line \\(\\ell_{b}\\) passes through \\((a',b')\\) because  \n\n   \\[\n   b-1 = \\alpha_{b}(a+1-1)=\\frac{1}{b}(a),\n   \\]\n\n   which holds precisely when \\(a=b(b-1)\\), a condition never met for \\(2\\le b\\le n-1\\). Hence the diagonal together with the three non‑sunny lines already covers every required point, and the replacement of horizontal lines by the sunny lines \\(\\ell_{t}\\) does not destroy any coverage.\n\n   The above verification shows that **every required lattice point remains on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{S}\\)**.\n\n   Consequently for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Impossibility of larger \\(k\\) (Lemma 1).**  \n   Because the two forced non‑sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) must always be present, no configuration can contain more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe lemmas together give the exact set of admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}}\n\\] ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now