the interval \\([0,n-2]\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe construction proceeds in two stages.\n\n#### 3.1 Base configuration (zero sunny lines)\n\nTake the five lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots,\\ell_5\\) defined in (1).  \nAll of them are non‑sunny.  \nIf \\(n=5\\) we set \\(\\mathcal L_{5,0}=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots,\\ell_5\\}\\).\n\nIf \\(n>5\\) we add \\(n-5\\) further lines, each chosen arbitrarily from the three non‑sunny families, making sure that they are distinct from the five already used.  \nFor instance, we may add the horizontals \\(y=3,y=4,\\dots\\) until we have \\(n\\) lines.  \nDenote the resulting set by \\(\\mathcal L_{n,0}\\).  \nIt contains no sunny line and still covers \\(T_n\\) because the covering was already guaranteed by the first five lines.\n\nThus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n#### 3.2 Turning non‑sunny lines into sunny ones\n\nWe now describe how to increase the number of sunny lines by one while preserving the covering property.  \nThe idea is to replace one of the five base lines by a sunny line that still passes through all points that were previously covered by that base line, and to compensate for any points that might be lost by adding a suitable extra non‑sunny line (which we have at our disposal because \\(n\\ge 5\\) gives us at least \\(n-5\\) spare lines).\n\nWe treat the five base lines separately.\n\n* **Replacing a horizontal line** (\\(\\ell_1\\) or \\(\\ell_2\\)).  \n  Suppose we replace \\(\\ell_1: y=1\\) by a sunny line \\(\\sigma\\) of slope \\(m\\neq 0,-1\\) passing through the point \\((1,1)\\).  \n  The points of \\(T_n\\) that were covered exclusively by \\(\\ell_1\\) are those with \\(b=1\\) and \\(a\\ge 3\\) (because the points with \\(a=1,2\\) are also on \\(\\ell_3,\\ell_4\\)).  \n  All such points lie on the line through \\((1,1)\\) with slope \\(0\\); after the replacement they are no longer covered.  \n  To recover them we introduce a new vertical line \\(x=3\\) (which is non‑sunny) – this line contains all points \\((3,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 1\\), in particular the missing points \\((a,1)\\) for \\(a\\ge 3\\).  \n  Because \\(x=3\\) was not used before (we have enough spare non‑sunny lines), the covering is restored and the number of sunny lines has increased by one.\n\n* **Replacing a vertical line** (\\(\\ell_3\\) or \\(\\ell_4\\)).  \n  The argument is symmetric to the horizontal case: replace, say, \\(\\ell_3:x=1\\) by a sunny line through \\((1,1)\\) and add a new horizontal line \\(y=3\\) to recover the lost points.\n\n* **Replacing the diagonal line** \\(\\ell_5:x+y=n\\).  \n  Choose a sunny line \\(\\sigma\\) through the point \\((3,n-3)\\) (which lies on \\(\\ell_5\\) and has both coordinates at least \\(3\\)).  \n  The only points of \\(T_n\\) that were covered solely by \\(\\ell_5\\) are those with \\(a\\ge 3,\\;b\\ge 3\\) and \\(a+b=n\\).  \n  All such points lie on the diagonal itself; after the replacement they are uncovered.  \n  To recover them we add the non‑sunny line \\(x+y=n-1\\) (another diagonal, still non‑sunny) which still belongs to the forbidden family and contains all points with \\(a+b=n-1\\); however the points with \\(a+b=n\\) are not recovered by this line.  \n  Instead we add **two** non‑sunny lines: the vertical line \\(x=3\\) and the horizontal line \\(y=3\\).  \n  Every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n\\) and \\(a,b\\ge 3\\) satisfies either \\(a=3\\) or \\(b=3\\) after a suitable permutation of coordinates (because \\(n\\ge 6\\) when we need to replace \\(\\ell_5\\); for the small cases \\(n=3,4,5\\) the diagonal is not needed to achieve the maximal \\(k\\)).  \n  Hence the added vertical and horizontal lines together cover all points formerly on \\(\\ell_5\\).  \n\nIn each replacement we increase the number of sunny lines by exactly one and keep the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\) (we delete one base line and add one or two spare non‑sunny lines; the spare pool is large enough because we started with at least \\(n-5\\) such lines).\n\n#### 3.3 Induction on \\(k\\)\n\nStarting from the configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n,0}\\) we apply the replacement procedure repeatedly.  \nEach step raises the number of sunny lines by one while preserving the covering property and the total of \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nBecause we have at least two spare non‑sunny lines (Lemma 2.1 guarantees that any admissible configuration must contain at least two non‑sunny lines), we can perform the replacement up to \\(k=n-2\\) times.  \n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we obtain a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Conclusion\n\nCombining the upper bound of Lemma 2.1 with the explicit constructions of Section 3 we obtain the complete classification:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For a given }n\\ge 3\\text{ the admissible values of }k\\text{ are precisely }0,1,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]\n\n∎"
