            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) let  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese are exactly the lattice points that must belong to at least one of the chosen lines.  \nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Upper bound: at least two non‑sunny lines  \n\n#### Lemma 2.1  \nIn any family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\) there are at least two non‑sunny lines. Consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the three sets of points  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nR &=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n-1\\},\\\\\nC &=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-1\\},\\\\\nD &=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n* The points of \\(R\\) all have the same second coordinate.  \n  A line that is not horizontal can contain **at most one** point of \\(R\\); otherwise two distinct points of \\(R\\) would force the line to be horizontal, i.e. non‑sunny.  \n  Hence covering all points of \\(R\\) requires at least \\(|R|- (n-2)\\) horizontal lines. Since \\(|R|=n-1\\) and we have only \\(n\\) lines in total, at most \\(n-2\\)