+1\\). From (3.2) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\). Because \\(a\\ge 1\\) we obtain \\(b\\le n\\).  \nIf \\(b\\le a\\) then \\(b\\le a\\) implies \\(b\\le a\\le n-1\\); the point \\((a,b)\\) then lies on the vertical line \\(\\ell_V\\) only when \\(a=1\\). If \\(a=1\\) we are already on \\(\\ell_V\\).  \n\nIf \\(b>a+1\\) then \\(b\\ge a+2\\). Using \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we get \\(a+2\\le b\\le n+1-a\\), which forces \\(a\\le n-3\\). In this range the line \\(L_{a+2}\\) has slope \\(\\frac{a+1}{a+2}\\) and passes through \\((a,b)\\) because\n\n\\[\n\\frac{a+1}{a+2}\\,a+1 = a+\\frac{a}{a+2}+1 = a+1+\\frac{a}{a+2},\n\\]\n\nand the integer \\(b\\) equals this expression precisely when \\(b=a+2\\). Hence the only uncovered points would be those with \\(b\\ge a+3\\), but such points violate \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) for the allowed range of \\(a\\).  \n\nConsequently every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n}^{\\max}\\). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Obtaining fewer sunny lines  \n\nStarting from \\(\\mathcal L_{n}^{\\max}\\) (which has \\(k=n-2\\) sunny lines) we can reduce the number of sunny lines one by one.\n\n*Replace a sunny line \\(L_t\\) by the diagonal line \\(\\ell_D:\\; x+y=n\\) (which is non‑sunny).  \n  The points that were covered **only** by \\(L_t\\) are those with coordinates \\((a,b)\\) satisfying \\(a=t-1\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\).  \n  All such points also satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and therefore lie on \\(\\ell_D\\) when \\(t\\le n-1\\). Hence the covering is preserved.*\n\nRepeating this replacement for any subset of the sunny lines yields a family with exactly  \n\n\\[\nk = (n-2)-\\text{(number of replacements)}\n\\]\n\nsunny lines. By performing from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-2\\) replacements we obtain configurations with every integer \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n-2]\\).\n\nAll lines remain distinct because the diagonal \\(\\ell_D\\) is introduced at most once; the remaining lines are the original two non‑sunny lines \\(\\ell_H,\\ell_V\\) together