is \\(t\\) gives  \n   \\[\n   y=\\frac{a}{a+1}\\,a+1 = a+\\frac{1}{a+1}.\n   \\]\n   Since \\(b\\) is an integer and \\(0<\\frac{1}{a+1}<1\\), the only way for \\((a,b)\\) to satisfy (1) is that \\(b=a+1\\).  \n   Thus every point with \\(b=a+1\\) lies on the line \\(L_{a+1}\\).\n\n   *It remains to treat the points with \\(b\\ge a+2\\).  \n   Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).  \n   Define \\(t=n+1-b\\) (so \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) because \\(b\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\le n\\)).  \n   Using (1) with this \\(t\\) and substituting \\(x=a\\) we obtain  \n   \\[\n   y=\\frac{t-1}{t}\\,a+1\n     = a-\\frac{a}{t}+1 .\n   \\]\n   The equality \\(y=b\\) is equivalent to  \n   \\[\n   b-1 = a-\\frac{a}{t}\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n   \\frac{a}{t}=a-(b-1).\n   \\]\n   Since \\(t=n+1-b\\), the right\u2013hand side equals \\(a-(b-1)=a+b-n\\).  \n   The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives \\(a+b-n\\le1\\), and because \\(a\\ge2\\) we have \\(a+b-n\\ge0\\).  \n   Hence \\(a+b-n\\) is either \\(0\\) or \\(1\\).  \n\n   \u2013 If \\(a+b=n\\) then \\(a+b-n=0\\) and the above equation forces \\(\\frac{a}{t}=0\\), i.e. \\(a=0\\), impossible.  \n   \u2013 If \\(a+b=n+1\\) then \\(a+b-n=1\\) and we obtain \\(\\frac{a}{t}=1\\), i.e. \\(t=a\\).  \n   But \\(t=n+1-b\\) and \\(a+b=n+1\\) together give \\(t=a\\) automatically.  \n   Therefore in the case \\(a+b=n+1\\) the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{a}\\).\n\n   Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines\n   \\[\n   \\{\\ell_H,\\ell_V\\}\\cup\\{L_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-1\\},\n   \\]\n   which consists of exactly two non\u2011sunny lines and \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \n\n3. **Obtaining fewer sunny lines.**  \n   Starting from the family above (which has \\(k=n-2\\) sunny lines) we can replace any sunny line \\(L_t\\) by the diagonal line  \n   \\[\n   \\ell_D:\\;x+y=n ,\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   whose slope is \\(-1\\) (hence non\u2011sunny).  \n\n   *Why the replacement preserves the cov