s\n     \\[\n     y=\\frac{a-1}{a}\\,a+1 = a .\n     \\tag{3}\n     \\]\n     Hence the point \\((a,a)\\) belongs to the sunny line \\(L_a\\).\n\n     Now suppose \\(a\\neq b\\).  Without loss of generality let \\(a<b\\).  \n     Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).  Define\n     \\[\n     t = n+1-b .\n     \\tag{4}\n     \\]\n     The inequalities \\(b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) imply \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\).  \n     Substituting \\(x=a\\) into (2) with this value of \\(t\\) yields\n     \\[\n     y = \\frac{t-1}{t}\\,a+1\n       = a-\\frac{a}{t}+1 .\n     \\tag{5}\n     \\]\n     Using \\(t=n+1-b\\) we compute\n     \\[\n     a-\\frac{a}{t}+1\n       = a+1-\\frac{a}{\\,n+1-b\\,}\n       = a+1-\\frac{a}{\\,n+1-b\\,}.\n     \\]\n     The equality \\(y=b\\) in (5) is equivalent to\n     \\[\n     b = a+1-\\frac{a}{\\,n+1-b\\,}\n     \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n     \\frac{a}{\\,n+1-b\\,}=a+1-b .\n     \\]\n     Multiplying by \\(n+1-b\\) gives\n     \\[\n     a = (a+1-b)(n+1-b) .\n     \\tag{6}\n     \\]\n     Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the integer \\(n+1-b\\) is at least \\(a\\) and at most \\(a+1\\).  \n     *If \\(n+1-b = a\\) then the right\u2011hand side of (6) equals \\((a+1-b)a = a\\), so (6) holds.*  \n     *If \\(n+1-b = a+1\\) then \\(b=1\\), contradicting the assumption \\(b\\ge 2\\).*  \n     Hence necessarily \\(n+1-b = a\\), i.e. \\(a+b=n+1\\).  In this case (5) gives \\(y=b\\); therefore the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_{\\,n+1-b}=L_a\\).\n\n     Summarising, every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered:\n     * points with a coordinate equal to \\(1\\) are on \\(\\ell_H\\) or \\(\\ell_V\\);\n     * points with \\(a=b\\ge 2\\) are on \\(L_a\\);\n     * points with \\(a\\neq b\\) and \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) satisfy \\(a+b=n+1\\) and are on \\(L_a\\).\n\n     Thus the family consisting of the two non\u2011sunny lines (1) and the \\(n-2\\) sunny lines (2) covers all required points.  Consequently the bound \\(k=n-2\\) is attainable.\n\n3