amily.\n\nThe same reasoning applied to the column  \n\\[\nC=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-1\\}\n\\]\nshows that at least one vertical line is necessary.  \nThus any admissible family contains at least two non\u2011sunny lines, and because the total number of lines is \\(n\\),\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Construction for the maximal number \\(k=n-2\\).*  \n\nDefine the two non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_H:\\;y=1 ,\\qquad \\ell_V:\\;x=1 .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\; y=\\frac{t-1}{t}\\,x+1 .\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\dfrac{t-1}{t}\\) is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); hence each \\(L_t\\) is sunny, and the \\(n-2\\) lines \\(\\{L_t\\}_{t=2}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe prove that every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines (6)\u2013(7).\n\n*Case 1.* \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\).  \nIf \\(a=1\\) the point belongs to \\(\\ell_V\\); if \\(b=1\\) it belongs to \\(\\ell_H\\).\n\n*Case 2.* \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nt:=\\frac{a}{a+1-b}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(1\\le a+1-b\\le a\\); the denominator is a positive integer, so \\(t\\) is an integer. Moreover \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\).  \n\nSubstituting \\(x=a\\) into (7) with this value of \\(t\\) yields  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{t-1}{t}\\,a+1\n  = a-\\frac{a}{t}+1\n  = a-\\frac{a}{\\frac{a}{a+1-b}}+1\n  = a-(a+1-b)+1 = b .\n\\]\n\nHence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_t\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered by the family consisting of the two non\u2011sunny lines (6) and the \\(n-2\\) sunny lines (7).  The bound (5) is therefore attained.\n\n*Obtaining fewer sunny lines.*  \n\nLet \\(\\mathcal F\\) be the family just constructed.  \nFor any subset \\(S\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) replace each line \\(L_t\\) with \\(t\\in S\\) by the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell